      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>ENGLAND'S EXPECTATIONS.</head>
        <p>ENGLAND'S EXPECTATIONS.</p>
        <p>Each Man must do his Duty.</p>
        <p>Nelson's name will for ever be associated with the famous signal that at Trafalgar ran along the line of Hs battleships, and nerved every Englishman's arm and mind with a stern resolve to fight and to conquer or to die. That glorious message, has become part and parcel of every loyal Englishman's creed, and alone is sufficient to make us feel to the full the pride and joy and distinction of belonging to the freest and greatest nation in the world. We must preserve the great heri- tage that N-Icon and our fathers left us, and we can do so by obeying in every department of life the spirit of Nelson' order. In our private and in our public life, and in small matters as well as in great, WA must do our duty to our fellows and to ourselves. If we catch cold even, we most take every care of ourselves, and use every remedy we can in order tl bring about a speedy recovery, for every one counts in the battle we have to fight against England's enemies. Lavan's Lun g Linctus will cure colds, coughs, and all chest and throat affections. Try a bottle, and judge of its efficacy your- self. Lavan's Lung Linctua may be had from all chemists, co-ops.. and drug stores, and post free, is lid., 2s 9c1., and its 63., from the proprietors, Green and Co., Chemists, Bolton.</p>
        <p>The Infirmary Concert</p>
        <p>This annual event which is taken up with char- acteristic energy by the Workingmen's Committee of the Infirmary and DispenSary was deserVedly attended with succesn on Saturday evening. Parr Hall was filled in every part and tha infirmary funds will no doubt benefit to a considerable ex- tent. An attractive programme had been arranged in which Mr T J Down's orchestra occupied a pie - minent position. The artistes engaged in addition were : Madame Conway (soprano), Mr euthb 3rt Allan (baritone), Mr Fred Pullan (humorist), and Mr W II Payton (accompanist.). The only new name is that of Mr Pullan. Ac his first contribution he sang several songs in a falsetto voice but one ad- mired his "get up "as a woman in preference to his vocal abilities. In the second half he was far more successful as a humorist when he endeavoured to reproduce a village concert, and although some of the items given were very old, still he interested the audience and proved himself to be a clever performer on the pianoforte in addition. Madame Conway's initial effort was A Summer Night "(Goring Thomas), with violon- cello obliglto by Mr. Edgar Baitnford. She cave a finished interpretation of the piece and was deservedly applauded but she scored a more tirc- flounced success in her interpretation of" Should he upbraid " (Bishop) with violin obligato by Mr. R Pearson. Mr. Cuthbert Allan is the possessor of a robust tenor voice and gave a vigorous interpreta- tion of the" Post thou" and "There's a land. In response to the continued applause after the sing- ing of the last named song he complied with such an old favourite as " The Village Blacksmith "and further delighted the audience. The accom- paniments were played with Mr. Payton's usually good taste and skill. No fewer than six items in the programme were allotted to Mr. Down'a orchestra and all were performed in such an admirable manner as to whe:., the apnetite for more. The beautiful overture " Stra- della "was brilliantly interpreted and was perhaps the beso, of the evening. The selection " Merrie England," introlucing as it does, many of the popu- lar English airs, caught the public ear and was a favourite with the audience. A descriptive piece imitating the noises of an express train in its various movements was the lass item on the pre- gramme, but the effect was somewhat spoiled by many in the audience leaving their seats in their hurry to get home. Mr J T Down, conducted the band and safely piloted all through the many difli- culties which beset them. The performance of "God Save the Qieen " cone! aded an enpyable evening's entertain nl</p>
      </div>
