        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>This time of the year always sees a change from straw to felt and fur hats, but still many beautiful varieties of the former are prominent. I have just seen a black hat which has a folded crown of black velvet, and a brim of the new sequins, which are in the form of little rings. Two very handsome black ostrich feathers hang over the brim, and underneath, on the left side, there is a chou ol shrimp-pink miroir velvet and a paste buckle. The brim of this hat takes very graceful and be. coming curves, and droops over the hair at the back. On another smart hat and under the brin: are two little crossway frills of blue velvet, corded at the edge. On the left side is a bow of velvet, with a handsome steel buckle, to which are attached four very tiny ostrich tips, which curl over on to the hair. The charm of this hat lies greatly in the colouring of the velvet.</p>
        <p>Fashionable sleeves incline to be uncomfortably tight, and it is often difficult to persuade a dress- maker to give the arm sufficient room in its cover- ing. The effect of a too-tight sleeve is to give a wooden look to the arm which is far from artistic. :Slashes have begun to appear at the elbows, which is a step in the right direction, and they may next be looked for at the shoulders. We do not seem to be able to wholly reconcile ourselves to the plain sleeve. On many of the new dresses there is a shoulder trimming of some kind. However slight it may be, it serves to break the line. in making sleeves the lining should always be fitted. first, no matter whether the fitter be an amateur or professional, as by all odds a close. fitting sleeve is the most difficult of dressmaking problems. Here again the little darts come to the rescue, and may be placed at any point where there may appear too much fulness. And the 'ining, also, and outside material must run together concerning the grain of the goods, sc they will stretch alike, unless the outside is made bias in case of stripes or plaids, when, of course, the lining runs straight of the goods, and must not be pulled out of shape in the fitting.</p>
        <p>In many of the coats for the autumn the back of the coat has seams running to the shoulders, and these, when sewn on and pressed, should be stitched twice on either side. The fronts have no darts, and should hang quite straight. In order to stiffen the lapel and keep the front firm and straight, a five or six-inch strip of canvas is laid to the edge of the front and firmly tacked to the cloth. The turn of the lapel is well pressed in the c s, and only requires to be again lightly pressed wnen the cloth or other facing is laid on. The collar is cut on the bias, and is of the same width all round. A small corner is cut off where it joins to the lapel, and the turn is made on the slope, bringing the fold quite narrow on the underneath part in front, and wider at the back, thus throwing the longest portion outside just above the lapel. A dress suitable for an afternoon is made of very finely striped white and dark grey silk. The skirt is scalloped out round the edge, and falls over a number of white silk muslin frills. The vest is also muslin, and the fichu matches it, but is trimmed with rows of narrow black velvet where it is drawn down towards the waist under a sash of chine silk. The bolero and sleeves are made of lace, and the scarf, which is draped round the figure in the newest way, is made of pale rose-pink -chiffon. The sleeves fall over the hand in a fashion that cannot be called new, but which is revived for the autumn dresses.</p>
        <p>Black house gowns are always useful and never out of fashion, so that one is invariably a wise choice. A very nice indoor dress is made in the simplest possible style with a long flowing skirt and a full bodice, over which is wont a fichu of lace, mousseline-de.soie, or soft silk, this crossing over on the chest in front and showing a very small V-shaped vest or chemisette of tucked chiffon or lace. The sleeves may be long and tight, or of the " pagoda " shape, reaching just below the elbow, with full inner ones matching the fichu. A dress that has rather more resemblance .to a tea-gown, but which is yet suitable for ordinary afternoon wear, has a short bodice with a broad falling collar of guipure lace, and a long sash of soft silk. It can be successfully carried out in wool, cotton. crêpe, cashmere, or veiling. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>THE VICTORIAN POETS.</p>
        <p>Lecture on "Tennyson."</p>
        <p>In the Cc-operative Hall, Cairo-street, Warrington on Wednesday evening last week, the first of a series of lectures on the" Victorian Poets" was delivered before an encouraging attendance. Mr. E T Campagnac. M.A. was the lecturer and his subject was " Tennyson." The Rev.J F Matthews briefly introduced Mr Campagnac and stated that although the audience was not consulted at the conclusion of the last course of lectures as to the period of English literature they would prefer, still it might be assumed from the audience he saw before him that the selection bad their very hearty approval.—The Lecturer said any one of the poets whwse names ap- peared on the syllabus deserved and demanded the study not of an hour but of many hours and all he could attempt or hope to do was to put before them certain general ideas which may help them in approaching these writers. The Victorian era was one wh'cia tad hen singularly rich in poetry of the highest crier. It Was also preceded by a period of poetry as remark- able as our own but different in quality. That period was the span of years covered by the life and most important work of Wordsworth. In describing the features of the poetry of Wordsworth's age he said it was distinguished by two dectriees, the doctrine of the dignity of manhood and the quality and worth of a man as a naan,and the doctrine that for purity and et- joyment we must look to natural and unconventional sources of delight.ln the ace of Tennyson these dectrines were taken up in a new:forrn.They were given a subordi- nate place in a fresh kind of teaching in which Tenny- son was the most eloquent, the most deliberate and most conscious exponent. If they looked at the works of Tennyson with minute scrutiny they would find three tendencies. There was first of all the tendency to chronicle in poetry social struggles, the upheaval of class feeling, the difficulty of adjusting the relations between master and servant, between rich and poor, between superior and inferior. There had also been a simultaneous development in the poetry covered be' Tennyson's life of interest in history and in science. They had gone band in hand and re-acted on each other with the result that a now meaning had been given to history and an impetus to science. The lecturer proceeded to show bow the poetry of our age hsd tended to give expression to our religious and highest emotions, and said it seemed to him that Tennyson was one of the greatest and truest moralists and at the same time and for the same reason oneef the greatest idealists in all literature. Dealing more particularly with the life of Tennyson he referred to his birth in 1809, and proceeded to sneak of his earliest verses and to the publication in 1827 of the "Poems by two brothers "in which work Tennys,n was joined by his brother. Comparing the poems eat 1832 with tho.e of 1842 the lecturer showed the marked alvance both in expression and in thought accomplished In the interval. No poet was so severe a Critiu of his own poetry as wasiTennyson, and he spent year after year in trying to perfect what he had once given to the world. If they compared the earlier with a later edition of his works they would find that though the general theme remained the same the structure and gen- erallwording hadlbeen altered in a remarkable degiee As an example of the melodiousness of Tennyson's verse, the lecturer read the poem " Break, break. breek " After a reference to the " Princess " writen in 1847. the:lecturer referred at length to Tennyson's "In Memoriam "of 1850, showing the manner of its com- position, and its peculiarity of construction. It was a style which had been used by earlier writers, but no one had ever shown such a complete mastery in the appro- priation of it ate Tennyson. The form of a staina had an especial suitability to his theme. It was an elegy which had immortalized Arthur Hallam. The father of Hallam wrote to Tennyson that he found it difficult to read this poem because the grief which was expressed was too much akin zo the grief which it revived in him. In that sentence was expressed a truth which applied to all Tennyeon's work. In what Tennyson said we found an expression of what we felt Ourselves. In col- elusion the lecturer described Tennyson as the represen- tative poet of his time, who gave hack to his age, per- fectly expressed, the thought and feeling of the age.</p>
        <p>neart were too full to dwell upon irrelevant questions. _ _</p>
        <p>- The boy led her down to the water and an old landing-stage, with a board above it, adorned with a hand and some faded black lettering, directing strangers to the Olde Bell Inn,_ _ _</p>
        <p>my father at that inn ? " asked Sybil, indi- cating the faded sign. Her companion shook his head, which was covered with a mop of dark, curly hair, longer than worn by most English boys. If Sybil had had time to think of it she might have wondered at that, too, and the odd habit the messenger had of dropping his eyes when he• was questioned, as if it were difficult or unpleasant to meet her gaze; but she had not time, and, besides, she knew little of English boys and their habits. "No, your father's not there," said the messenger; "but it won't be very long before you come to the place now. See, here's the boat, safe enough, where I left it." Tied to the landing-stage was a small skiff, so old that it would hardly have tempted a thief. The boy helped Sybil in, rather awkwardly, and quiakly followed, beginning to handle the oars with some skill. The shore seemed to slip away behind them, and Sybil' eyes strained ahead, looking for the 'house where her father lay waiting for her. Those who had sent to fetch this messenger, she vaguely thought, had been fortunate in obtaining one who could seull so well.</p>
        <p>They had gone on almost in silence for nearly an hour, perhaps, when Sybil saw an old-fashioned, carved waggonette drawn up by the roadside close to the house. At that moment her companion turned the boat nearer to the shore, and it seemed to the girl that his white, thin face looked anxious and troubled.</p>
        <p>"Is it possible," she asked herself, "that father is worse than the letter said, and the boy knows but is afraid to break it to me? Now we are coming to the end of the journey—such a long, tedious journey I—and he feels he must tell me; yet he does not know how to begin." She glanced at the messenger almost timidly. Yes, the expression of his face was certainly very strange. "Is there anything that you have been told to say to me about my father ? " she ventured. The boy started and stared. It was the first time that his eyes had willingly met hers. "Anything I want to say to you ? " he echoed, roughly. "What—what should there be? It's my business to carry letters not to give explanations." He spoke so rudely that Sybil flushed to her forehead.</p>
        <p>"I only fancied," she answered, "that you looked distressed. But, maybe, you have some trouble of your own to worry about, and were not thinking of me at all?" "Yes, I have a trouble of my own," repeated the boy, as he brought !lis boat to shore. Then he turned to her abruptly.: "Look here," he said, "I didn't think you would be the sort of girl you are or I should never have taken up this job. I've nothing to say to you, and nothing to tell you, except just this If you want to run away from me before the driver of that waggonette catches sight of you (he's sitting with his back towards us, so he can't see us, .and we can't see him, though I know he's there), I won't put out a hand to stop you. There That's the best offer I can make, and you must take it or leave it inside two minutes or it will be too late !"</p>
        <p>Sybil's eyes widened in profound astonishment at so strange an outburst from the messenger. "Run away and not go to my father when I have come all this long distance to see him ? " she exclaimed. "Oh, whatever I may have to bear I would not be such a coward as to do that!"</p>
        <p>"But I don't promise you that you will see him when you get to—to the journey's end t" protested the boy, with an odd, restrained excitement that began to pilule Sybil exceedingly, and made her heart beat with a dull, vague appreheosion. "Perhaps—perhaps by the time you get there he will have gone." "Then if there's a chance of that he can't be sa seiously ill or injured after all " retorted Sybil, with reviving spirits. "But he would not go away after sending for me I am almost sure, and, at all events, I must risk that."</p>
        <p>"Well, you've made up your mind, and the two. minutes are up," said the boy, gloomily. "Here, give me your hand, and I'll help you out. What little fingers you've got You can't be much more than a child—years younger than I am." "You are a very queer messenger-boy ! " ejaculated Sybil, involuntarily. "Do you always talk like this to people you have to travel with? Why, now you are going away without tying up your boat." "It will be all right," returned the boy, irritably. "Come along, since you are bound to come. That waggonette is waiting for us and the driver doesn't like waiting." "Do you know him, then, and the people who live in the house where we are going ? "asked Sybil. "Oh, yes, I know them. You see, I—l live near by." "It seems a very lonely place," remarked the girl. "I wonder why my father came— But look The driver sees us. lie is peeping round the corner of the waggonette." They were on the road now, and close behind the old-fashioned, dilapidated vehicle, which had all its curtains down.</p>
        <p>After the first peep, by which he ascertained the fact of his passenger's arrival, the driver kept him- self out of sight. ,Sybil had only caught a glimpse of a bearded face, and an eager flash from eyes set under dark, peculiarly-bushy brows, when the messenger threw open the door of the waggonette, motioning the girl to step in. Once inside it was much like being in a tent, for iron rods supported a flat, canopy top of leather, with curtains at the sides, hack, and front, which would have afforded complete protection from rain. But there was no rain to-day, and the atmosphere under the curtains was so heavily-laden with mustiness and a choking smell of dust that Sybil would fain have unbuttoned a section of the heavy hangings. "Don't do that!" sharply exclaimed the boy. "He won't have it."</p>
        <p>"He ? " echoed Sybil. "Do you mean the driver ? "</p>
        <p>_ "Never mind who I mean," the boy rejoined, roughly. "I didn't intend to say that, but it's said now and can't be helped. There are reasons why those curtains shouldn't be up, or they wouldn't be fastened down, you know. Remember, you would come when I risked something to give you the chance of getting away. So now you must make the best of things as they are." Sybil was silent. She could not understand the curious, personal interest this boy, in the uniform of a hired messenger, betrayed in her affairs. Had she lived longer in England her bewilderment would have been more intense, and perhaps she would have repented her decision not to turn back. But as it was feeling as if she was in the midst of a strange, unpleasant dream, she sat still, her heart heavy and oppressed, her mind busy with. a tangle of conjectures. The waggonette jogged on and on, and the twilight under the curtains grew greyer. It seemed to Sybil that several hours must have passed since she had left Mitchell-street with the messenger ; but, long as the time had appeared, she was sur- prised, when at last they came to a standstill and her companion opened the door, to see how the evening was closing in. She had a fleeting impres- sion of a long, low house-front of red brick, mellow with age, and draped with ivy, trees that over- shadowed it, and, across the width of an ill-kept lawn, a steely gleam of river. Then the boy hurried her up the two or three stone steps to the house- door, which had been opened from inside. The wagonette rattled away along a gravelled drive that wound round to the back of the building, and she was alone in a square, dark ball. Sybil glanced about half-involuntarily for the person who had thrown open the door, but he or she had already vanished; only the painted eyes of a few old portraits stared down at her from the walls.</p>
        <p>In the hall were four high windows with tiny, diamond-shaped panes ; but though there were neither blinds nor curtains the drapery of creepers outside hung so heavily as to darken the place ‘rith a green dusk, the deeper now that evening shadows had begun to fall. Opposite the door by which she had entered a wide staircase, with shallow steps and old-fashioned dog-gates at the bottom, led up into dimness overhead.</p>
        <p>"This is the way," said the messenger-boy, mounting the first steps. Sybil followed. Half-way up was a landing, with a window of painted glass and a seat that ran round the wall. There was a tall clock, too, of quaint, foreign make, with a grinning moon-face and glass eyes, that moved backwards and forwards as the seconds followed one another.</p>
        <p>So strange was the effect of the gleaming eyes in the gloom as they darted this way and that on the big white disc that Sybil started, and shrank closer to her companion fur an instant. The boy laughed unpleasantly. "There are queerer things in this old house than that ugly Dutch clock," he said, going on ;last the landimr. "You may see some of • them befwe you</p>
        <p>say good-bye to shouldn't wonder. Anyway, your father has." He stopped before a door at the end of a corridor.</p>
        <p>"There," he went on, "if your father hasn't gone you may find him there. Remember, I didn't promise, and remember, you had your choice of not coming at the last."</p>
        <p>Finding-that he made no move towards opening the .door Sybil impatiently turned the handle, and without waiting for further words stepped over the threshold. Beyond she saw a long, low-ceded room, with large, old-fashioned furnittire, that loomed among the shadows in uncertain shapes. In the most distant corner stood a four-post bed- stead. canopied and curtained; but it seemed to the girl that something lay there ominously still. "Father!" she cried, impulsively, and hurried forward never dreaming of danger, though half- unconsciously she heard the click of the latch as the door closed behind.her.</p>
        <p>The girl feared to push aside the bed-curtains behind which all was so still, but summoning all her cburage she flung them apart. She had half- ' Ipected to see a dead face on the pillows; but the eed was bare save for a neat pile of sheets and blankets that lay folded on the uncovered mattress —a pile that had assumed the shape of a human body in her imagination. The hand which held the curtain dropped ~..rvelessly to her side. Where was her father? What had the messenger-boy meant by saying that if she found him at all it would be here?</p>
        <p>She ran back to the door, questions trembling on her,lips. But she rattled the handle in vain; the door was locked on the other side.</p>
        <p>For the first time Sybil began to be frightened for herself. Until now she had thought only of her father, and had no time to spare for puzzling over outside matters that appeared unusual. But as she heat upon the door in vain, only the echoing silence' of an old and lonely house answer- ing her, her heart grew cold. There was a hateful air of mystery about this place, and now that her mind travelled back to review it there was mystery in every stage of the journey she had made to reach the house. Why had she been brought here, if not to see her father? And where was this rambling old mansion by the river? Knowing nothing of London save the few streets in Bloomsbury through which she had walked with her father—Regent-street and Hyde Park; knowing even less of the outlying country, she could form no idea of the way in which she had come. She could not even remember now, in the confusion of her mind, how many times she and the boy had got in and out of omnibuses and trams, how many bewildering, monotonous streets they had hurried through before they gained the river and found the waiting boat. "To save my life I could not find my way back!" ;lybil said to herself,. with a thickly-beating heart. "Oh, Why doesn't somebody come to the door? Surely there must be a mistake? Why should anyone wish to keep me locked in here ? " Amazed, and growing more and more alarmed, the girl tried to reassure herself with various far- fetched suppositions. Of course, there was nothing to be terrified about. She was only an insignificant little girl, of not enough importance for anyone on earth to care to do her harm. Her father had strange whims sometimes. And when he had been in difficulties abroad, he had devised curious methods of escaping from troublesome creditors. Could it be possible that he was not ill, or injured, after all, but that this means of getting her away from Mitchell-street was one of those devices?</p>
        <p>Hastily Sybil thrust her hand into her pocket for the letter from her father, which she had placed there after reading it. Her little fingers searched from corner to corner, but the pocket was empty. The sheet of paper, with its faint red stain, and its envelope were both gone. The thing seemed impossible, for the pocket was a deep one, and as she had held her handkerchief in her hand and had possessed no purse (the messenger paying all fares), she had not touched its contents since slipping in the letter. It must be that her pocket had been picked in one of those trams or omnibuses, which formed themselves into such a tedious procession in her mind. As she so thought, happening to raise large, startled eyes, she caught sight of her own slender form in a long mirror, which ran from floor to ceiling on an opposite side of the room. She did not realise the beauty of the pale young face, with its crown of rippling, moonlight hair, and the contrasting darkness of the long lashes and delicately pencilled brows ; the childish curve of the upper lip, that described a Perfect Cupid's bow, and was one of her greatest charms; or the grace- ful moulding of the little figure. As she saw her- self, she was only a shabbily-dressed girl, whom no professional thief would think of troubling himself to rob.</p>
        <p>"It seems as if no one could have taken the letter, except a person who knew it was there, and had a very particular reason for wanting to get it away from me," she argued, slowly. "Yet no one did know it was there, for I had left the house and was standing on the doorstep when the letter was given me. No one did know—at least, no one but the messenger-boy." Sybil's face flushed. If he were a thief, no wonder he had acted so strangely. She must see him. He had brought her to this room and locked her in; he must come and let her out again. With all her might she shook the door, and beat with her small gloved fists upon the panels, calling aloud for someone to come and open. But nobody came, and the shadows in the lonz, low room seemed to crowd closer about her. What was she to do? What was to become of her? She felt as ;f she were in an evil dream.</p>
        <p>She hurried to the window and looked out through small leaded panes. A ragged garden lay twenty feet below; but, even if she had been inclined for such a desperate jump, the attempt would have been rendered impossible by the fact that the window- sashes were securely fastened, being apparently nailed together. There was nothing to do if her calls remained unanswered but to wait. Yes; there was one other thing—she could say a prayer for her father and herself.</p>
        <p>Sybil Wade had had practically no religious teaching. Had she depended for such knowledge on what she learnt from her father, she would not have known of the existence of a God. But once an English chaplain in a foreign town had taken pity upon the beautiful, forlorn young waif, and implanted ideas and hopes in her mind which sho had never forgotten. Now she knelt down, bowing her head in the deep window-seat to pray that God would keep her father from harm, and help her to bear any trouble or suffering that might come. Somehow she felt better when she rose from her knees, and the shadows that were slowly shutting her in did not seem so dark with unknown dangers. She had been in a mood at first to reproach her- self desperately for being so easily deceived by the treacherous messenger ; but she was calmer now, and saw that, considering her youth and lack of experience, knowing so little of England and its customs, she could hardly have been expected to be wiser than she had been. . _</p>
        <p>For some moments she stood by the window, watching the night drop its dark, brooding wings over the garden, her ears strained to catch the slightest sound in the passage. Then she returned to the door to knock again and call aloud for some- one to come.</p>
        <p>Only silence answered; and at last she gave up, hoping that there had been a mistake which would presently be rectified. She ceased also, or almost ceased, to hope that the strange adventure had been planned as a practical joke by her father. There was something deeper than that, though what it could possibly be strive as she might she could not guess. At last the room was quite dark, except for a ghostly gleam of light still lingering in the depths of the long mirror opposite the window. Sybil lost count of time, and only knew that it must be growing late because she was faint and hungry. Last night at this time she must have been at Lady Northmuir's dinner, talking to Jack Churchill. Somehow the thought of his kind, handsome eyes brought a pang, yet at the same time a dim sense ;NS comfort. "He has forgotten me by this time, and perhaps he is with Gladys," was the thought, which was charged with pain. "But he would help me if he knew," came next into her heart, leaving a faint glow of warmth. Sybil had scarcely slept during the night before, because of the heavy presentiment which seemed to have been so strangely, if indirectly, verified. And now, despite her fears and her keen, constant suspense, the silence and darkness began to make her drowsy. Her eyelids drooped, and once or twice she roused herself with a sudden start from falling into a doze. She was afraid to let herself lose consciousness ; but at last the effort to keep awake became insupportable. Nothing appeared to matter if only she might close her eyes and yield to the overpowering desire for oblivion, if only for a moment.</p>
        <p>"If anyone here wished to harm me by violence," she thought, "they need not have left ine here undisturbed for so long. I'll -call out and beat</p>
        <p>on the door once again, and then, if no on answers, I shall have &amp;vie the best I can." She found her way to the door, and for a moment or two the old house echoed with her appeals to be set free. But when only silence responded she went back to the seat under the window, curling herself into a piteous little heap on the dusty cushions. Five minutes later she was dreaming that Jack Churchill had opened the door to say he had found her after a , long search, and that she must come with him at once. "I will come! I will come!" she heard herself say, and the sound of her own voice awoke her. As she opened her eyes in the dark she still fancied that Jack Churchill was calling. But in an instant everything had come back to her. "I'm here sU lone!" she thought. Yet—was she alone? iturely something in die room was moving! (To be continual.)</p>
