        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>C"4.1, f 5,.. •,</head>
        <p>C"4.1, f 5,.. •,</p>
        <p>aREAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
        <p>(CoPYrt:o4)-</p>
        <p>THE HOUSE BY THE RIVER,.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. C. N. WILLIAMSON, Author of "The Secret of the Pearls," "The nom by the Lock," "Queen Sweetheart," "The Barnstormers," "Her Royal Highness," Scc.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Lancelot Wade, a well-connected ne'er-ao well, lives with his daughter Sybil in fourth-floor rooms in Blooms- bury. One day the father brings Sybil the extra- ordinary news that she—she who never went any- where or saw anybody in her life—is to go to a dinner-party at Lady Northmuir's house in Berkeley- square, where she meets a certain Jack Churchill, who is expected by Lady Carstairs, another guest, to marry her daughter Gladys. The dinner, like the house, is very grand, and Sybil is supremely uncomfortable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CHAPTER IV. THE LETTER WITH A RED STAIN.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IV. THE LETTER WITH A RED STAIN.</p>
        <p>The dinner at Lady Northrnuir's seemed like a dream to Sybil as she looked back on it, sitting in the homeward-bound cab by her father's side. But, after all, she was not sorry that she had gone, even though there had been a trying half-hour in the drawing-room before the men came in, and she had frozen and burnt alternately under Lady Carstairs's and Gladys's cold eyes. Somehow she had been repaid for all by Jack Churchill's kind- ness. And though her experience of men had not been ikncouraging, she told herself wistfully that she would have liked to have him for a friend. "But I shall never see him again," she thought. "He was kind because he was sorry for me ; yet by to-morrow he will have forgotten that there's such a girl as Sybil Wade in the world."</p>
        <p>11 Look here, Syb, I'll open the door for you with my latchkey," said Lancelot Wade, as the stopping of the cab broke in upon her reflections, "and then be off. I have an appointment—something rather important. It may make a lot of difference in both our lives. But there's no use asking questions. I'm bound to secrecy for a time. Ta-ta, my dear. I should say you'd have plenty to dream of to-night." lie had been thoughtful during the drive, and though there was nothing new for him in late hours the excited ring in his voice gave significtince to the words. To Sybil it seemed as if they were echoed by a whisper in her ear—a mysterious whisper from lips unseen that foretold disaster. She was not a superstitious girl, but a chill of pre- sentiment struck like a dagger of ice at her heart.</p>
        <p>For a moment she stood-looking after him when he had dismissed the cab, and only closed the door. with a little shivering sigh as he turned the nearest corner, his figure swallowed up in the night. It was true, as her father had said, that she had "plenty to dream of." But she could not sleep, and her dreams were waking ones. She thought of Lord Carstairs's light eyes, and the peculiar look he had fixed upon her; but his face had changed, and a vein had begun beating in his forehead, when he read the telegram that was handed him. She thought of Gladys Wade's dark beauty, and was angry with herself for the little stab of pain that came as .she wondered if her cousin and Mr. Churchill were engaged to be married. Then her mind would go 17aelc to her father, his unusual excitement and his hint of a secret. He had many secrets from her. Why should the existence of this one fill her with such dreary foreboding ? Every time the church clock a few streets away boomed out the hour she heard it with a start. She was sure that her father had not returned, for, if he had, she must have heard the. creaking of the stairs in the dead silence of the waiting night. Still, she might unconsciously have dozed, she told herself. And she did not dare tap at the door of his rolm until long after she had risen and had tier meagre breakfast. _ _ _</p>
        <p>It wit; ten o'clock when she ventured. Twice she knocked, and when no answer followed timidly opened the door and ventured in. The room was empty, the bed smooth and untouched. Mrs. Goman, when applied to, was inclined to be cross rather than anxious. It was the day when she expected to be paid, and there were alitady two weeks owing. Lodgers had strange ways of evading their obligations sometimes. Noon came, and still Lancelot Wade had neither come nor sent an explanation of his absence. Sybil could bear the suspense no longer, and determined, despite the landlady's sneers, to go to the police- station and tell her story. So scornful of this resolve was the experienced Mrs. Goman that the girl would not ask guidance as to the direction from her, but hurried out, hoping to find some- one in the street who would tell her which way she ought to go. While she still hesitated on the doorstep, wondering whether to turn to the right or the left, a smart-looking messenger-boy came briskly along. Sybil noted -vaguely that he appeared to be older than the majority or his fellows, and, as he paused to glance at a letter lq•Id in his hand, she addressed him shyly: "I beg your pardon, can you tell me.where is the —the nearest police-station ?" The boy lifted his eyes and stared at, her. "Is your name Miss Sybil Wade?" he quickly asked, without answering the question. "Yes," said Sybil, in surprise. "Then this is for you. Lucky I caught you, the messenger remarked, holding out the letter. Sybil flushed scarlet and tore open the enielope with trembling fingers, for it was directed to her at 28, Mitchell-street, in her father's writing—shaky, curiously painful in the uncertain formation of the letters, but unmistakably his. . . "Dear Syb," she read, "come to me at once. The bearer of this will bring you. lam ill and in trouble. Say nothing in the house. You can return later and explain." _ -</p>
        <p>Glancing up suddenly she met the messenger- boy's eyes fixed upon her face with a gaze of devouring curiosity, which would have caused her surprise, perhaps resentment., if she had had time to speculate upon it ;, but a dull smear of dark red at the corner of the page had set her heart wildly beating, and she could think of nothing else. "What is this ? " she ejaculated faintly. "It— it looks like blood. Did you see the gentleman who wrote the letter ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes," said the boy, moistening his lips, his eyes falling before Sybil's frightened, questioning gaze. "I'm afraid he's had an accident." "Take me to him at once!" Sybil implored. "Oh, how thankful I am that I spoke to you, that I hadn't got away before you came !" It did not even occur to her that she might have to be away a long time, and that Mrs. Goman would naturally think both her lodgers had very cleverly given her the slip on the day when their second week's beard was due. But if she had thought of it she would hardly have stopped for explanations then. Sybil slipped the latter into her IDnekst and walked on in silence by the messenger's side. They went very fast., and, having passed through several streets which she had never seen before, the boy hailed an omnibus. There he would have run on top leaving her to go inside ; but she stopped him. "Please stay here and tell me all you can about my father," she begged. "For it is my father whose letter you brought, and I am very, very anxious."</p>
        <p>The face under the smart cap grew sullen. "I was told not to answer any questions, miss," he returned. "I'll come down for you when it's time to get out." With that he ran up the stairs and disappeared. It seemed a tedious time to Sybil before he came back again, stopping the omnibus, and offering to help her down. "Now we take a tram. It's a good distance yet," he added. They were in a mean street with long rows of sordid shops and tramcars passing up and down. The boy hailed one, and in a moment the journey was resumed. At last he rose and held up his hand to the conductor.</p>
        <p>"'We get down here," he said to Sybil. Utterly bewildered as to direction she followed his guidance. A muttered word of advice that she should "make haste" was not needed. The girl almost ran by the messenger's side as they turned corner after corner. Presently the shops dis- appeared, the houses were scattering. From a street they came out into a suburban road, then into the country. They were near a river now, and Sybil could hear the monotonous sound of a weir.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. AN OLD HOUSE BY TIM RIVER.</p>
        <p>"We'ye got to take a boat now," said the boy. "I left it close by here coming. I'm going to row you to the house where your father is. It's a good deal quicker than walking." Dimly the thought came into Sybil's mind that, since the house where her father lay waiting for her was so remote, so isolated, it was strange that meesenger had been obtainable ; but her bead and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>MIRS)I A considerable amount of entertaining is being done in London just now,and this state of affairs promises to continue until Christmas has come and gone, when a general flitting will be the order of the day. Monte Carlo and other places on the Riviera will doubtless then be extensively patronised ; also Egypt and Algiers, which are fast growing in favour as winter resorts. No a few will go still further afield, Lord and Lady Hope- toun having invited—in view of the visit of the Duke and Duchess of York to Australia—many of their friends to join them there in the early spring. One of the most remarkable signs of the times, is the decay of the town hcuse. Formerly every great land- owner had his town house where he spent the season, but now-s-days a flat, or a suite of room in one of the modern hotels, takes its place, at, needless to say, cm- eiderably leas cost than would be involved in keeping up an expensive house in a fashionable quarter of Lon- don. These ideas British women also follow in Paris, where numbers have adopted the freer life of an " ap- partement " in preference to a house, and all its accom- panying domestic trials. Though many priceless treasures have been lost in the wing of Welbeck Abbey, lately destroyed b fire, the safety of the three bonny little children of the Duke and Duehess of Portland very much more than compen- sates for all damage done. The Duchess is devoted to her children, and has them with her on every possible occasion. Little Lady Victoria Cavendish Bentinek gives promise of great beauty' and her sweet: winning ways make her a huge favourite with all with whem she comes in contact.</p>
        <p>After the cubterranean rooms and passages, upon which the late Duke of Portland is said to have expended about seven millions, the most famous thing about Wet- beck Abbey is the Park, where may be seen standing some of the finest oak trees in the country. Through the trunk of the renowned Greendale oak, a carriage road once ran, but seven hundred years has made suffi- den impression on it to render extraneous timber de— sirable far " prcpping-up "purposes. Many distinguished women figured amorg the success- ful exiiibitors at the Dairy Show this year.amang wham were the Princess of Wales, the Duchess of Newcastle, the Duchess of Atercarn, Lady Rothschild, the Baroness Burdett-Coutts, the Countess of Craven, and the Coun- tess of filarrowby. Some ict'resting butter making and milking contesta were to be seen, and altogether the show, the twenty-fifth annual one, was declared a great success. Some time ago, a number of our farmers in- terested in dairying, went to Denmark to study the methods that have made it such a profitable industry to the Danes.ln their opinion,which has just been issued in a report by the E:sex County Council, this is the result of legislation, education, and co-operation. Small farming with an average of less than 100 acres is the rule, and cc-operation, while reducing the cost of pro- duction, has enabled better prices to be obtained. All this was, of course well known, but the report &amp;es not seem to hold cut much hope that similar methods will be readily adopted in this country. In a General Election, many women who pay rates, present them.elves at the polling-b,oths, and can only be persuaded with difficulty that they have no vote. In some few instances women who have been given male Christian names have got upon the Parliamentary register, and occasiomally these have been known to vote. It is said that M. Ernest Flower had the assist- ance of such a vote in West Bradford, but it would not be allowed under a scrutiny. The popular belief is that all and everyone on the register can vote, but as a matter of fact it is not so, and the votes of women, minors, or other iisqualified persons, can be rejected afterwards, even though they are on the register. "Where is little doubt that women will obtain the Par- liamentary franchise before long. In former times when the education of girls was generally considered to be a superfluity, there was some reason for their exclusion. Very few men had votes then, but now almost every labourer has the franchise, while women, who are nowadays reprrsented in every trade and professioniand are in all other respects upon an equality as ratepaying nccupiers, areexcluded. The times have changed, and though many women may not desire the franchise, the tennency of the progressive movement of the age, is all towards the removal of this inequality.</p>
        <p>There are, at least, a million and ri half of women in the United Kingdom who am earning their own living and paying rates and taxes. But while Parliament decides how many hours they shall work, and regulates such questions as their meal timf-s, holidays, and condi- tions of livine,they have no voice at all, as far as voting goes. This is Government without rf,presentation with a vengeance and it is a condition of things that is not likely to be much longer maintained. . -</p>
        <p>Mr. Lake, the vice-president of an American Tem- perance Society. says that the women of her country are fast falling a prey to the "Manhattan Cocbtail." We too, hear a good deal of the growth of intemperance among women in this country, but unfortunately tem- perance advocates are so given to over-stating their case, that the public seldom takts them very seriously, and they consequently make little or no impression among the people. There is another evil, the gambling evil: and this has certainly made great headway in re- cent years. Among the poorer elasses,the woman book- maker finds a ready welcome from her own sex. To the factory girl, as to many other people, the po,sibility of making money without work, offers great temptations, and is very easy for her to point to the lady plunger at Aseot for her justification. Undoubtedly the big sleeve looms ahead. For months past the sleeve fashionable has been exhibiting a marked irresponsibility, a puff liere, a frill there, and by next spring, we shall in all probability, be donning the huge baloon variety deemed correct some five or six season's back. Tnis way lies expense. It is quite possible, as many of us know full well, to convert a voluminous sleeve, into one of the tieht-fitting variety, but the other way about would baffle the most clever among us. Those women therefore, whose gowns are comparatively few and far between, would do well to secure an extra length of the material each dress is built of, that full sleeves may ba introduced when necessary, and thus bring the gown up-to-Jate. Nothing dates a gown more than sleevas, and the wise willcarefully bear in mind the certain coming chance. The mcst becoming hats are those bent mach down at the back, and a little down in front. A few upturned, and turnee-hack brims are in evidence, hut only faces of the petite, delicate order look well beneath them. The slightly beet bum on the other hand i becoming to all, large featured, and small teatured ; pretty and plain alike. All trimmings are kept low, feathers, wings.etc , curling rcund the hat or toque, not standing upright,as Was once their wont.</p>
        <p>Pretty drop buttons, accompanied by jewelled but!'or.- boles appear en some of the latest creations, and a very charming addition they prove to a well-thoueht-out toilette. The drops are translucent crystals, imitating emeralds, pink and yellow totonv,smethysts, ftc., and the buttonholes are rimmed round with gems to match these drops. Thelwhole thing is, of course, not for use, only for ornament, hut in effect it is delightful; Orange SPonCe.—Dissolve half an ounce of sheet gelatine in half a pint of wat”, add the rind of two oranges and two ounces of white sugar. Strain all through muilin. Mix the wkite of one eeg with the above, also the juice of two oranges. Whisk 20 minutes, colour to taste, and place in a wet mould. Turn out to serve.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the moat suitable for tropical climates ; It is light, clean, cool, comtert and durable ; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITH Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Reashaw-street, opposite top of Bold-street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>RESERVIST'S WIFE IN DIFFICULTIES.</head>
        <p>RESERVIST'S WIFE IN DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>The Way Out.</p>
        <p>Eight above the breezy uplands between WeTwin &amp;ad: Luton, lies Ereachwood Green, reputed the healtkee_e vtllsge in Hertfordshire. Its modest population the made ample contribution to tbe military fercee of Empire, and among its sons called up from th is Private Ernest Lawrence. It was no slight wrench to give up good ,employment, leave a pretty home,and3ol the Bedfordshire Itc.,giment (in which be is plc:mei" with three good conduct stripes) at Shorocliffe CAMP' One need not enlarge on the hardship of parting frons wife and two bonny children, but the trying illaels Beeerve which overtook Mrs. Lawrence, soon after her husband S departure in the early part of Jauuary, was of such se unusual character (het a " Beds. Advertiser" reporter was deputed to make a visit of inquiry. "I don't want to •go through a trine like that agairt:' said Mrs Lawrence, " fdr you must know that in ter° weeks I slept only two nights. My illness was sciatic* it first attecked me in the right leg, the limb beconsieg painfully swollen ; then itotook me in the hip ever, I kept about in the bore that it would go 01— Instead of that, it got worse until I could not get oP unless I took hold of the table. and I could not cress tbe„ room witirut using astiok. Later on my father bong": me some crutches When I RO: to bed, I could net reire and the pain was so acute that it seemed like being Os with it knife, if I tried to turn. One night they were afraid I was going off altogether." 46 1 Continuing her narrative, Mrs Lawrence said : d had read in little books of people suffering like me, an said to mother. 'You might get me a box of mr• Williams' pink pills for pale people.' So they sent intl Luton for a box. and I seemed to get on as soon ag took the pirs. I had thrre boxes altogether, ard gone on improving ever since." , When the Commander at Shorncliffe got tbl doctor's cerificate saying my wife ceas to ill," OW Private Lawrence (who was present), "I was given a month's furlough. I hurried home and was surprised to find my wife out in the road. Only three week/ eV she was using crutches, but they are now sent ascef' My wife is doing her housework as usual, and I can 5 put it down to anything else but the pills." Mrs Lawrence added that she had used only thrae boxes, and was now quite well; but she WSS getid,il two and ninepence for a fourth box that day. She 137.. told the messeneer to be sure to have the seven 'wor?ci on the box. "Yes ; for one has to he careful." elle, her husband, "because come tradesman are very anli°" to hut you off with something else." • Mrs. Lawrence showed a very proper precaution, f(1 the impression left on the public mind by the cures often effected in apparently hopeless cases by v'' Williams' pink pills has undnubtedle led, here and there, to unscrupulous dealers endeavouring to palm off ilea: tions in place of the eenuine article, which may alwaY° be distinguished, however, in the way described sagaciously by Mrs. Lawrence. Ia case of doubt it' easy to send direct to the Dr. Williams' Medicine Coo' pany for a supply, at their offices on Holborn ViadoOt• London.with the price enchmed. The other disorder§ that have been cured by this remedy include erremist rheumatism, indigestion. the ailments that affliet the_ weaker sex. rickets in children, STh. Vitus' dance, loss ot strength, and even paralysis and consumptien if taken in time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>AUTOMATIC PARLIAMENTARY REPORTING. Is it Probable in the near Future.</head>
        <p>AUTOMATIC PARLIAMENTARY REPORTING. Is it Probable in the near Future.</p>
        <p>A most instructive artiee a0p33,8 in the Octobir ni,mber of the" Wiadsor Magazine," on the new phone that is working such a n-volution i the matter of loud-speaking and long distances. Tbe Peseibilit!" Of this development seem practically inexhaustible. One of the most strik;.ng applications of the Germain tele- phonejs in connection with the phonograph and the cinematograph or biograph. As everyone knows the phonograph cannot register sounds not produced 15 close proximity to the receiver, and specially direct°, into it. If we desire to record Ile actual stage delAivearei of a famous aeor, for instance, the phonogroP,..b us no help. The loud-Speaking telephone here 11".n1 and supplies the missine. link. A large metal frame -4:- taming a consV.erable number of microphonee-4131` six are rrquired for the Paris Opera—is suspended eflia the stage. In an adj)ining roum is the receiver Plahriin immediately above the phonograph, to tha telephone conveys every sound from the audit:o2'in; , By a combination of the three instruinebte " thus practicable to record and reproduce eCiir with the act6.r's every word and gesture. A Henry Irving and other public favourites will live tie; move and have their being long after they theraseiveh have gone the way of all flesh. before long a biogreP.," theatre, with the best actors and vocalists of the .da4 at its noinmand, will form an indispensable Per 6,4, every well-regulated ocean liner. In combination 91" A the phonograph, the loud-speaking telephone is destiet to be of great service in recordict the debates of pub% assemblies. These two instruments will turn eel,. verbatim report, the accuracy of which cannot be ww puted ; and if we go a step further and cause the Pbtl,, nograph to dictate to an expert operator on a tYPeseqb. lug machine, we shall produce a verbatim report out writing a line! Obviously, however, this le's would be of the nature of a tour de force. The gentlen!eng of the Press in the gallery of the House of C0M121,,%. have nothing to fear. They can 'co t" tidense,' which i telephone and phonograph cannot do. It is, nev eel; less, by no means improbable that the two instrun3oo may some day provide Hansard with a model steno' grapher who never tivs and (lever makes a tni stake' Automatic Parliamentary reporting! Shade of D 6 JChnson ! ! "</p>
