      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>"Row did Flimgilt get rich ?" "By his shrewd speculation.", "And how did Fucash happen to lose his little property ?" "Oh, he went and dab- bled in stocks." MRS. GAELETON—" I understand that your hus- band cannot meet his creditors." Mrs. Fale—" On the contrary, he says he can't help meeting them. That is the reason he goes out so seldom." AT MIDNIGIET.—First Cat: "Why SO sad to- night, Thomas ?" Second Cat: "I feel so lonely and neglected ! I've been weeping and wailing for ao hour, and nobody has thrown anything at me !" WHEELER—" Miss Yellowleaf, will you ride tan- dem with me for--" Miss Yellowleaf—" Oh, Mr. 'Wheeler, this is so sudden; but you'll find ma be. hind the parlour door ; speak to her." DOMESTIC SIGNS.—" I can always tell when a man is the -head of his family." "How do you tell ?" "'When the man is boss the first boy is put in trousers before he is a year and a half old."</p>
        <p>TOMMY (fer vently) : " Miss Thomson—Bessie— may I eali yon Bssie ?" Miss Thomson—" Yes, I think you may, as--" Tommy : "Oh. thank You, Besgie—thank you!" Miss Thomsou--"It isn't my_ name."</p>
        <p>"I DON'T know which is worse," muttered young Blunderheels, at the swell reception, retreating to a corner and wiping his perspiring brow; "bun I believe I would rather bs run over by a train than to step on one." ETHEL: "Isn't it strange that Flossie attracts such intellectual men?" Maud: "Oh, no; she told me she always planned her gowns when they talk to her, and that gives her face that interested ezpression." Mr. CASEY " a-goin' to take little pat to the circus when it comes. They charge half- price for children, don't they?" Mr. O'Rourke: " Yes ; an' bedad, it's no more'n right they should. The children don't enjoy it half as much as the grown folks." . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>On. AY !—Editor: "The metre in this poem is defective." Contributor: "I meant it to be. The poem is entitled The Charge of the Gas Company.'"</p>
        <p>DEPENDS ON THE POINT OF VIEW.—" What is interest?" "Interest is what a man pays you when he borrows your money." "What is usury? "Usury is what you have to pay a man when you borrow his money." THE PRUDENT COURSE.—" Updyke is an ex- ceedingly quick-witted chap." "So?" "Yes. For instance, last night, at Jammer's party, he unfortunately stepped on Miss Quickfire's dress, and ripped in the neighbourhood of three miles of ruffle off it." "lace. He immediately mollified her by some exceedingly witty apology." "Nob much ! He immediately disappeared." AND GOOD HOPS Too.—Kate: "Well, it's a mercy you left Wurzelshire and have come to spend a few days with us, Cousin Joe I I suppose down where you live the people know nothing of concerts or even social hops ?" Cousin Joe: "Aw ! My father ha' ten acres o' nowt else but hops, but I dunno about social hops. We call um Kent hops." AN IGNORANT CAPTAIN.—Farmer on boards, a steamer (suffering a good deal from the rolling of the vessel) to friend: "This capt'n don't under- stand his business. Why don't he keep in the furrows ? "</p>
        <p>RATHER INCONSISTENT. —Father—" Come, young Irian, get your coat off and come with me." Tommy—" You're not going to lick me, are you, da,l ? " Father—" Certainly. Didn't I tell you this morning that I would settle with you for your bad behaviour ?" Tommy—" Yes, but I thought it was only a joke, like when you told the grocer you was going to settle with him." "YES, sir," said Jordan, "I've come to the con- clusion that I amount to something after all. There have been times when I was disposed to believe that I was a mere cipher in the world, but I can never have so small an opinion of myself again." "What has caused this sudden change in your estimation of yourself ?" "1 have just been talking to a politician who wants my vote." "PROVIDENT IN PERIL."—A youth who wanted money wrote home as follows :—" Father,—lf you don't send me a cheque I'll drown myself." He presently received the following reply: "John,— Pull off your clothes before you jump. Your mother can cut 'em down to fit Jemmie."</p>
        <p>Mr. Dapper is one of that class of men who are scrupulously neat in their personal appearance, but who never fail to leave chaos behind them in the scene of their preparations. A neighbour recently called on Mrs. Dapper, and remarked: "One rarely sees a neater man than your husband he always looks as if he had just come out of a band-box." "Very true," returned Mrs. Dapper; "but "—with a sigh—" you ought to see the band- box I"</p>
        <p>MR. SHENTPERSHENT (at hotel table, a summer resort) : "Mein eracions I Isaac, you little vool. Tat for you ask for bret?" Little Isaac: "1 vant bret mit my meat, fadder." Mr. Shentpershent : Shust hear dat ! He vants bret ven bret sell for enly fife cents a loaf, an' he gan't eat a kavarter off a loaf, an' I pay fife dollars a day at dis hoteL Here, Isaac, eat di s bottle off olives. Dey cost on tollar a bottle."</p>
        <p>BAGLEY (sternly): " Aurelia, let this thing stop right here. M. De Tvvirliger-" Aurelia: "()h, paw! Do you forbid Arthur the house?" Bagley: "He may come every night in the week if he pleases, but he shall not bring his dog into my parlour. Last night its howlings kept me awake for an hour." Aurelia.: "Why paw! that was Arthur singing a selection from • Erminie.'" PROOF OF WEAKNESS.—Isaacs: "A young man clamed Green vas abblying for a bosition in mein ehtore, and he gives you as reference. Do you know him, Stickenheimer ? " Stickenheimer "Yes. He vas a good mans. He vas von of mein very pest gustomers for den years." Isaacs (in disgust) : " Ach I He gets not der bosition i I embloy 140 man vot is a tool.'" COULI4 N'T MAKE IT FAY. —Political Farmer (despondently) : "Somehow I don't seem to make tannin' pay." "Maybe you haven't tried the right way." "Yes, I've done ev'ything. I've tended rneetin's, and j'ned clubs, and voted for every candidate that said he knowed the way to help us along. But it don't seem to do no good, an' I must say I'm gettin' clean discouraged." GEATITUD E.—Clerk at the Gas Office; "Well, I suppobe you've come to complain about your gas bill again, have you ?" Patient Citizen: " Sir; l'.ve.come round to thank you people. Some- body left a jet on in my daughter's bed-room last night, and she lay there with itigoing at full head all night." Cleric:r " Great heavens, man ! But why should sou want to thank us ?" Citizen : "You see, there was so little gas in the air which came from the pipe that it Javier hurt her a bit."</p>
        <p>A MISCARE.—The Sunday school teacher had reached a point in the lesson where she was dwelling upon the future reward of those who be- haved properly here, when the aziacious small boy who frequents most Sunday schools spoke up and asked if all good people went to heaven. "Certainly," replied his teacher. " Well, has my grandmosher gone to heaven!" persisted the youngster. "Surety she has my boy, if she was a good woman." "go, she has, declared the fan.loving youngster; "there she is, aver there 1" The teacher tarried to other phases of the lee=ion.</p>
        <p>"I WILL not deny, ;dr. Harkalong," the young 'Woman said, with downcast eyes, "that your attentions are agreeable to me. I—l am always glad to see you." Mr. Harkalong attempted to shorten the distance between them. "And yet," she went on, restraining him by a gesture' "I hard!" know my own mind. I"-- "Miss Gillenwaters—Bertie !" interrupted the young man. "Never mind your mind. Listen to your beatt !" "That's what I am trying to do.' she replied. "If I could only"--. "If you could only 15e sure I am worthy? Of course, I'm not, dear! No man on earth is good enough " " No, it isn't that. I "-- "Your people object ?" "Oh, no !" "Neither do mine!" he exclaimed, rapturously. Again he attempted to shorten the distance, and again she restrained him. " Bertie Gillenwaters," hoarsely he asked, "would you ruiss me if I never came here again?" "That's it, Mr. Harkalorig," murmured the agitated maiden, looking at him with tears in her eyes. "I don't Want you to atop coming. Indeed, I don't. I Should miss you. Indeed, I should! Mr. Harka- long "—and a tear rolled down her cheek—" you bring me the best chocolate, creams I ever get 1"</p>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN should send two stamps for our 32 page Illustrated Book _containing valuable information of how all Irregularities ';r11 Obstructions may be entirely avoided or romoved '2Y simple means. Recommended by eminent Physicians the only Safe, Sure and Genuine Remedy. Never I. its. Thousands of genuine unsolicited Testimonial V." 03 all over the Weld -P. BLANCIIARI Dalston-lane I`4"lden.—Advt.</p>
