      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Parish Church. The harvest festival services which were commenced on Thursday evening were contineed on Sunday. the preacher in the morning being the Rev Morley Strven- son, while in the afternoon there was a servico for the Young conducted by the Rev A S Cameron. In the evening the Rev C C Elcum, of Liverpool, preached in the absence through illness of the Rev E E Hardine, Principal of St Aidan's Theological College, Birkenhead, and took for his text the words : " Whoso offoreth praise glorifieth Me." The preacher, in the course of a somewhat lengthy sermon, contended that whether in prosperity or adversity they ought to he able to give thanks to Almighty God, "for His mercy endureth for ever." It was a difficult lesson to do sometimes, but surely not this year in the present state of England. They had to thank God not only for the harvest but for the wonderful outpouring of temporal mercies, the vast increase of Empire which seemed to be the prelude to more to be ottAined in the East, the wonderful iecrease of power and undoubted respect for the English name throughout the world, and the marvellous welding toget- her of their colonies with the mother land. The anthem "The Laird is loving "(Dr Garrett),was rendered by the choir very creditably, and appropriate hymns were also sung. Mr W S Woods presided at the organ for the last time. The collections for the whole of the festival ser- vice realized £46. and they were in aid of the clergy fund, for which £5O is required. St. James', Latchford. These services were continued in the above church on Sunday. The preacher both morning and evening was the Rev LT J Darwall, vicar of Walton. There was a large congregation at the evening service, every seat being occupied. The rev, gentleman took for his text the words " Thus I say then, walk in the spirit, and ye shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh But if ye be led of ths spirit, ye are not under the law." Galatians v. 16-18. The choir Molt rendered the anthem " Thou visiteth the earth," the organ being presided over by Mr. F. Booth. organist and choirmaster. The collections which were in aid of the new mission funds amounted to a satisfac- tory sum. Spiritualist Hall, Bewsey-street. The safe ingathering of the harvest was celebrated at this place on Sunday. A number of the friends worked hard in collecting, contributing.and arranging an assem- blage of fruit, flowers, vegetables, &amp;c., and the result of their labour of love was seen in an excellent display ar- ranged with considerable taste. In the evening there was a very large audience. Miss Smith. of Southport, was the speaker, and based her remarks upon one of the hymns :—" What shall the Harvest be 2" She pointed out the lessons the harvest should convey to us ; that as the farmer prepared the ground, sowed the seed, or set the plant which in due time by God's blessing attained fruition, so we were daily sowing seeds either for good or ill, the harvest of which we should inevitably reap in the life beyond. After the address Miss Smith was pre- sented with a bouquet. Hymns suitable to the festival were heartily rendered, and the choir was augmented for the occasion. Mr. Foster presided at the organ, and a solo, "Seeds of Promise," was tastefully rendered by Miss A. Furness. On Monday evening a "fruit social" was held, when there was a good attendance. An enter- tainment of songs, recitations, &amp;c.. was given ; refresh- ments, fruit, &amp;c., were supplied ; a gramaphone gave considerable pleasure. and Miss Smith and Mr Llewellyn gave sittings in te Borderland." Orford.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services were held in Orford Church on Sunday. The interior of the building had been prettily decorated with plants, corn, towers and vegetables by Mrs. Vale-Owen, Mrs. John Cann (who were specially responsible for the pulpit and reading desk), the Misses Melbourne, Woolmer, Povey, Monks, Massey, Dakin, Richardson, and Fisher. The pulpit was occupied at the morning service by the Rev. H. A. Lester (lecturer at the Training CoVege). The choir rendered the anthem "The eyes of all wait on Thee "(Caleb Simper) in a creditable manner. In the evening the church was cr.wded, when the preacher was the Rev. George Vale-Owen (curate-in-charge of Orford). The choir sang the anthem " Fear not, 0 Land " (Cook) efficiently, Miss Melbourne effectively rendering the treble solo. and Mr. Walter Povey the bass solo. Mr. John Cann presided at the organ. In the afternoon the Rev. G. Vale-Owen conducted a children's service and delivered an address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TWO HOURS IN A CELL.</head>
        <p>TWO HOURS IN A CELL.</p>
        <p>"I wonder how a fellow feels locked up in there, said the writer to the Governor of a big prison as we were peeping into a cell through the grated iron door. "If you want to get a taste of it, lock you up far a couple of hours and then let you out," answered the Governor pleasantly. "All right, go ahead." I stepped inside and he closed the door with a clang, locked it and left me without a word. The sources of amusement in that apartment were exhausted in half a minute. Four paces one way, two paces the other, was the limit for pedestrian exercise. No sound, no view, nobody to talk to. Even if a bore or a bill collector could have got at me it would have been an agreeable break. I lay down on the iron cot and wondered when they would take me out and hang me ; or whether I was likely to get a new trial. At the end of the second hour the Governor released me.</p>
        <p>"Had enough ? " he asked, "Quite enough—for now." I answered.</p>
        <p>And yet I would rather take a month of that sort of hing than the eleven weeks of elafinemon: Mr: Gore Robinson got—in kis house, mind you—after the fashion the tells of.</p>
        <p>"I was laid up for eleven weeks with acute dyspepsia and the maladies that are part and parcel of it. 'Of course I bad abandoned my work, as a man can't work when be feels the way I did. - "The trouble began back in 18881 before that I was healthy as anyone wants to be. The first of it was just weakness, loss of appetite, and a low, miserable state of feeling. Then there was pain at the chest anti stomach after eating—a time when veil people are at their best.</p>
        <p>"After that I couldn't sleep at nights, except .by naps and catches, when I got completely tired out. In the morning, as you will see, I W4B in poor form for busi- ness, but stuck to my work much as a worn-out horse leans against the collar and staggers on with his load. "øo the complaint,went on, getting worse and worse until I dropped everything and was laid up for eleven weeks. I saw one doctor and another, but they didn't seem to nit my ailment so as to relieve me for more than a day or so at a time. On a neighbour's word I resorted to Mother BeigarssSyrup and began to mend almost at •once. My breathing and my appetite both improved, and in a few weeks I was in sound health, and have been so ever since."</p>
        <p>This Mr. Robinson wrote on Auguat 24th, 1899, from his home st Rawcliffe, Yorks, R.S.O. Mother Seigel's Syrup frees captives locked in the prison of disease and pain.</p>
        <p>Mistaken Ideas about Alcohol.</p>
        <p>A good number of the children and friends at Bald-Areet Wesleyan School assembled on Tuesday evening in the schools to hear an illustrated lecture entitled Mistaken ideas about alcohol." Mr Hoult presided and introduced the lecturer, Mr J ilale. In the course of a highly instructive lecture, which was well illustrated by experiments, the lecturer showed the effects of alcohol on the human frame and warned his audience against taking intoxicat- ing liquors. At the conclusion of the lecture Miss E Taylor sang a solo. On the motion of Mr Sander, seconded by Mr T Bodle, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer. Telephone 2339. Established 1849.</p>
      </div>
