      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CONDITION OF LABOUR IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>CONDITION OF LABOUR IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>[BY HENRY WHITIIIM] We all know the oid saying about " distance lead leg enchantment to the view." In few things does this apply with such force as to the industrial matters of the United States. To the British work- man, who cons his paper over pretty closely, there is a certain amount of information given touching industrial conditions here that is wanting altogether, I fear, in the southern parts of Europe. This accounts for the great disparity in numbers of those of the labouring classes who leave the shores of Europe to seek a living in the" land of the free." This statement needs a little qualifying,inasmuch as we should note the Irishman as being greet at emigrating, and, if he can only get across the Isocky mountains, no doubt he batters his condition, so far as amount of wage received goes. American newspapers, and politicians, never weary of keeping to the front all the advantages that surround and make up the life of the American labourer. The two work together all the time. It sometimes seems to me as if the people, the labourers, thought there was some sort of a mine into which these cormorants could dip at will and , extract all they needed to make life, to them, worth I living. The people seem to ignore the fact entirsey that State purse and municipal nurse can only he replenished in one way, and in that way lies their own impoverishment. Taxation is moving along upward and onward in such dsgree that the com- parisons between wages earned by the commen labourer, and the cost of living bear no relationship, and must needs increase the growth in number of industrial disturbances. I forward to the Editor of the " Examiner" an article of mine published in the Journal of the Rail way Employees. It is a plain statement of facts as to the position of the labourer west of the Rockies. What is known as America's greatest strike is now on in the eastern state of Pennsylvania, and is being watched with the most intense interest. To English readers, who are only used to know of strikes as happening among men who are of one nationality, all speaking the same language, having the same traditions, and in a manner, having all grown up together in a certain homely fashion, the strike in Pennsylvania needs some explanation as to its human material. There is and can be, no true cohesion among the Unions, and their various members. The Huns, Poles, Fins and Slays of vari- ous nationalities are not noted as students of later questions. They know nothing of the struggle of labour since the day Watt noticed the lid of the kettle bob up and down. They have a certain viciousness of temper, making it hard to control them, and, worse, they lack leaders. The man who aims at leading men must have something more than mere courage. He must have that something which comes from a wide study of labour and its laws,'"politics, and its snares. The labour leader of America is a politician, and nothing mere. He is in politics for all it is worth., and other questions must be worked for his personal:profit. Therefore I fear there is little hope for the American labourer. llt may be that the etrike in Pennsylvania will gain some of the minor points asked for by the striking miners, but I doubt it. Large numbers of troops are being massed in the various mining districts, and great excitement prevails, and I am rather afraid before these words are read the telegraph will give you accounts of 'rioting. When upwards of 120,000 men are idling around,and fooi and other essentials are needed, it will need a strong hand to keep order. There has already been some shooting done, and lives lost, touching which event the leader of the men, Mr Mitchell, says :—" From in- formation I have received from those who have in- vestigated the situation it appears that the shoot- ing on the part of the Sheriff and his deputies was entirely uncalled for, inasmuch as the strikers had not injured a single member of the Sheriff's posse, and the circumstance forcibly illustrates the ruth- less disregard the Sheriff and his deputies have for the lives of persons whom it was their plain duty to place under arrest if they bad violated any of the laws of the State." It is rather a curious feature in the American's love for liberty that he always calls upon the Sheriff, and his specials, or upon the soldiers to put down strikers so soon as they be- come the least demonstrative. Now, what are these men striking for.? We may waive the question as to whether men have any "natural rights." or not, we may even try to forget that man needs a "living wage," so that he may,at least, be enabled to show up on time each morning when the whistle goes off, but if we do those who comealong after we are dead and gone ' will make a hot time for the money barons. The average wages of the miners for years have been less than ..E1 per week. The laws of the State of Pennsylvania make 2,400 pounds a ton of anthracite coal, yet anthracite coal miners are compelled to mine from 2,700 to 4,000 pounds for a ton, end in addition to that are docked exhorbitant amounts, often reaching 12 per cent of their daily earnings for any impurities which may be sent out with their coal. W bere they are paid by the ear instead of being required to furnish a well-rounded beep on the car at the breakers as was originally agreed to, they have gradually been compelled to increase the amount of coal in each car by building the same perpendicularly from six to eighteen inches above the edge. They are com- pelled to purchase the powder used in mining from their employers. paying 2dols. 75 cents per keg for a grade of powder that can be purchased elsewhere for 1d01.50 cents per keg,and which wholesales for about Idol. per keg. They are required by many of the companies to deal in" pluck me " stores or leave their employment. They must pay to the com- pany Idol. per month for a doctor whether they need him or not, and have no voice in saying who the doctor shall be.</p>
        <p>These ." pluck me' stores are common all over the United States. They are what used to be known ix England as "Tommy shops," and "Truck shops." To the late Charles Bradlaugh the thanks of the British workman are due that these thieving institutions no longer grind out of him his hard earnings. There needs a Char;es Bradlaugh in America. I have myself travelled through large districts when the workmen eeldom or never received cash payments as wages. An order is given on the Company's store, where all that a workman is supposed to require may be had. In some cases these profits are so enormous as to en- able the employers to pay wages and all other expenses of their businoss out of them. Just recently a son-in-law of mine paid me a visit. His home is some 200 miles from San Fraacisco. He tells me that in every case in his district where large numbers of men are employed under one com- pany cash payments are never made. In some cases the order is made payable at any of the stores. There is a degree of fairness in this, but the man is a slave all the same.</p>
        <p>There are any number of laws on the statute books but no one dare force them. If a workman pro- tests and insists upon being said in cash, be is no longer wanted. If a committee waits upon the employers they are at once dismissed. Of course these things only exist in a land of liberty. The Federation of the United States is a grand thing in theory, in practice it is a fearful and mot wonder- ful affair,</p>
        <p>With respect to child labour some of the most devilish features in unrest rioted competition are seen. At this present tito,,cores ot towns in the Southern States are seeking to attract capitalists by advertising the abse.ticrt ot any legislative res- trictions upon child labs- ur in their zy;ates. In the mining districts where It., trike is now on a large number of young chil.,ita are omployed, and the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>• 4 P</head>
        <p>• 4 P</p>
        <p>etrikers have been giving the people a startling object le,son. A parade of 2000, slate pickers, runners, drivers, door tenders and helpers from the mines of Scranton and vicinity was the feature of September 22nd. The parade was planned by Or- ganizer Dilcher to show how many children who ought to be in school were forced into the mines by reason of their fathers being paid such poor wages. It was a sight that would move the hardest heart. Fully a third of the boys in line appeared to be about nine or ten years of age, and inquiry among them elicited the startling fact that not a few eight-year-old children were numbered in the parade. It is no tve moralising on all this. Those who read and think of these things will understand where America stands on the labour question. They will also be able to see how it comes about that American coal can be exported to Europe. I hare seen figures on this question that are simply start- ling. Before I left home I was often told I had competition on the brain. Like unbridled ambition it spares neither 'father or mother, child or nation, all must be sacrificed.</p>
        <p>I feq I have not done full justice to this mighty question, but I must plead not being well; the " grippe " has had me pretty tight in its clutches. San Francisco, September 21th, 1930. WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY. The. Opening Meeting.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of the session of the Literary and Philosophical society was held io the Masonic Hall, Bold•street, Warrington, on Monday evening. On the proposition of thq Hon. Secretary (Mr. G. CARRUTHERS), Miss Annie Porritt was electtd a member. _ . _</p>
        <p>In the absence of the Vice-President. Mr J Rymer Young, Mr Charles White presided. Toe Rev. MORLEY Stevenson, president. addressed the members on" Too aim and object of our Society, with some thouelats oe its future idevelopinent." The CHAIRMAN observed that the subject would be familiar to him as one of the first officials of th seciety and though not one at the present time he always took the greateat possible interest in the society and re- frained from being on the Council 89 that the younger thinkers in the town might join it. The PRESIDENT said they were all aware that the Rev. W 11 Drummond was elected president of the society for this session. They would also b 3 aware that Mr. Drum- mond had been called to worn in Ireland, and had,thete - fore, left the town. To his very great regret, as well as theirs he was obliged to retinvieh the post of presi- dent. Tney extremely regretted the loss of their right and lawful President. They were very glad if the new sphere of work to which he had been called was one that was congenial to him and would promote his use- fulness and work, but at the same time he (Mr. Steven- son) felt that the society had been distinctly the loser by his removal from the town. (Hear. hear.) Tney had all looked forward to hiving him as president this session. Ever since he came to the town ho had been a prominent and useful member of the society, and had delighted them nor only by his papers but by the tveful practical and intellectual!reinarks which from tim a to time he had made in their discussions. Tney would have had in him one who would have filled the pesition of president worthily and who would have sustained the high reputation of the society. They regretted the loss of Mr. Drummond from the society, and although they were very glad to know that he was coming back during the session to give them a paper, they felt that the society had in his sud- den removal experienced a very decided blow. Mr. Drummond's departure was communicated to him dur- ing his holidays, and according to the rules of the society he was again their presieent and expected Co contribute the opening paper. Be had not hal time to prepare a paper, and decided to speak to three on the am and object of the society, with some thoughts on its future development, chiefly with a desire to promote a useful and profitable discussion. The society was com- mencing upon the 30th year of its existence, having been founded in 1870. In 1677 a si,;.ilar address to the one he was delivering was given by Mr. Charles White, who reviewed the seven years' history of the society and summed up some of its achievements in literature and science. In 1870 there existed in War- rington what was called the Microscopical Society. Geatlernen mot together at each other's houses and studied microscopical science. It was suggested that the society might do a useful work by extending its boundary. It was necessarily a small society, and it was felt that very useful work in the realms of litera- ture and science might be done if the society could be spread and enlareed. In 1870 toe Microscopical Society met to discuss this matter, and determined to found the Literary and Philosophical Society. The first president was Mr. Glazebrook Hylands. In his presidential address he referred to the fact that in 1612 a similar seciety was started in Warrington, and lived for eight years, which, he supposed, was fora society of that kind a long life. fie also predicted for the present society a longer life than the previous one. That prediction they who lived further down the street of time could see bad been fulfilled. Their society was now 30 years old, and as far as they could see showed no signs of decay. (Hear, hear). The numbers of the members kept up, the quality of the papers was good, and he confidently looked forward to a bright future for the society. The society seemed to him to have in many ways justified its existence. It was founded for tee advancement of literature and science, and that that object had been achieved by the many papers read before tee society no one could doubt. Its object had been turther achieved by the fact that it had given birth to other intellec- tual societies. In the realms of science, he thought he was right in saying that the present Field Club had sprung from the Literary and Philosophical Society. They all knew that the Field Club for a town of the size of Warrington bad done and was doing a most excellent scientific work. In the realm of literature, be believed he was right in saying that the whole system of the University Extension Lectures carried on irs the town for years was esti a child of their society. They might hope to do similar work in the future. (Hear, hear.) Cer- tainly there was no organisation which had a better right to be the foster parent of such efforts [ban their society. They must recognise the excellence of much of the work done by the society. Considerine the size of the town the society would bear comparison with those in any part of the country as regarded the excellence of many of the papers to which they had listened. He should like in addition to call attention to the excellence of the speeches which had followed the reading of be papers. The success of the society depended entirely upon the interest taken in it by its members. IThe weak point of their society was that they had so many piper members, members whose names were on the list, whose subscriptions, he hoped, were in the bands of the treasurer—(laughter)--but whose atten- ante was very rare indeed. The very raison d etre of a society sach as theirs should prevent that sort of mem- bership. He did not see why they should join unless they intended to take part in the meetings. (Hear, bear.) This ought to be a real gathering together of the intellect of the town. He hoped they would spread an interest in the society in the future, and that the new saembere they made would not be simply those they captured for the sake of their names and subscriptions, but those who would join their ranks because they wished to be students and wished to lead an intellectual life. By joining such a society they were able to listen to the fruits of the study of those who had made a special subject their own • a distinct stimulus to study was given, and ladies and gentlemen who differed in many ways could meet together there in peace, unity, and communion. (Hear, hear.) He might sum up by saying that the society taught them how to hear, how to reed and how to speak. With regard to suggestions for the future, he advocated something on the lines of a home reading circle, as tending to give a definite aim to their reading, which in the majority of cases was of a too desultory character. There might also be an exhibi- tion of objects of interest on some special night. (Ap- plause.) The CHAIRMAN said with regard to what bad been said about a reading unien, the idea was an admirable one, but he did not see hew such a union could be affiliated with the society. It might be brought out separately. He also suggested that the President would speak to them on some interesting event of the month. A discussion followed, in which the Rev. F. E. Powell and Messrs. Madeley, May and Greening took part. It was understood that the suggestions in the Presi- dent's add te.:s would received the consideration of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOURERS</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOURERS</p>
        <p>In Conference at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Autumnal Convention in connection with the Warrington and District Union of Young People's Societies of Christian Endeavour, which was held on Saturday, was very meagrely attended. The proceedings commenced in the afternoon with a meeting in th 3 Workingmen's Mission Chapel, Liverpool-road, which was presided over by Mr. tieorge Dale. ' The CHAIRMA.N delivered a short and appropriate ' address in which he urged upon his hearers the value of unity in the work of Christian Endeavour Rev J F MATTHEws, the president of the society read a paper on "rhe future of the active member,' in which he laid stress upon the value of reading good books, adding that too much time was wasted in recreation and more time was spent in physical than in mental culture. The active member, while having to take an active part in God's work must be familiar with good literature, and a specialist in thinking and working. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>s'lr. A. MOUNFIELD— and the Rev. T LAMB also addressed the gathering. lathe evening a public meeting took place in the new school,, Thewlis-street, the chair being occu- pied by the Rev J F Matthews, the president of the Union.</p>
        <p>In the course of his remarks, the chairman said there was no great movement which ever proved a magnificent success, unless the fervour and passion of the individuals comprising the movement were ( dominated by divine enthusiasm. It must not be r spasmodic, but long and continuous, like a. magni- ficent river which ever increased in power and volume until it reached the mighty ocean where it emptied itself. They in Warriogton were contem- plating holding a great mission, the object of which was to get at those who did not know Christ and create a spiritual sentiment. Drink, gambl- ing. and other vices, would, if they did not receive a death blow, receive such a blow they had never experienced for many a year. This could only be brought about by the concentrated enthu- siasm of youth, used in the Master's service. They would have noticed on the part of the great par- ties in the State, that not only a great deal of en- thusiasm had been introduced in the election, but astuteness had also been exercised. They wanted to be astute for Christ. Their Roman Catholic friends were exceedingly astute in laying hold of boys and girls and training them in such a manner that they would be safe for the rest of their life. The endeavour mevement should bring up the children by astute means, in the Pro- testant and Free Church principles. They also required unselfishness ta win people to Christ. Last but not least, their services must be character- ised by warmth, sympathy and unselfishness. (Applause). The Rev. C. FINCH said he had been interested in the movement since its inauguration. Since he had been in this district he bad badthe privilege of being the founder of a society at Widnes and of a society in connection with Legh-street Chapel. There were many features about the society of which he highly approved. One was the opportunity the society gave to its members for Biblical study. The Bible had God for its author and Truth for its motto: The time was coming when all books would lose their interest except the Bible. Therefore let them be led by its counsels and they would one day stand in the presence of Him whom it revealed, when they would again thank God for his wonderful book. The Rev. JOHN YONGN also delivered an address and said he sympathised very cordially with the movement. He only regretted that the movement did not seem to have "caught on" in Warrington as it had done in other towns, but he thought those who had been engaged in the work might thank God for past mercies and take courage. Although they had not gathered together the large numbers of young people whom they had desired to lay hold of it was a pleasant thing to know that substantial spiritual work had been done in the Sunday schools and churches by the movement. The movement would be more and more successful if the mensbees worked accerding to their motto, "For Christ and the Church." It was very easy to have and to forget a raotto. The motto of the Christian Endeavour was a very good and sugges- tive one. They could not have had a better, and if they never forgot it the movement would greatly in- crease in extent. The motto seemed to assume that as Christian Endeavourers they recognised that they were living by Christ,and in Christ,and it summoned all to live for Him. They must live a Christian life under all enterprises and relationships possible. He urged them to avoid everything that partook of the merely sentimental and emotional character. There was no earthly reason why they should be miserable because they were religious. A great deal would be accomplished if they were faithful to their own church services and set an example to those who did not belong to a society. They should attend both services and detest the " half-time " business on the Lord's day. It was laziness on the part of some of the young men. They claimed that they wanted rest on Sunday. What was the Satur- day afternoon holiday for? It was really meant for rest, but they made themselves more tired by cycling,&amp;c. Out of the 64,000 persons in Warring- ton not more than 20,000 ever went to Church. He referred to the coming simultaneous mission, and expressed the hope that all members would take an active part in it. The work, he added, could only be done by the young people. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Ohmy's Circus, The large audiences which have assembled at the circus every evening during the past week, have testified to tbe increasing popularity of this place of amusement. The programme attains the usual high standard which visitors to the circus have become accustomed to expect, numerous original and up- to-date " turns" being introduced. Those desirous of witnessing a really astounding exhibition of sword swallowing should not fail to see the performance by Chevalier Cliquot. This artiste goes through a series of remarkable feats, including the swallowing of twenty two inches of a United States sabre, a bayonet, fif- teen tin swords, and a watch and chain. To prove the genuiness of the last named accomplishment, gentlemen in the audience are invited to come into the ring and listen to the watch ticking in the per- former's inside. His concluding item is the swal- lowing of a sword to which an electric lamp is at- tached, and the light can be distinctly seen through his throat, by the onlookers. It may be stated that the sabre and bayonet, are tested by military men, selected from the audience, to show their stability. In addition to this sensational act there iscapi- tal variety entertainment. One of Warrington's favourites, Mr Harry Dale, the well-known jester, who was formerly landlord of the Hop Pole Inn. makes his re-appearance after an absence of a few years and contributes a number of original poems, songs, and selections on the concertina. A recitation introducing the names of every public house in Warrington is an especially popular item. Hanlon and Fernandez give a diffi- cult performance on the rings, and are adepts in their particular line of business. Claude Ohmy scores a great success in a skilful juggling exhibi- tion on the rolling globe, and young Reiff, in his jockey act, displays marked ability as an eques- trian. The Padley Brothers are exceedingly clever contortionists, and their comic boxing act almost convulses the house with laughter. Miss Transfield and Miss Ada Ohmy are da•hing bare-back raders. A band of clowns furnish excellent comic relief" and the whole entertainment is one which cannot fail to please the most fastidious.</p>
