      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIA'NS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse on Wednesday merning. Mr A J Jolley presided, and there were also present :—Dr Patterson, Rev T R Matthews, Airs Clay, Messrs J H Ashcroft, J Billinge, A Brooks, J Clarke, P Cunliffe S Davies, R Forshaw, W Hickman, H Hitberr, W Houghton, A Jolley,G Pilling,H Thornton.with J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk), C H Ward (master) and A Wright (superintendent).</p>
        <p>INCRiASED •ItXPENDITURiI.-" BOX RONR'S FOLLY."</p>
        <p>Cheques were signed for the payment of accounts amounting to £3,109 134 7d. Mr THORNTON asked if the expenditure could not be kept down. The CHAIRMAN observed that the only thing was for committees to exercise care in regard to their expenditure.</p>
        <p>Mr. iiicKmAN : The only thing :is to grin and pay. (Laughter). The CLEnK hoped the various committees would take note of the fact that the expenditure was increasing.</p>
        <p>Mr. DAV.IES (indicating a booklet descriptive of the Padgate Cottage Homes) : We are paying £2O here for someone's folly. The CIE/UFA:IAN did not think the remark a fair one. They could not, however, discuss it now. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY ; He will take a book away with him. (Laughter). Mr. HII3BEBT Twenty shillings not £2O. GIFTS.</p>
        <p>The Workhouse MASTER acknowl.dged the receipt of fruit from Holy Trinity Church, Miss Crosfield, Walton Lea,Miss Green,Arpley Mission,and Mrs Tar. buck ; papers from Mrs Crosfield and Mrs Ashcroft ; plants from Mrs Broadbent, Mrs Frost, Mrs It Gar- nett, and bulbs from Miss Crosfield; vases and flowers from Mrs Fox, Bylands-street. Mr Wright (superintendent of the schools), also acknowledged the Rift of two hampers of apples from Mr William Houghton.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE AND PADGATE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>A deputation from the School Attendance Com- mittee consisting of Alderman Hutchinson and Councillors Ellison, Peacock and Duncan, who visited the Padgate Schools on behalf of the Com- mittee expressed,in a report,their cordial congratu- lations to the members of the Board on the Perfect manner in which the institution appeared to be managed. The cleanliness and smart appearance of the children left nothing to be desired.</p>
        <p>fe:-74 st,t -$Ol •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CHAPLAINCY.</head>
        <p>THE CHAPLAINCY.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote apprzving of the appointment of the Rev. W Pollock Hill as chaplain of the Padgate fichoole.</p>
        <p>4 THE DEATH OF AN INMATE. LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY ' TO BE HELD. The CLERK read a letter from the Local Govern- ment Board stating that in response to a request by the Board of Guardians an official inquiry would be made into the circumstances attenaing the ad- mission into the Workhouse and the subsequent death of Seth Wood. Dub notice would be given of the time and place of the inquiry. A small committee was appointed to deal with the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>OFFICIALS' SALARIES.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS' SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read an application from Mr. T. H. kukland, relieving officer of No. 3 district of the Union, for an increase of salary. He stated that be was appointed in 1893 at a salary of £95, which in 1894 was increased to £lO5. He also received about £5 as vaccination officer.</p>
        <p>The Board considered the application in commit- tee. It was moved that a committee be appointed to consider the general question of the officials salaries. An amendment to the effect that it be dealt with by the whole Board was lost. A further amendment that the matter be deferred for a month was then carried.</p>
        <p>This was all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.—The first public meeting of the session was held in Hill dire Schoulroom on Monday evening, when there was an unusually large attendance. The chg ir was occupied by the Rev. J S Hughes (president of the society), who was supported on the platform hy the Rev E T Reed, Messrs. A H Dunn,and G Whitfield,secretary. An interesting programme had been arranged, which was admirably carried out. The proceedings opened with an instrumental trio—first and second violins and piano—by Masters B and P Moss, and Miss Esther Fair Moss, these young instrumentalists acquitting them- selves very creditably in their two selections, " Ora pro Nobis " and "True Blue." Miss Mounfield gave a capital rendering of the songs " A Dream of ParAise " and "The Harbour Bay," and was loudly applauded' A selection on the mandoline by Mr. T Lloyd was much enjoyed, whilst Mr. John o,borne was heard to actva‘itage in his soncs, "The Village Blacksmith" and " Ihe Miner." The songs of the young friends Miss Ada and Masters John and Percy Grounds were loudly applauded. Recitations were given by Miss Barbara LAWIe, Nellie Whitfield,and Masters Joseph and George Arthur Whitfield. The newly-formed Band of Hope Choir made its debut on this occasion, under the care of Miss Alice Woodward,as organist, and delighted the audience with the cho) us •‘ Britannia's Sons." An address on " The effects of alcohol, on the human body," Maw reed by diagrams, was given by the Rev Edward T Reed. One of the most attractive items was the dialogue " A drunken spree, and what came of it," which was well received, the following members representing the various characters : —Messrs George Moss, B Ken worthy. S Moss,W Wilde, J Worrall, E Wilde and B Moss ; and Misses S J Worrall and Clara Kenworthy. During the evening a prize,con- sisting of a teachers' Bible, was awarded to Septimus Moss, by the Rev J S Hughes, for having brought the largest number of new members to the Band of Hope last session. A similar prize was also offered by Mr Hughes to the boy who induces the largest number to sign the pledge during the present session,Mr A El Dunn also offering one to the girls on the same conditions. Miss Alice Woodward proved hers-lf a most efficient accompanis:, to the various musical items.</p>
        <p>SECONDHAND CYCLES t cleared ch.ao. from 3's.--J. S. GARLK:. o, Ironmongc 51 to 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
