      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. [By " Ovalist.l</p>
        <p>Three successive aefeats have dispelled the illusion, if there was any, that Warrington were going to lick creation and most of the Lancashire clubs as well, this season. It was hoped that Swinton would not be allowed to repeat last year's success, but they did so, though again by only a small margin. Oee try to nil was the final score, and the winners were much the better team. This does not mean that they overplayed the home lot, for the opposite was the case, bat they were remarkably smart in the back division, and will want a lot of defeating with such accompiished manipu- latcrs of the ball. Warrington's superiority lay in deter- mination, and the strength and vigour of the forwards. The latter were in their best humour, and deserved to have pulled the game out of tbe fire.</p>
        <p>There is something radically wrong with the War- rington back division, which is particularly harmless and weak in attack, whila the defence might be im- proved. The men do not play together somehow,though Ido not think it is from any unwillingness. To begin with the centres are weak. Isherwood being far below form, and so far be has given nothing approaching his displays of last seaion, when he and Fish were as good a left wing as any in the caunty. The fact that Fisk has only scorcd one try, and that in the opening match, is proof positive that something is wrong, and it does not seem to be with Fish himself. Ha would be as pro- lific a scorer as ever were he only looked after- It is the general combination which is at fault. Sometimes the half backs are to blame, and sometimes an opening is made which should have resulted in peints if the cen- tres only backed up the half back. But generally speaking there is no system of attack and until the men start playing a combined game they will never win matches. It is a long time since the passing was ea uncertain and useless as it is at present. In nearly every match Warrington get possession two times out of three and yet the scoring is conspicuous by its absence.</p>
        <p>Saturday's lesson would profit Warrington if they took it to heart for nothing finer than the way the Swinton backs played every move on the board and relied on one another could have been wished for. Yon never saw a Swinton half-back break away and then find all his three-quarters 20 yards in the rear. No! There were always at least two levul with him and wait- ing for the pass. Surely our centres are equal to follow- ing up their own halves. The latter also should have learnt something. They saw no sticking to the ball on the other side. It was promptly sent along to the wings, who were, in conse- quence, always dangerous. I think the team is capable of much better work, though there is still a need for at least one claB3 man. Let us see the improvement early and to begin with thrash Wigan on Saturday. We won there last season and it will be a big disappointment if two points are not forthcoming this time.</p>
        <p>The Club was very well supported on Saturday, and the solid crowd round the rails must have made a grati. Eying spectacle for the officials. Swinton hal a fair following alsa. By the way Jim Valentine was absent last season, the first time on which his team ever won at Warrington, and people were saying on Saturday that he was never going to see them win, meaning of course that they were doomed to defeat. " Jim's" ambition has been realised, however, for he led his men to victory on Saturday, though he had legs to do with the win himself than usual. Be does Lot shice very much among the forwards, but he has his uses all the same.</p>
        <p>A high wind considerably interfered with play OH Saturday, but the game was a good one all the sarne. Play was very even and often exciting, while the skilful tactics of the Blues were always a source of pleasure to the onlookers. Warrington won the toss and made such poor use of the wind that they never penetrated the Swinton defence once. In fact they were as near doing so in the second half with the gale against them and Swinton playing to score. It is jast pcssible we should have done better to have lost the toss. There is no excuse for the poor play in the first half however. A couple of tries at least should have been obtained from the chances there were floating about. Fish had the hardest of luck, his individual effort just before the interval meriting better fate. It was just a question whether he was pulled down short of the 'inc and scrambled over after,but most referees would have given the try in. He certainly deserved it. Swinton were destined to have the luck, for two penalty kicks went very near to lowering the goal of the Blues.</p>
        <p>I On the home side Hallam gave far and away the best display,:his tactics and ability in the second half being of the highest class. He has improved, and no mistake. Few men kick with more power or better judg- ment, while in fielding the ball old " Surefoot " himself was hardly better; Hallam has undoubedtly found his right place and I hope he will keep it. Fish also played well, and was always a match for Lewis until the Swinton flier scored, when, as at Broughton, Fish seemed out of his place. He had better pay atten- tion to the fact that all the scoring of late has been on his wing. Bai P and Burgess both played a good game, but were no match for the visiting pair, while the re- maining three-quarters were " oph." The forwards worked well to a man. On the Swinton aide Bob Valentine played a strong. reliable garne,Lewis being the best of the wings. Dai Davies was the real " star " hiawever, and his display at half was tip-top. Be has improved a lot since last season and deserves the County cap he has won. The forwards were big but did not appear to be over vigorous. They are the only weak sp3t in the Swinton team.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire League table up-to-date is ag follows : For. Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G T. P. T. P. P. Swinton Runcorn St. Helens Leigh Oldnam Salford Rochdale Hornets 5... 2... 1... 2... h 5 25... 6 5 27... 6 Broughton R'r.g's 6... 3... 3... 0... 2 7 25... 3 8— 30... 6 Stockport Warrington 6. 2.. 3. 1.. 3 6 24... 3 7 27... 5 Wigan blillom Vildnes 6. 1 5... 0_ 4 3 17... 7 14— b 6... 2 Barrow</p>
        <p>Warrington's position has alteady to he reckoned from the oottom, which is tar from satisfactory. We are in very poor oimpany at present and it is high time the team sec. about climbing the league ladder.</p>
        <p>To-day's match is at Wigan where Warrington got two points last season pretty easily. They will have to fight harder this time, or lam much mistaken• lhe Colliers have not been setting the Douglas on fire, but they have been playing a game superior to anything they were capable of la,t soosun. Wariingtort have been doing the, opposite, so that they may expect a atiff struggle. Stilt we can win if the men only put their hacks into it and try. It th• y have any combination and smartness left: in them, Wigan is the sort of club they ought to liy no difficulty in showing it against.</p>
      </div>
