        <p>IT was Lord BEACONS- Honour and Politics. FIELD who said that "in politics there is no honour." The statement ought not to be true, and it is not more than half true, at any time. When public men are lost to all sense of honour they are unfit to lead a party, or to govern a country, or to take part in its administration. But at an election time there is constantly a lapse from the high standard of truth, personal honour, and patriotism which is upheld at other times. ; We have seen some sad examples of this fall in the present electoral campaign. When :opponents are vilified as traitors, as enemies of their country,and as dishonourable men, party politics are degraded and individual reputations must suffer. Mr. BAL- FOUR ought, for instance, to be above the tempta- tion to decry men who differ from him on political questions and have formed an entirely independent ideal of what is best for this country. Yet we find him, in his latest deliverance, declaring that one- third of the Opposition "had never disguised their hatredlof England and their favour for the enemies of England." This may be a remark solely intended for the Irish Nationalists, who are fighting for their own cause, and if it be so it is subject to this qualification—that it is political and not personal. The Irish Nationalists desire self-rule within the lines of the Imperial Constitu- tion, and because they think that even the beaten Boers of South Africa would be better off than they will when the final settlement comes, they are hostile to this country—but on this ground only. If the remark applies to any English or Scotch members it is untrue, and it ought never to have been made. To say that any opponent of the policy pursued in South Africa is sold ,to the Boers is an accusation which Mr. Chamberlain has withdrawn, and attributes, conveniently, to a telegraphic mistake. The Liberal ideal is neither dishonourable nor unpa- triotic. Where the country would have been but for Liberal statesmen, measures, and foreign policy, the historians have decided for themselves. It is net for party politicians to challenge their decisions. The facts are beyond dispute. Some of the electors who have been misled by silly allegations owe their political power to the Liberal party,which has believed in the people and worked for them in season and out of season. Several constituencies have manifested a higher regard for personal and national honour,than the members of the Administration. Mr. John Morley has been a persistent opponent of the policy which led to the war, and yet Montrose has returned him in his absence, and without his even ' having made a single speech. Mr. John al.'s, whose private letter to a British lady in Capetown has played such an extraordinary part in the campaign, has been again returned by his old constituents. They have not doubted either his personal honour or his patriotism. Illustra- tions of this kind could be increased if we could find space to set them out in detail. It is stis- factsry that they can be found, and that political men are not wholly at the mercy of traducers and ,1 the ;nventors of spiteful epithets. The number of Liberal members returned is also evidence of the existence ef a sense of honour in politics that is very gratifying. It would, indeed, be a bad out- look for this country if the independent action of members of Parliament, if different ideals, and ways of looking at any public question, exposed them to imputations which swept them out of public life because persons were found credulous enough to believe them. Patriotism is a 'quality, which cannot be squeezed into OHO mould only and honour is as compatible with Liberalism as it is with Conservatism. We are all patriots, each one in his own way, and we ought to be so jealotas of our honour as to scout the men who would assail it for temporary objects. The bitterness of the present contests would have been avoided if the Government had been honourable enough to go to the country on a new register, and to have refrained from screaming out " traitor " and "Boer "to every opponent.</p>
        <p>THE war in South Africa A Year's War, has now been going on for a year, and it is confi- dently predicted thcit it is near the end. We sin- cerely hope that it is. The country has been called upon to make very great sacrifices,and it has made them cheerfully. All classes have contributed to its successful undertaking. In the dark days the response of the Volunteers and the Yeomanry is not readily to be forgotten, and if some members of Parliament who were absent during the current elections have been beaten at the polls it can be no reflection on their sound patriotism or their general politics. What a wonderful year it has been ! The Government entered upon the war with the idea</p>
        <p>that about 70,000 men would suffice to defeat the' Boers. This time last year there were confident predictions that the war would be over before Christmas. The resources of the Boers in men and artillery, in stragetic and tactical skill, were much underrated. It was not thought the foreign contingents would be half as numerous as they turned out to be, or that the military , adventurers of the world would have rendered such substantial help to AO invaders of British territory. Nor was it iniagined that the Boer artillery would so completely out- class our own, or that the Boers would be so well versed in the art of making entrenchments and schanzes to defend the positions they ocenpied• Their shooting has been much better than vie bad expected, and the Mauser rifle, in their hands, has proved to be an effective and even a terrible weapon. The opening pha,es of the cainPalga were followed closely for evidences of our vast superiority in skill and valour, and the Battle of 6r-lencoe was hailed as a brilliant victory before we understood its real significance. Thenceforward for many weeks we were under grave apprehensiene' and our forces in the field had to be very considf ably augmented until we had more than '200,0t" men under arms in South Africa, guarding com- munications and making careful advances bY the several railways which led. into the interior. Ine sieges of Mafeking, Kimberley and Ladysmith are bright spots in the war ; but there were attendant misfortunes which ought never to have occurred' and which did not raise the estimate of our militarY prowess in Europe. Lord ROBERT3'S brilliant ad" Vance, first towards Kimberley, and then to Bloo°. fontein and Pretoria, seemed to encourage the be lief that the war would soon be over. But montb! elapsed after he had made Pretoria his beau- with quarters, and still the Boers held out, most wonderful tenacity and skill, attaclollo our communications, eluding our pursuit, and sar" rendering one day only to reappear the next. 118 time it has taken to deal with the dispersed coin' mandoes has been much longer than we ever es.- pected. The Boers made their secret depots of arnis and ammunition, and recovered them whenever they grew short; and one or two daring leaders, OuvrEn and DE WET,gave us an immense arannn of trouble. The after-parts of the war testifY t° the great knowledge of the country displayed 14 the Boer leaders, and the bravery of the inee, under them, as well as the sympathy of the tants in whatever quarter they were fighting. 1116 qualities displayed by our troops have been inest remarkable. They have made long and tedious marches, in a ragged and half-starved condition' They have surmounted many obstacles, they have borne cheerfully all the hardships of a most er hausting campaign, and they have commanded the admiration of the world. But our officers to° not come out so well as our private soldiers lias.e, done. There have been " regrettable incidents which ought never to have occurred. There have been carelessness, blundering and neglect. On complete survey of the ycar's war we find man:: thingsgto deplore, and the genius of Lord RonEaT has saved us from threatened misfortune.</p>
        <p>The electors of Northwich Division have now had ample time to make up their minds on the allt; important question of who to voto for, if they havt previously had any doubts. We believe a great majority of :the electors of this district have needed needed to trouble themselves ever, for for it will take more than the we" 7-,rao ments and unproven accusations of Mr. shake the constituency's faith in its " Id love, J. T. Brunner, who has rendered yen? 3,,ll;nsels vice to the country ever since he had a affairs. In addition, too, he has helpoi uIS stituents in innumerable ways and with !arra° hearted generosity. These are things which. we not think the electors of Northwich Division itd likely to forget for a moment, but we nevertbele.; counsel them to religiously exercise the francl e and make Sir John's majority this week-end °lit that will add lustre even to the "one bright sP° in Cheshire."</p>
        <p>• is The report of the Philippine Commission d intended to reassure public feeling in the 150 hi , States with regard to Uncle Sam's hold over .11 , new possessions, but it is doubtful whether it 'chi, really have that effect. It is too obvious that ?•,!Commissioners have shut their eyes as rellc,/ possible to the military difficulties which are 011'1," themselves manifest in a series of small disaste'e while they are by no means oblivious of the g that within a few weeks the Presidential electiorio are to take place. The document is said to h 5 been drawn up at Mr. McKinley's request,and itiod too evident that in their kindness of heart, or ho by their own political bias they have said,t6,o things which Mr. McKinley desired t,'":;io to say. The Commissioners speak of the Fibr, , forces as broken up into predatory bands, ladrones, they say, which existed under Spaltli , rule in the islands; but they forget to add Ithe movements of these bands—guerrillas, 10 Igents, or ladrones, whatever they may be ealleing harassed the Spanish forces continuously cluir'—',l a century, and gave the interior of the islae°s.ss Ipractical independence of foreign rule. Theriell tone of optimism throughout the report W' of , conflicts strangely with the expressed opinioll, military experts. The present conditions resi., it allowed to remain until after the election, br is more probable that before the winter is ov-the great force of 100,000 men will have to go Philippines to to subdue finally the unwilling 5' jocts of the Republic.</p>
        <p>Whatever the grumblings about the incoun c- once of a General Election', we may take sstrf tion to ourselves that our constitutional inachrT pit 3 funs easier than that of most countries. :T;ths ook at America we find that four weary ru'i," use are occupied in gross scurrilities and vulgar 600 precedent to the choice of a President. If to the German Press we see that citizens (Dirt" the speak with any measure of frankness ahn part the Head of the Empire plays in the are of Europe. Five sentences for lese jfje chronicled in the Berlin newspapers in tvir°;:ot the most significant being the condenmat',-„- tba the editor of a well-known newspaper to ai-11310 to imprisonment in a fortress. It is conceive - conceive of a parallel case in Great Britaiet ,od cause our own Sovereign does not shew herr T: 'an in politics. It is really as supreme t:3-elir)gu Minister, standing above the so-called l'n-ret-he Minister of the German Government, „tiliitittbi° Kaiser in the interests of the State derua,-;:sting immunity from criticism. It would he im•n`; to see what the public would think if a sugg'a-tlar were made to invest Lord Salisbury wall ,s`7,tlioo sanctity, and to try every case of alleged imra-e, ju m camera. That is what they are doing of Germany. Not only is the right of freedol33ted. speech but the right of public trial abregfirie is r . oy,ali There need be no wonder that Socialism g there, or that men veil their utterances. Wesvol know the story of the man arrested because be .ser overheard in conversation to assert that the IL“lthe, was a fool. He protested that he meant rot Russian Kaiser. But the Police officer would hear of the explanation. "You must have 15,110 ours," he said. "There is but one ICaiser spoken of in that way !" • ussio° The most important subject under disc ea at the meeting of the Warrington Town Co. 12134 last week, was that re ferring to the dredgmg_'et the river. The rnainte Dance of a dredged clum.ll t the is of vital importance to many of the see :tha 0„ of the town, and we are glad to Rivers Committee are determined to .10 th' the Ship Canal Company strictly industries</p>
