      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>ligations. For some time past the work' of dredging has been perfunctorily done With disastrous effects upon the navigableness of the river. Vessels having a draught of:six feet have found it impossible to proceed from Bank Quay to Walton Lock, and if the matter is allowed to go on Rs it has done latterly the river will be practically unnavigable. The railway companies would pro- bably rejoice at such a state of things but it would certainly re-act unfavourably on the commercial stability .of the Borough The importance of cheap freights which the river gives cannot be over- estimated, and in taking steps to insist upon the town's rights in this matter, the Corporation will have the support of every well-wisher of War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOM EN T</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOM EN T</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>There will be many a happy homestead in the Provinces at Christmas, if it be true, as is more than Whispered and there is good reason to believe, that the majority of the gallant lads who have spent so Inany weary, arduous months on the battle-stained Veldt of South Africa, will be home by that time. Lord Roberts, it is said, is endeavouring to arrange matters to this end, though a large force will necessarily have to stay behind for some time yet. "The Guards are coming home, boys; the Guards are coming home," as the song runs, and several battalions of these and of the Volunteers are due at home next month. It is hoped also that the Re- servists will be found homeward-bound before Yuletide. These should, indeed, have prior con- sideration, seeing how many situations, as well as families and friends, are awaiting them. They will no doubt be very glad to settle down once more to the occupations they followed in the piping times of peace.</p>
        <p>- There has always been a literary flavour about the British Parliament. The last two Liberal Premiers, as well as Lord Salisbury and the late Lord Beaconsfield, ,' may be mentioned, to begin at the top. Then Mr. Arthur Balfour, although often described as a landowner, is a author of some pro- fundity. On the other hand, Mr. Joseph Morley is a very acceptable writer, while even more so, per- haps, having regard to his well-rounded pleasan- tries, is Sir William Harcourt. Lord Avebury, better known as Sir John Lubbock, or as Saint Lubbock, is something more than a banker; and Mr James Bryce is an author of good standing. Mr Augustine Birrell,who has been unfortunate enough to lose his seat, is one of the few really entertaining essayists who continue to be read. Journalists have long abounded in the House of Commons, among the best known being Mr. Labouchere, Mr. Justin McCarthy, Sir John Willox, Mr. C. P. Scott, "Tay Pay:" Sir John Leng, and Mr. Maclean. Mr. Leonard Courtney, who recently resigned his seat, Once did much journalistic work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Beaumont, candidate for North Bucks, has this week been credited with introducing the phonograph into electioneering. But this is a mis- take. Mr F.l Horniman, member for the Penryn and Falmouth constituency, has a" prior claim "to that distinction, having sent his phonograph from London into that far away division, instead of transporting himself, in the 1895 election. But it Was not a decided success; the interruptions of the audience and the voice of the absent one as heard through the " phono " resulting in the opposite of a combination of sweets sounds, and becoming absurdly " mixed " and disappointing. Mr Beau to.ont's excuse for sending several instruments into the various parts of the North Bucks constituency Ilas been that he could not attend them all in person, which seems reasonable and plausible enough. But in 1895 the Cornish constituents in- vented other pleas, not very flattering to Mr llorni- man, as candidate.</p>
        <p>--- From the final reports on the state of the crops, It is only too clear that the promise of 11ay, or July rather, was not fulfilled. So that wheat and barley, in particular, cut a poor 'figure in comparison with the crops of last year, and, with the exception of roots and grass, nothing turned out quite so well as had been expected. Taking the entire country, there has been a decline all round in the condition of crops on the Ist of this month as compared with the. let of September;. but the forage crops are, nevertheless, in a more" promising condition than has been the case at this date for &amp;number of years past. . -</p>
        <p>-- -- The annual report of the Labour Department of the Board of Trade for 1899 affords very instruc- tive and entertaining reading. It is evident that the wage-earning classes had little cause for com- plaint last year. The percentage of unemployed members of Trades Unions was less than at any time since 1890. The aggregate rise of wages was £115,000 per week, while one very satisfactory feature is that the number of persons whose wages were increased as the result of strikes only amounted to about three per cent. of the whole working-class community. The figures quoted afford conclusive evidence of the continued activity of trade ; but the returns for the present year bid fair to show even better results, as already the wages of about a million workers have been affected and the increased payments for labour amount to the large sum ot £150,000 per week. Clearly the Working men of England, and the United Kingdom are wise in their generation, in deciding to pursue the even tenour of their way, without resorting so hastily to the expedient of strikes as in some pre- vious years.</p>
        <p>The professional waiter everywhere has long been a man of many grievances, and it is not because he Z 8 " nr)re so" in London than in the provinces, that the demonstration in Trafalgar Square on Sunday, appeare'l to be confined to a number of waiters bent Upon voicing an appeal to the London County Coun- cil. The " .nass meeting," it seems was under the wing of the Amalgamated Waiters' Society, and the "tip system" was again the evil complained of. Thousands of these waiters have to depend upon the douceur of patrons for their livelihood, and many not only work without salary, but have to pay the hotel propri6 tor four to five shillings a day for the privilege of waiting. The greater the tip the more obseq lious the waiter. The man who does not give, or who cannot afford to give, has to wait proportionately longer for the "Coming, sir," with which the waiter responds to the call of his gener- ous diner. One way to bring about a change would be to cease to "tip,' but thus the present genera- tion would starve for the benefit of posterity Besides which the " tip " system if it ever can or will die at all, must die very hard.</p>
        <p>The "latest from Paris," is a proposal to check the automobile scorcher by means of a cinemato- graph apparatus with which every policeman is to be sup plied, so that the rates of speed can be ascer- tained, and the offending vehicle be identified. The Proposal is under consideration, and is said to be 4` relatively" simple. Many English people will be glad to hear that it is as simple as it appears,betore being tried, extravagant and hopeless. If something could be devised to capture cyclists who run down deaf aad elderly people, and then disappear more quickly even than they come, there are places in the provinces where it, would be esteemed a God- send. The other day, at Walsall, an elderly man was thus run down by a cyclist, who left him bleed- ing and unconscious, and was not heard of again. The old man died of his injuries, and the jury have returned the customary verdict, "Accidental death!" " Manslaughter by some person unknown at pre- sent " would have seemed more appropriate, but provincial juries are not educated to this point set.</p>
        <p>Many persons will be pleased to know that con- fucerable success has atteuted the new treatment of lupus by strong concentrated rays of light, 'which, it has been found, tend to destroy the bac- teria that are the cause of the disease. Sufferers from this dreadful disease may take new hope from the fact that apparatus have been fitted up at the London Hospital for the treatment of lupus, through the goodness of the Princess of \Vales, and it.may therefore, be reasonably expected that there Will be a reduction in the number of cases of this kind.</p>
        <p>BIBLE CLASS SOCIAL AT NEWTON. In connection with bt. Peter's Bible Class, the second annual social gathering was held in the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday evening. There Were about 70 persons present, and dancing was cotamei;ced at about half-post seven, and was kept 11P with spirit until nearly 11 o'clock. At intervals recitations and song i were given by Messrs. J. H Lane, Perry, Mather and Houghton. Misses Wilkin- 13„son and Baybut. A short address was given by the 4eT J H Ryder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SEVEN DAYS FOR STEALING SIXPENCE</p>
        <p>At Widnei Police Court on Monday Jonn Davies, a pedlar, was sent to gaol for seven thys for steal ing sixpence from trio till of a shop in Victoria- road on Saturday. THE TOY PISTOL DANGER. ABo Y SHOT.</p>
        <p>A icl—th named William Hanlon, living at 5, Nlid- land-street, Widnes, was playing with a toy pistol on Friday night, when he was accidently shot. He was removed to the Accident Hospital, where he was attended to by Dr. Donnelly. FATAL RESULT OF AN ACCIDENT AT WIDNES. A child named Herbert Wood, son of John Wood, undertaker, 85, Victoria-road, Widnes, died on Mon- day afternoon from injuries received on Friday last, Deceased accompaniea his fattier to the stables at the rear of the Grosvenor Hotel, and climbed up the ladder. When at tie top he fell to the concrete be- low,receiviug serious injuries to the head. Dr. Estock attended him, but he died, as stated, pro- bably from concussion of the brain. NEW POOR RATES.</p>
        <p>A new poor rate has been made for Runcorn of Is 3d in the .E, to realise £3,491 13s. This is appor- tioned as follows :—Caunty and poor law purposes, 7id in the £ ; school board, 5-g ; cemetery pur- poses, 11 overseers' expenses, lid. The total rate is equal to that levied at the corresponding period last year. On Monday, at the Runcorn Petty S-ssions, a new poor rate was confirmed for Norton of is 2d in the .£, to realize £365 Is 31. RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The annual prize distribution will take place at the Runcorn Technical Institute, on Wednesday next the 17th of October, when the prizes will be handed to the successful scholars by the right Rev. Dr. Jayne, Bishop of Chester. The chair will be taken by Mr. William Handlay, J.P., chairman of the Urban District Council. For further particulars see advertisement. RUNCORN HARRIBII9.</p>
        <p>A good numl-:;W -of the Runcorn Harriers jour- neyed to Woolton on Saturday, to take part in the opening run of the cross-country season, under the auspices of the Liverpool and District Cross Country Association. Fifteen clubs responded to the invita- tion, representing an aggregate of 163 runners. Cho trail was laid over some capital country by Messrs H Boothman, E Bsttersby. and W Allen, the route being through Halewood, Gatacre and Child- wall, returning home by Woolton-road, a distance of about six miles. The usual mile break took place at the finish, the first three home receiving prizes. As was expected, J T Rimmer, the tour-mile English champion was an easy winner, a most promising young runner in R W Sheratt (Liverpool Harriers), being his nearest attendant about 20 yards behind, with W E Richmond, West Cheshire) third. Prizes were also given for the first three novices home. This proved to be a most interesting item, the honours going to P Flynn (Farnworth Harriers), who succeeded in gaining first position, C Jones (Richmond Harriers), second, and W Lewis (Runcorn Harriors), third. After the run a hot-pot was provided at the Coffee House Hotel, and a most enjoyable evening was spent. A word of praise is due to J Hosker, of Farnworth, who has been able to find the winner of the novices for four years in succession. This reflects great credit upon his club. Toe Runcorn Harriers have also commenced the winter season in a business- like fashion, and there is no reason why this club should not develop into one of the best in the dis- trict. It is an encouraging fact that at nearly every run there is a good attendance of members, and if only the enthusiasm can be kept up there is no doubt, with the facilities for cross-country runs at their command, a successful career is in front of the club THE CoLONIAL BAZAAR AT RUNCORN. . . _</p>
        <p>The bazaar in aid of St John's Church Building Fund was continued on Friday. The weather was very unsettled, but in spite of this there was a good number present to witness the opening.—Mr W Cerro!, presided.—Mr J Humphre3s who had journeyed from Nottingham, stated that he was glad to see the industry of the workers in connec- tion with St John's, and he wanted to encourage them further in their efforts. He liked to see the elder members help the younger ones. He was sure a great deal of the credit was due to the younger people. They had been labouring for a good while, and he hoped they would stand firm. He was deeply touched by the remarks which Mr Carrol had made. He would remember old S. John's as long as he lived. He declared the bazaar open, and wished them every success.—Mr Richardson proposed a vote of thanks to the Chair- man and Mr J Humphreys. He remembered some very pleasant afternoons in the class conducted by Mr Humphreys's father. He would never forget how useful he had been to the schools.---Mr Elias Jones, of Liverpool, seconded the proposition,which was supported by Mr James Paitchard and the Rev T S Morris, and carried.—The afternoon was specially set apart for children, and this was evidenced by ? very large number of school children appearing after school. The attrac- tions on the previous day were very successful. They included children's drills, Mr. P Johnson's concert party, Mr J Robinson's party, and a ladies' sawing competition. The successful competitors in the ladies' sawing competition were-1, Mrs John- son; 2, Mrs T Kirkham. Miss Warburton was highly commended. The adjudicator was Mr T Robinson. The takings for the second day. Thursday, amounted making a total for the two days of .2400. —The bazaar was continued on Saturday, and by to .ClO :1, the time it concluded the totai proceeds amounted in round figures to .£5lO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LONGEST BOREHOLE IN ENULAND.</head>
        <p>THE LONGEST BOREHOLE IN ENULAND.</p>
        <p>The Work of a Runcorn Firm,</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gainstiorough Urhan District Council the Waterworks Committee recommended that the final instalment, amounting to £1,475 13i 11, be paid to the contractors, Messrs. TIMMICI9 and Sons (Runcorn), upon the certificate of the engineer, and Mr. Dixon moved a resolution of thanks to the contractors : " That this Council desires to express its appreciation of the patience and perseverance shown by Messrs Timmins and Son, and congratulates them upon the way they , have done the work under almost insurmountable diffi- culties." Continuing, Mr. Dixon said the work was commenced in 1895, the upper portion being lined with 36-inch cast-iren cylinders to the depth of 30 feet 6 inches. A 36-inch boring was then carried down to a depth of 321. feet 5 inches. This is lined with wrought-iron tubes, the whole when screwed together being 286 feet 101 inches long, and weighing about 28 tons, and were lowerel into position without accident, the annular space beine- afterwards filled up with concrete. Water was then poured into the bore hole, when it was found to be abso lutely watertight. A 24-inch boring was then continued to 724 feet, when, on April Ist, 1897, the unfortunate accident happened by which the boring tool, weighing about 45 cwt. together with 500 feet of rope weighing 11 tons were left at the bottom. After trying for several months to recover this, tart without success, it was decided to enlarge the borehole to 29 inches from 300 feet to 724 feet, and after 22 months' exertion the tool was successfully recovered on January 287 h, 1899. The boring was then continued to 775 feet, when the next section of tubing, 475 feet long and 18 inches diameter, was successtully lowered. In consequence of the large annular space at the back of the tubes caused by the enlargement of the borehole (about four inches all round) it was decided to fill up this with a special grouting, 110 tons of Portland ceiner t, sonl, &amp; being used for that purpose. After allewing for this to eet, the bore-hole was again tested and it was found that the lining was perfectly watertight. and the Council are to be congratulated on having a watertight bore-hole down to the sendetune rock, and that the specified lines and sizes to this depth hays not been deviated from. IThe 18-inch boring was then continued to 1,076 feet 6 inches, when, in consequence of continued in-talling, it was deemed advisable in order to ensure the permanency of the work (after corpulting Mr. Griffith) to further line the bore with tubes. The boring was consequently reduced t ) 15± inches, and this diameter was continued to 1,515 feet 1 inch, which was reached on July 2nd last, 9?hen in consequenco of the appear- ance of beds of marl in the reek it was decided not to go any deeper. The further length of 343 feet, of inch tubing was subsequently lowered into position,this portion being perforated where the sandstone occurs. The work has cost £6,645 13s 11. ,and has been five and a half years in execution.—Mr. Dixon then paid a high compliment to Barker, the foreman, and moved that the Clerk be instructed to forward a copy of the re solution to Messrs. Timmins.—Mr Gray seconded,and the recom- mendations were agreed to. The work throughout was superintended by Mr Arthur Timmins, A.M.1.C.E., F.G.S., and carried out by special machinery, designed by him, and which is now at work in the immedurtE vicinity of Runcorn, boring to a depth of 3,000 feet.</p>
        <p>RECEIPT BOOKS, MADE TO ORDER JOHN WALKER &amp; GO,</p>
        <p>Telephone C4E9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUN CORN NOT ES. ---- [By a Correspondent.]</p>
        <p>One would like to keep off it; but then one can- not. Election is the only topic that the public will discuss. You may try S3rile other game on. You may even indicate some really requisite town re- quirement. But it won't do. The whole universe, so far as Runcorn and the Northwich Division is concerned, revolves around the chances of Brunner and Samson. When one gets home to lodgings, Brunner is about in photo or in large block type, and one cannot contemplate the solitary egg and plate of bacon with which he is wont to break his fast,without having served up with it a dash of the omnipotent political Brunner and Samson sauce. The efforts put forward by either side are, un- doubtedly, tremendous, and looking back up)n the past three Weeks one wonders how it is the people have not succumbed to it.</p>
        <p>What has the election been fought upon, and what are the issues which have been laid before the enlightened electorate? The war, and the settle- ment thereon; with an intermingling of the short- comings of that t-Trible radical BrunnorT have been the sum and substance of the Tory programme. On the other hand, Sir J. T. Brunner and his sup- porters have outspokenly deprecated the fact that they:have been called upon to.deal with nothing but refutations of slanderous aspersions upon Sir John's personality; but, having disposed of them, they have honestly tried to lay before the electorate the short-comings of the present Government; the needs of legislation in the direction of furthering the interests of the masses; and the broader issues which have in the past inscribed indelibly upon the Liberal standard peace with honour, retrenchment and reform in domestic politics. As political cam- paigners the Tories have locally, miserably failed ; and, it their tactics elsewhere have been no better than those which pertain to the Northwich Divi- sion, their appeals to the polls should result in nothing less than a defeat—i e., a reduced majority. And talking generally that now seems assured.</p>
        <p>Locally there has been plenty of incident in the electioneering line, and the streets of Runcorn have been considerbbly enlivened by the appear- ance of Messrs. Billam, McHugh and Hoose, wel'- known labour representatives all of whom had been on the "stump," drawn out purely and simply to defend the honour of Sir J T Brunner which the Conservatives were foolish enough to call in ques- tion. The combinations now upon the Liberal side are numerous, potent, and influential, and it is with confidence that Sir John can look to the electorate) to-morrow. They are ,unmistaken and unswerving in their purpose and appear to have fairly tak,u him at his word and will allow him" to devote/he remainder of his days" to serving them in Ve re- presentative house.</p>
        <p>Like the " politics " the literary effusions and cartoons on both sides bays been in very striking contrast. Some, have been witt3 and:laughter pro- voking, but others —and by far the:greatest number of these latter emanate from the Tories—have been frivolous and insulting to the dignity of any elec- torate. Whoever is responsible for the" Diner out," and such like effusions has nothing to boast of, and a candidate, who allows it, only succeeds in getting a party (in which there are some undoubtedly honest straightforward men) a bad name.</p>
        <p>Let me just conclude with the exhortation that all good Liberals do their duty at the polls, and I can confidently leave the election to its fate.</p>
        <p>The distribution of prizes at the Runcorn Techni- cal Schools is on the programme for Wednesday next, and the successful scholars will be honoured by having their certificates handed them by the Bishop of Chester. Another item of interest is Saturday's football match, but of that I speak further down. There is one word, however, that I must add and then I bid adieu for the week, and that is that the poll will not be declared until Monday, and the news should reach Runcorn about the time of the mid-day meal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The supremacy of the Runcorn Football Club, is again en evidence, and to those who witnessed the magnificent way in which they disposed of the premier club of Lancashire there remains little doubt, as to what their position will be at the end of the season. The match with Oldham and Run- corn, is always considered the match of the season, and though the actual play was not of the usual high order characteristic of the annual encounter at Irwell-lane, when the high wind which pre- vailed is taken into account it must be admitted that at times it reached the required standard. Oldham were bested at all points, both in attack and defense the " Linnets "led the way, and the only wonder is that the visitors were not given a much more severe thrashing. The form shown, if adhered to, or improved upon, should 9ee Widnes defeated on their own ground, which by-the-way is next Saturday's engagement. On the other hand, there is so much local feeling infused into the games between Runcorn and Widnes that one can hardly know what to expect. Still it cannot for a moment be doubted that Runcorn are the superior combination, and though the corresponding fixture read a draw of five points each, it is my opinion that Widnes will have all their work cut out to prevent going down" two points."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual nveting of the above Council was hnld at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. Mr J Knowles J.P., presided, and there was also present :—Admiral Jones Parry, Wore J Mclntyre, T Darlington,T Wright, G J Kydd, C E Linaker, E Clarke, T Antwiss, P Gleave, G Filling, C Roylance, T Lewis, J Spruce, J Stokes, P Leather, with MN,srs Gorge F Ashtln (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), W Diggle (surveyor), J Farring- ton (insnector), and Dr J Adams (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>THELWALL Nt7ISAIOE.</head>
        <p>THE THELWALL Nt7ISAIOE.</p>
        <p>Cuercien Hall, The'wall, October 6:h, 1900. Dear Sir,—l am much obliged for yours of September 25th, contents of which are duly noted. It is now high time some definite action was taken. lam having daily re- ports prepared of the Tannery nuisance and as you promised to keep the matter under observation I trust for comparison you will have had detailed reports also prepared to submit to the Local Government Board. We have had one of the worst weeks recoadecl, and on Tues- day last the putrid foam in the cesspool was 4ft. 2in. thick. The exceptionally bad smell caused the mau who took samples of the efillaent to vomit and he was very ill for.some time afterwards. Your medical officer has declared this nuisance to be injurious to health, and he and your inspector exp'ained to me that our coach- man's two children died through the tannery smell find- ing its way along the drains, and through two open water spouts, being the first outlet for it. I beg to thank our local representative and the other members of the Council who have been good enough to treat a rea- sonable complaint with the consideration it deserves,and should all possible means fail to secure justice I can then appeal to the law which fortunately is with me.— Yours faithfully, R. k. Naylor. To George F. Ashton, Esq., Runcorn. 'the Clerk said he had forwarded a copy of the above letter to the Medical Officer and Inspector on account of the grave charge it contained. Dr. Adams referring to the subject said they would remember that on every account he declined ta state that the Tannery smell was i ajurious to health ; and with regard to the statement in Mr. Naylor's letter that he had declared it injurious to health,ho could only meet it with a direct contradiction. He never held the view nor expressed the opinion that the smell was injurious to health in so faar as it regarded the deaths from diph- theria. Both himself and the inspector pointed out that a possible outlet was furnished by the untrapped down- spouts which communicated with a very defective system of sewerage and which had possibly allowed:the ingress of sewage gas co-.eingled with the smell of the ,1 Tannery into the rooms. The Inspector pointed out how ridiculous it woull be for him to assert that the nuisance was, injuriouato health when he could not prove it. He pointed out to the coachman that the place not having its sanitation perfect could not be conducive to the public health. For the places he spoke of were close to the room windows. It was doing the children no good, but as for saying that it killed them, he did not for he didn't know. Mr Linaker questioned whether they were to under- stand from the Medical Officer ef /leo Ith that it was not a nuisance Injurious to health ? He felt certain it was reported as such. Dr Adams explained that the qnestion had been put to him rather peculiarly. He was ask-d "If a man met a bad smell, and it put him off his food, would the fact that he was off his food prove that it was injurious to health ? " To that he had replied that it would to the extent of the man being off his food. Admiral Jones Parry was of opinion that it could be found in black and white somewhere, Hilt the doctor had stated it was injurious to health. The Clerk : It is not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>After a little further discussion it was decided, on the motion of Mr C E Lineiker, seconded by Mr C Roylance, that the Inspector be requested to report on the nuis- ance at the next meeting, and get certain ffluents analysed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board, was held at the Workhouse, Duttan, on Tuesday. There were present :—Mes9rs. J Stokes (presiding), A R Norman, J M-3adowcroft, S Taylor, the Rural District Councilors, with Gorge F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk) aad Joseph Cunnah (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TUE INSPECTION COMIIITTEE.</head>
        <p>TUE INSPECTION COMIIITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A R Norman submitted the repi4zt of the com- mittee appointed to visit and inspect the conditions of the lunatics chargeable to the Union at present confined in the County Asylum. They found everything satis- factory, and it was pleasing to know that Lhey were in good hands. Though there was a slight increase in the lunacy returns it was worthy of notice that one or two of the inmates chargeable to the Union, had been dig charged cured, and there was every indication that tilt ir- minds would keep right and enable them to follow their usual avocations. (Hear, hear.) The cost of the depu- tation was £2 43. sd. Mr. Antwiss endorsed all that Mr. Norman had said. Mr. Pilling reported upoa the inspeztion Lif the Buck- ley Hail and Pantasaph Orphanages. It gas a pleasure to the committee to see the children in each place so well looked after. At Pantasaph the children were all girls, and they all seemed satisfied with their lot, only they expressed a wish they would like to go home and come back again. The expenses were £3 12i 61. The visit to the Buckley Hall Orphanage was equally satis- factory. The expenses of that visit were £2 9i 51. Mr. Meadosvcroft dealing humorously with the cost of the deputations said it was rather unfortunate that the detailed accounts were not before them. He would like to see the items for whisky,shilting cigars and car- riage drives. (Laughter.) The Clerk explained that he had followed the usual practice and paid the accounts out of the petty cash account. Mr. Meadosvcroft could have His particulars if he required. Mr. Pilling . Put him on next time, and it will stop, all this (increased laughter.) Mr. Meadowcroft good humouredly said the produc- tion of the accounts would not matter, for no would not get a single vote. The matter then dropped. OTHER BIISINESS.</p>
        <p>The amounts for out-relief were as fellows •—Runcorn £80; Prodsham. £2O: Great Budworth, £26.—The Ladieat Visiting Committee reported visiting the house and finding everything in the sick ward satisfactory.— The financial report and Ladies' Visiting Co.nmittee re- port were unanimouily agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>TEE POOR L&amp;W CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>TEE POOR L&amp;W CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The report of Messrs. C E Linaker, Thomas Reynolds, i.nd William Turner, (members of the board), and Ur leorge F Ashton, (clerk), who attended toe North , Western Poor Law Conference, held at Southport on the , '2Bth, and 29;h September last as delegates of the Run- corn Union was as follows:—The confers-nce was opened with an address from the president, Sir John T Eiibbert. He gave most interesting an I gratifying statistics of the gradual diminution of pauperism. He stated that whilst pauperism in all classes of England and Wales 48 years ago was 62.7 pr milion, t.-,-.lay it was 25 4 per million. During the same period out door paupers had decreased from 955,000 to 580,000, child pauperism from 24.8 to 73 per million, widows receiving out door relief from 3.2 to I'2 per million, and adult able bodied pauperism had decreased from 132 to 3 per million. And during the past year alone there had been a decrease of 13,625 paupers in the country. Comparing Lancashire and Cheshire with other parts of the country he stated that in Lancashire there a, ere 185 paupers per million of the population in Cheshire 19 8 per million, in London 27.6 and in the West Midlands 27 4 per million. Comparing the Pauperism of last year with the average of the previous 5 years, he stated there was a decrease in Lancashire of 2,617, and in Cneshire of 926 p supers. Referring to the lunacy of the country, he regretted to say there had been an increase during the year from 95,462 to 96,865, aria this followed a continual increase for the last dozen years.— Mr. R. A. Leach, clerk to the Rochdale Union, then read a paper upon" The Codification of the Poor Laws." In the course of his paper Mr. Liach pointed oat that at the present time there are s )133,.thic14 like 350 Acts of Parlia- ment, and about 5,000 decisions of the Courts to make up the poor laws of the country. He strongly advocated that, as a step in the right direction, the Guardians throughout the ceuntry should worry the Local Govern- ment Board into an early issue of a New Consolidated Order, and into the making of a promise that the B )ard will seek to induce Parliament.at the earliest opportunity of a favourable sort, to deal with a measure containing a complete codification of all the laws that Poor Law Guardians as such, have to deal with.—There was a short discussion on this paper, the general feeling tying in favour of it. —ln the afternoon of the 28th September, Mr. E. Burns, Chairman of the West Derby Union, read a paper on "The proper use of Poor Law Infirmaries." He con- tended that Workhouse Infirmaries, and especially those , in the larger 'Unions, were being gradually diverted from their original purpose and that patients who were not destitute were gradually making use of them. At the ' end of this paper he moved the following resolution : " That the Conference re-affirm the principle that Poor , Law Relief, including assistance in times of sickness or accident, must be confined to cases of destitution, apart from considerations of character and desert." A dis- cussion followed and the almost unanimous opinion of the Conference was that Mr. Burns' Resolution was too strong. On the morning of Saturday the 29th Septem- ber, Mr. Horrigan, the Chairman of the Liverpool Select Vestry read a paper on the "Inspection of Poor Law Children," in which he dealt with the relative advan- tages and disadvatages of transferring the Inspection of Children in Poor Law Schools from the Local Govern- ment Board to the Fducation Department. There was a long discussion on this paper, and many diverse opinions offered. The majority however appeared to favour a transfer of the Inspection of the Poor Law Schools to the Education Department. Tne expenses of the Delagates amounted to £7 14s 4d. The report was received very satisfacto</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Newton Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>THURSDAr.—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie (in the chair), Mr. R. _ Stone, and Colonel Wood.</p>
        <p>TRESPASSING IN DEARCII OF GAME AT NEWTON.—SSMtIeI Mains, Thomas Downs. and Joseph Naylor. were summoned' for trespassing on land in the possession of Lord Newton, in search of game, on the 7th inst.—Mr. H. J Longton appeare for the prosecution, and stated that the trespass occurred between four and five o'clock on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Watkins, the hand gamekeeper for Lord Newton. was in Bob- lane and taw the defendants with a lurcher dog. He went away for a short time and on returning he saw the defendants at the end of a cover on the right hand side of the road leading from Newton to Golborne. Watkins entice aled himself in the cwer and saw the lurcher dog hunting in the wood. It also hunted in the adjoining field occupied by a tenant named Heywood. The dog then returned within a few yards of the men and Downs said "Get on Paddy " and the animal went hunting through the cover. Subsequently the men went down the road leading to Newton and Watkins saw the dog enter A potato field and hunt in a pit hole. The defendant Mains gat over the hedge and went into the field. Defendants had a conversation and one of theirs asked " Where's the dog now." The reply was " He's all right, he's bunting amongst the potatoes." After the dog had hunted among the potatoes it crosseci the road and resumed hunting down the fence on Heywood s land. Watkins then went to the men and obtained their names and addresses. It was not essential for him (Mr Longton) to prove that the men actually went on the land, to enable the Bench to record a conviction. He should be able to prove °lnclusively that Mains went on the land but Watkins did not see the other defendants go on it. The conversation which he heard was quite sufficient. The land on both sides of the road was owned by Lord Newton and it had been decided that if they used the road for the purpose of hunting for game. the fact of taiem not being sn the land made no legal difference.-- Mains did not appaar and a warrant was granted for his ap- prehension.—The other defandants, who sail the dog was only hunting for rats, were each fined 5s and eoste. Nelonnauns' QUARREL—Sarah Oritchley was summoned for assaulting Ann Hammett, Chester-road, Vulcan, last Saturday,—Mt. B A White prosecuted and Mr. J SV Knowles defended.—The ease for the p,osecution was that at nine o'clock on the evening in question the complainant was In her own house, the door being shut, She heard somebody rattling at the door and on opening it saw the defendant standing there. The defendant asked her what d-he had been saying about her. Mrs. Hammett replied that she had not been saying anything, and the defendant called her liar and pushed her fingers in her face. Complainant told her to keep her hands off, and the defendant's husband who was standing at the door called out Give her a good warming." Defendent then seized complainant by the hair of the head and pulled her down the steps, and threw her to the ground. Complainant did not retaliate.— Complainant gave evidence but in cross-examination admit- ted that she had previously called the defendant's daughter a "little faggot '—The Chairman : What is the meaning of the word faggot, I should like to have my vocabulary en- larged.—(Laughter.)—Mr. Knowles: I don't think it is found In any standard work. but I think it means something de- praved. fit for anything.—(Laughter.)—For the defence it was stated that Mrs. Hammett was the cause of the affair as she had used filthy language with regard to the daughter of the defendant. Defendant went to see her abont the mat- ter and complainant repeated her assertions. Defendant then became exasperated and committed the assault com- plained of. No blows were struck. —The magistrates decided to dismiss the case.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED SAVAGE ASSAULT AT RIXTON.—James Fury and John Fallon. were charged with ass lulting James Parsonage at Rixton on the 6th inst.—Mr. A. Browne prosecuted, and the defendants were represented by Mr. T. H. Granger.—Mr Browns applied for an adjournment. He said that the defen- dants roaaulted Parsonage by throwing him to the ground and kicking him on the back of the head. The round was two inches long and bare to the bone. Dr. Roberts certified that toe scalp wound was somewhat dangerous, as there was matter under it and extensive auppervation or eryelpelas might set In. He (Mr. Browne) thought it would be some- what dangerous to proreed with the case. and asked for an adjournment to the Warrington County Petty Sessions.—The adjournment was granted. THE MAGISTRATES AND THE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COM- MITTEE.—Abraharn Wood, John Williams. and John Monks, were brought before the bench for neglecting to send their children to school regularly. They stated that owing to ill health their children had been unable to attend school, and when they were summoned to appear before the School Attendance Committee, they were not allowed to speak.— The Chairman said that the committee summoned the parents to answer to a charge and he thought it was illegtl and irregular not to hear what they had to say It was their bJunden duty to hear them.—The cases , were dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
