      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>Distribution of Evening School Prizes.</p>
        <p>The prizes and certificates gained by the pupils attend- ing the evening school were distributed by Mrs. alliance on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by the Rev J Chambers, and he was supported by Messrs W Ballance (H.M. Inspecor), E Imisoa, J Pritchard, J Littler, (.i Moss (clerk to the Board), J F Moulton and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>HEADM&amp;STSR'S REPOUT.</head>
        <p>HEADM&amp;STSR'S REPOUT.</p>
        <p>Mr. J F Moult'en submitted the following, report : The session 1899.1900 was the 10th for the men's and boys' department, and the 8h for the girls'. The school met on 62 evenings in the boys' department. and 44 in the girls'. 'lle number of pupils admitted included —Males, 85 ; females. 31. The average attendance was —men and boys, 52 ; girls, 30, total, 82. The Govern- ment grant earned was as follows :—Boys department, £46 43 ; girls' £24 18s 6d ; total, £B9 23 61 ; being an average of £1 23 2d per head. Unfortunately this amount was not paid by the Goverment, £l6 103 being deducted under Art. 17 a) of the Code, known as the 17s 6d limit. It is a source of regret that whilatithe 17s 6d limit has been eliminated from the day school Code, it still remains in that of the evening school, and efficient and successful continuation rchools do not receive all the grant earned. Out of the pupils attending the evening schools 27 were successful at the Lancashire and Cheshire Unions of Institutes' examina- tions, and have been awarded certificates of merit by that society. For the first time a class for men was formed in connection with the evening school. It is pleasing to report that it was well attended by men anxious for self improvement. The school was visited for inspection several times during the season by Mr , W Bsllance, who reports on the work as follows : Men's and boys' department : "The school continues to do remarkably good work. The newly formed men's classes are a most pleasing and encouraging feature." Girls' school : " Notwithstanding the change in the head-teachership during the school year, the usual good standard of work has been well maintained."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ballance Shen graciously distributed the prizes, a list of which appear below. Mr. Irnison, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Ballance, said as the Board of the town they were greatly indebted to Mr. Ballance for the interest he had invariably evinced in continuation schools—(applause)— in fact he should be quite safe in saying that it was owing to his stimulating encouragement that they had an evening school at all. When they considered that they had nearly 3,000 children in the day schools and in the evening schools and, so far as he gathered from Mr Moulton's report, they had only about 82 students, they could not regard that as satisfactory. The ideal state of continuation schools in the town was that there should be a continuation class in every school attended by young children. Unfortunately they could not in- spire the youth of the town with the love of education which seemed to exist elsewhere, but they were hoping in the future ta make progreas in this direction. (Applause.) Mr. Pritchard seconded. There was no greater friend to that evening school than Mr. Ballance. (Applause ) It was he that encouraged tbe Board and the teachers to commence the evening school, and he had taken a deep and c:aasiderate interest in it. Hundreds who bad passed through that school during the past ten years were greatly indebted to Mr. Ballance for help and encourage- ment. ( Applause.) The resolution was unanimously carried. Mr. Ballance responded. When he commenced that sect of work he made up his mind to be just and tear nought, and whatever shortcoming he might have had he had kept that resolution up to the present. Educa- tion assisted in the formation of character. These schools were for the object of teaching students to love knowledge for its own sake, that they might be made intelligent, accurate, and observing individuals. The most trying time in a boy's life was when he was neither a man nor a boy, and then it was that the character was developed. In spite of the vicissitudes of changing codes and other things, the school had remained stead- fast, and was the only oce that was doing really goad work. After referring to the men's class, which he said had given great pleasure, he told them candidly that ex- cepting the matter of numbers that school was second to none. (Apolause.) This was due to the masters and mistresses who had made the place as attractive as pos- sible, and at the same time useful. He should like the boys to have a gymnasium and the girls to receive prac- tical instruction in cookery. He hop-d the present year would be as successful as last year. (Loud applause.) Mr. John Littler proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman and the artistes, and remarked that it was lamentable that of the children who attended the day school so few continued their educatien at the evening school.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moss in sennimsr, said the evrming scholl was not a rival to the Technie,al Schools. The one was supple- mentary to the other. Mr. Moulton suppo:tei, and expressed his indebted- ness to Mrs. Baliacce.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with acclamation. During the evening songs were rendered by the Misses Wright, Wood, and Carter, and Mr. Branch. Mr. Vernon accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>BOYS' CLASS —First class —Austin Potts, Albert Fox, W H Broughton, Henry Suett, Alfred Carter, Henry Grubb, Thomas Peers, Thomas Clare, Henry Edwards, Charles Stewart, Joseph Hindley. Second class : Charles Faulkner, Joseph Nixon,Walter Shingler, Joseph Boukem, David Pilgrim. Robert Etkcood, Nathaniel Jones, Malcolm Jones, Arthur Barnes, Harry Boukem, Charles Taylor, Douglas Battersby.Wllliam Butterworth. Third class :—Fred Harrison, Alfred Clark, Thomas Smith, Herbert Carter.</p>
        <p>MEN'S CLass —James Lightfoot, John Parry, Harry Bell, Ralph Wainwright, George Smith, Arthur McNorton, Samuel WalkE.r, Arthur Hayes, JabEz Scrutton, James Hembleton.</p>
        <p>The following pupils won certificates, awarded by the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Institutes : Thomas Austin Potts, Charles G Faulkner, Henry. Waterworth, Henry J Suett, Thomas A Smith, Thomas Peers, Joseph W Nickson, Harry Grubb, Albert Fox, Herbert Carter, Thomas Clare, Robert Ellwood, Alfred Carter, William H Broughton. Hygiene : Beat- rice Britland, May Woodier, Dora Boyers, Majorie Faulkner, Gertie Clucas. Science : Mary Moores, Lily Carney, Margaret Gillighan, Mary Widders. Attend- ance and general diligenc,z : Lucy Bradshaw, Bessie Bratt, Annie Andrews. Ada Burrows, Marion Holt, Hannah Wood, Ethel Lightfoot, Edith Smith, Ada Bowman, Deanor Bell, Hilda Williams, Florence Banks Mabel Williams. Certificates.—Arithmetic : Florence, Banks, Beatrice Britland, Ada Bowman, Lily Cam.y, Mary Moors. Annie Claim), Edith Smith,ililda Williams Mabel Williams. Handwriting : Beatric3 Britland, Mary Miores, and Hilda Williams.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. A Warrington Youth's Sudden Death. Mr F A Jones, the Deputy Coroaer, conducted an inquest at the Warrington Police Station on Monday afternoon, on the body of Richard Thomas Burnett, aged 17 years, of 19, Beswick-street, who died very suddenly on the previous Friday—Sarah Burnett, mother of the deceased said that her son went to work at six o'clock on Thursday evening. He came home at eleven o'clock and complained of pains in his head and stomach. tie went to bed, and the following morning partook of a very light breakfast. At noon he had a fit and died—Dr. Naden said that he was called to see the deceasel, but when he arrived he was dead. On Saturday morning witness made a post mortem examination. The body was well nourisned and there were no external marks of violence. Both the lungs were congested as were also the membranes of the brain. Death was due to congestion ot the brain and con- vulsions.—The jury brought a verdict of " Death from natural causes."</p>
        <p>The Bishop at the Prison Gate. About seven o'clock on Saturday morning the Lord Bishop of Liverpool paid a visit to the Mission Room of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission I of the Liverpool Diocesan C.E.T.S. in Hornby-roadl near Walton Gaol. His Lordship was accompanied by his chaplain the Rev H l 11 Probvn. There were also present at the Mission Room the Rev J Hirst, secretary of the Society ; Mrs Hirst, Mr J M Sadler, and three of the missionaries, Messrs H Goldstone, George Whitehall and H Cann. A number of dis- charged f,!male prisoners were invited into the room, and they were provided with hot coffee and bread and butter. The Bishop spoke to them kindly and earnestly and begged them to lead a better life. Two of the missionaries followed by a few warning' words and prayer. After the women had left the room the men entered,and they were also spoken to by his Lordship, and the Rev Ii E H Probyn offered a few words oi prayer. Mrs Hirst, who presided at the harmonium sung two hymns. Several men accepted tickets for work at the Society's Firewood and Mat Fdctory at Bootle, and other men and women signed the pledge. This branch of the Society's work is in operation every week- lay morning and has now been established twenty-one years. Last year 6,969 persons were assisted by breakfast, advice, employment, &amp;c:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendanc3 at the service in connec• tion with the above-named society on Sunday. Mr. Farrar presided and delivered a brief address, basing his remarks on Deuterotocmy Bth chap.. verse 2. Mr. G. Power of Manchester was the speaker. " Heroism " was the title of his address, which was listened to with markel attention, and much appreciated. Miss B Boughier, contributed a violin 8010 " The Star of Bethle- hem " which was well received. Mr C Eccles read the Scripture lcsson. The membership is still increasing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Police Court, before Mr Gillespie, Pat Larkin was charged with being drunk at Earles- town on Saturday night, and was fined 23 6d and costs.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRWTy.S.A.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above a Musical Service was given in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown on Sunday afternoon. There was a fairly large con- gregation, and the service was under the conductor- ship of Mr A Knight organist and choirmaster of the Congregational Church, Newton. Mr T William- son presided and the principal soloists were Misses Corlett, and Mather of Earlestown, Messrs J Ather- ton and Knowles of Wigan, and reciter, Miss Roberts. The collection amounted to Li 14s 6d. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL. 1</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a conversaz onne was held in the Mission Hall in connection with the above, there being about 60 persons present. There was dancing for the young people and games for those who did not care for the giddy whirl, whilst at intervals vocal items were given by the Rev John Matthews, "The lads in navy blue," and .Nlr John Isherwooi, who also gave a capital imitation of a cornet solo as reproduced by the phonograph. For the dancing Messrs Spencer and Hardman officiated as The proceeds were in aid of the debt on the prize fund. NEWTON-LE- WILLOWS MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The inauguration of the ;winter session in con- nection with the above consisted of a Literary entertainment in the Congregational Schoolroom, Newton on Monday evening. The Rev James Allatt, presided.and there was a very fair audience. The chairman said he was very pleased to be pre- sent at the opening of the third session of the society. The meetings were not only interesting but instructive, The entertainment consisted of a couple of songs by Mr John Sephton, and Miss J Howard, and recitals by Mr Fred Morgan, elocu- tionist. The following items were submitted "An Awkward Fix "(Mark Twain), "The Lucky Six- pence.' Mr Squeer's Academy " (Dickens),selection from "Julius Uzesar," recital "The Cuckoo Clock." The recitals were most enjo able, and given in Mr Morgan's inimitable style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] Much disaster was predicted for the Town's Water Scheme, by those who could not be counted as its friends. To their discomfiture, however, it has so far turned out a complete succcess. Unlike the supply obtained from the Railway Co., it is not intermittent, and the pressure is all that can be de- sired. Of course the supply at present is only from the bore hole, and when the wells have been sunk and made it is anticipated that the supply will be even better than it is at presen'. In the higher reaches of the town, chiefly at Newton Common, the pressure is not what might be termed excellent, but even in these parts there will be improvement when the tank which it is proposed to sat up has become an actual fact.</p>
        <p>With the opening up of new roads and streets, it will be necessary for the Council to push on, and secure a supply of water about which there will be no fear. It is pleasant to think that at last the Council have corns to a decision to open up the new road leading from Warg rave-road, near the bridge, into Crow-lane. This will not only be a conveni- ence to very many people, but will open out a most desirable building sice,and if the Lord of the Manor's agent will only have the good sense to keep the jerry builder off the spot, with his "rabbit warren" houses, the road could be made into one of the most respectable places in the district. I know it is the desire of the Council to have only fir.st class houses on the front land,but if they are not backed up by Mr Leslie it will very soon decline into au ordinary working class street. No doubt many cot- tages are needed, but we want a few villas also of a most modern type, and front land it at present most difficult to obtain.</p>
        <p>So Newton is to have a public clock. They could not have the Town Hall built there but they mean to have some of the public money expended in their village, und so they have asked for a clock and the Council have agreed to comply with their request. A tower is ia course or erection in con- nection with St. Peter's Cdurch, and the wardens have asked the Council to put a clock in it, and to light i by gas on dark nights, at the expense of the ratepayers. The request is a cool one, but seeing that it has been acceded to, it is to be hoped that the Council will so secure themsslves that there will b 3 no fear of the clock becoming the property of the church. It will, no doubt, be ot considerable convenience to the people of Newton, as the old clock which was in the church, was most erratic in its movements and at no time could be depended upon.</p>
        <p>Although the Newton Division was spared the turmoil of a general election, Colonel Pilkington was likewise spared the unpleasant necessity of having to reply to some very awkward questions, which woulctdoubtless have been levelled at him had the division been conteste 1. At the final meet- ing held last week, the stroking down prozess be- came positively mcnotonous, and the thanks which were poured forth were really profuse, and, one might add, uncalled for. There had been no work to do, in a fighting sense, yet Colonel Pilkington spoke as if he had been helped through a severely fought contest.</p>
        <p>At the best of times Colonel Pilkington, is no platform orator and his style is such as to give his hearers an attack of nerves. On the Thursday even- ing he was on his feet above an hour and fairly revelled in his pet subject of the army. Judging from his remarks one would be tempted to think that the War Office is still an incompiete establish- ment, and that the worthy Colonel was graduating for the vacancy. His platitudes were not inspirit- ing, and his statement that he wou:d force all young men over the age of 21 years to become Volunteers, appeared to fall flat with his hearers. If young men were forced to join, that branch of the military service would at once cease to be known as the "Volunteers." Colonel Pilkington should have said at once that he was in favour of conscription, and not voluntary military service. His views on this are something on a par with his views on the half-timers qu. stion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The foptball in this district on Saturlay was not of an interesting character. The only match was between Earlestown and Ifaxdock in the prelimin- ary round of the English Cup Competition. No doubt the previous display bawesn these clots in a friendly enciunter at llaydock, was responsible for the small muster of spectators, the result of the tuatch being considered' a foregone conclusion. The Haydock team, for want of support, is not what it was last year and consequently Earlestown had an easy task on band. Whitehouse failed to turn up, and Hayes, the full back, went centre forward, No goals were scored in the first half of the gam-., but in less than three minutes after turning round, Earlestown put on a couple of coals. This took the heart out of llaydock, and when Earlestown in- creased their lead byanother goal theywere a beaten team</p>
        <p>To-morrow, Earleetown have St. Helens Recs. as visitors in a friendly match, and as it is two years since the teams met, it will be interesting to see the relative capabilities of the teams.</p>
        <p>Newton, who this year joined "The Cambina- tion," are finding it rather more expensive than they expected, 1 fancy. They have so far had to undertake some long journeys, without any com- pensating gates at home. On Saturday they have to travel to Bangor to meet the club belonging to the Cathedral town, and a good game should result.</p>
        <p>The Earlestown Rovers will have their work cut- out on Saturday in their match with Prescot Juniors on the lattera' ground.</p>
        <p>Lord Gerard's Homecoming.</p>
        <p>On Monday the ratepayers of Ashton near Wigan met to consider the best way to celebrate the home- coming of Lord Gerard, who has a Lancashire resi- dence in the township of Ashton. During the war his lordship has been an active campaigner with Sir Red vers Buller. It was decided to present his lordship with an illuminated address, welcoming his return, and commending his services in the war. There will be a firework display, and the Lancashire Yeomanry, of which Lord Gerard is the colonel commanding, will take a prominent part in the proceedings, which will take place upon his lord- Ehip's arrival from London. He has sent his horse, wounded at the battle of Colenso, to Garswood stables,</p>
        <p>ISudden Death of a Local Magistrate. The people of Warrington will hear with regret of the somewhat sudden death of Mr. Claude John Morris, who held the position for many years of managing director for Messrs.RylanAs Bros.,Limited, wire manufacturers, &amp;c., Church-street, Warring- ton. The deceased, who was a bachelor, was about 58 years of age. Ile caught a chill while travelling from Warrington to Buxton last Wednesday,whicli resulted in his death on Monday night at his resi- dence, Rutland Lodge,Buxton. The deceased gentle- man was one of the Mayor's guests at the dinner held at the Town Hall on Monday week to celebrate Warrington having become a county borough. Ha was a magistrate for the county of Lancashire, and was frequently in attendance at the Warrington County Petty Sessions. His last appearance on the Bench was on Wednesday, September 19th. For many years the deceased lived at the Mount,Altrinc- ham, where, as in Warrington, he was well-known aud much respected. He .was a leading spirit in the deliberations of the Altrincham Liberal party. For many years he acted as Chairman of the Altrincham Liberal Council. The deceased gentle- man was a very fair platform speaker, but it was as an organizer and worker that he was chiefly dis- tinguished. He was a nephew of the late Mr Peter Rylands, who was at one time M.P. for War- rington and subsequently for Burnley.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S Cocoi has a world-wide reputation as a deli— cious, strengthening beverage, aLd a valuable nutritive food The "Lancet" says it represents " the standard of highest purity." Always insist on having CADBURY'S—soId only in Packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of extra prof3t.—Advt. (2)</p>
      </div>
