      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>DISTURBANCR AT THE PARR HALL-CARETAKER ASSAULTED.</p>
        <p>On Friday, at the Warring,ton Police Court. before the Mayor and other magistrates, Herbert Heuehton, Stam- ford-atreet, was summoned for assaulting George Albert Powis on the 29th September—He pleaded guilty—Mr. T. S. Steele, who appeared on behalf of the complainant said that Mr.Powis was the hall-keeper of Parr Hall and also the caretaker of the Town Hall in the employ of the Corporation. On Saturday the 29th ult., Kiddie's Pan- tomime Company was performing in the hall and Powi4 was in charge of the bailding. At 10.30 he was in- formed that three young men were creating a distur- bance at the entrance, and endeavouring to force their way into the paybos, so frightening the ladies in charge that they had rushed out in fear. 01 going to see what was the matter he saw the defendant and two other young men, standing oppasite the entrance. They sv.re making use of filthy and dis4usting !alien-tee to the ladies. Powis told them to go away quietly and two of them did so, but the defendant refused. He attempted to get back into the hall and told Fowls to "put his props up "placing himself in a fighting attitude. Powis called for assistance and one of the defendant's com- panions came and tried to get him away.. Houghton, bowever, struck him in the face.. He then struck Powie a Now iu the right eve, with his closed fist. He after- wards ran awpy and Pwris pursued him along Palmyra- -square, Bold-street. Egypt-street. Cairo street, and Bankay-street, where he lost him in the crowd. He had suffered considerable pain from the effects of the blow and his eye was blacked. The assault was witnessed by Mr Walter Longdin, who was passing the hall on his bicycle. When seen by a police officer the defendant admitted the assault. •He (Mr. Steel) asked the Bench to impose a substantial penalty fa order that it might go forth to the public that they were not going to allow the hall keeper to be interfered with and assaulted in his endeavours to preserve order in the hall.—The Bench d,cided to impose a fine of 104 and costs and allowed an advocate's fee (El 14,18 d in al.). A BRUTAL EIUSBAND.—SAVAGE ATTACK ON A WIF3 AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Elizsb,th Beckett, whose face was dreadfully dis- figured, appeared at the Warrington PAice Court. on Wednesday morning to prefer a charge of assault against her husband. Samuel Beckett, who resides in Turner-street. —Complainant stated that on Saturday. September 29th,she was discharged from the Infirmary, where she had been treated for burns to the arm. She met the prisoner at the corner of Scotland-road, and he at once informed hr that the home was b okPn up, that their children were in the Workhsuae and that be was lodging with Mrs Richardson, 14, Turner-street Be asked her to go with him to hi .3 tod:fiegs, and she</p>
        <p>did so. When she had heen there a weriCohe of her -children died in the Workhouse and the b)dy was brought to the house in Turner-street. Shortly after th* arrival of the body prisoner gave her 14s with which she paid Mrs Richardson 8s 7i and bought some food. Her husband went out and when he returned he asked her what she bad done with the money, and without</p>
        <p>any pvvocation struck her several tim9s in the face with his fist, giviry, her two black eyes. She then refused to give him the money she had, whereupon he thumped her agaln and made use o f u 1 larieun t".He went away for a short time, and cam,. back about eight o'clock, when. after ill-using her he went out again. He returned about 10.30, when he dragized her by the skirt off a chair into the back yard, then upstairs, and after again thrashing her put her on the bed, telling her to remain there. She was bleeding, profusely from the</p>
        <p>•rnouth and nose. Ile renewed this conduct about three -o'clock on the following morning. Soo, got up and he sat on the bed with a knife in his hand and threatened "kick her to death." S'lP was oblized to run down- stairs:for safety, and Mns Richarrisons's son prevented him from again striking her.—The Chief Con- stable remarked that the woman's face was no great bruise. P.C. Corb,tt stated that at 330 on Sur- day afternoon he saw the complainant in a bed at Mrs. Richardson's house, and the prisoner wa; standing by her &amp;de. She was blee:ling in various parts of her body, and her facP was covered with blnod. In reply to a question she said th-t prisoner had caused the injurirs ein the previous evening with his knuckles. Prisoner . _ _ . . _</p>
        <p>_ said that was quite correct, and added that his wife had , been drunk, and had spent the money he had given her. Complainant said he had only had a glass of hoer. He took her to her home in Eldon-qtr.:pt. The Chief Con stable remarked that there were thirty-five prnvinua con- victions against the prisoner. Mr. Taylor, Police Court Missionary, pointed out that the prisoner had left his children and he (Mr. Taylor) and Inspector Hackett had to feed them. Prisoner : I have nothing to do with that. I was thriwn out of the house. Prisoner was sent to goal for F.ix mnnths with hard labour, and his wife was granted a separation order,with 7361 per week in ainteziaflCe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair). Councillors S me</p>
        <p>bnrst and Roberts. and Mr J Hepber.i.</p>
        <p>, WIFE nEFIERTION.—ThorMS Warburton was summoned for deserting his wife Mary Hilen Warburton.—Complainant said that her husband WRS sent to prison nine years ago, and since that time he would have nothing to do with her. She had had to work at the factory to keep herself.—The magis- trates granted a separation order with 10s a weAr mainten- ance.</p>
        <p>SATITRDAY.—BArore Mr. J W Hayward (in the chair), Coun- cillor John Roherts. Mr. U T Fairclough and Mr. Henry</p>
        <p>Thornton</p>
        <p>Wom.ot CHARGED WITH THEFT —rathPrina KDOCktOD , was charged with stealinz from the Vaults at the Norton Arms Hotel. on Wednesday afternoon, a jaeket, containing four keys and a pocket book, value:ls. the property of George Glassey—The Chief Constable said that Glaseey was s cabinet maker, and carried on business at 21, Old-road. On Wednesday afternoon he was engaged in fitting a spring on one of the doors at the Norton Arms Hotel, and he had placed his jAcket on a form ia the vaults. He had occasion to go to his shop, which was opposite the hotel. for some screws and on returning five minutes later. missed the jacket. The documents in the pocket were of considerable value to him as they related to business transactions. In con•equence of something that came to his knowledge he went to the prisoner's house and accused her of the theft. She denied taking the jacket but a girl who was in the house said that the prisoner had stolen it, and had burnt some of the documents. When arrested the prisoner said she took the jacket home but did not intend to do anything with it Up to the present time she had borne a fairly good character --A fine of 10s. was imposed.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). Alderman Hutchinson and Heaton, Dr. Gornall and Messrs. J. W. Hayward, S. Wright and Henry Thornton. STFAUNG A BAC/oa CoAT...—Albert Edward Jeffs. Whit- held-road. Stockton Heath, was charged on a warrant with stealing a bag of coal value is, [the property of the Warring-</p>
        <p>There Is Security ln A few dreoje:yolottinteog thbraah CARTER'S ITTLE ila§‘, .......-"iit ____ . ~,,r I . ... 4, IVER Small Pill. Or PILLS :mall rDroiscee.SOZODONT Will Sweeten az Breath all day, and make all the difference be- Absolutely cure Sick Headache, tween— biliousness, Torpid Liver, Ind, Good Teeth and Bad Teeth. iyestion. Constipation. Dizziness, White Teeth and Yellow Teeth. Furred Tongue. They Touch the Pretty Teeth and Ugly Teeth 1-iver. ast be son tlor asi CARTER•B. complete in i,Toilet Case, with</p>
        <p>ton Slate Company, on October. 2nd.—Mr. F. R. Roberts I prosecuted on behalf of the company, and stated that the prisoner had been in the employ of the company for eight monthe as a carter. Last Tuesday he was engaged to take thirty bee's of coal to two ladies in the town. Haying delivered one set of bags he proceeded to the fte.ond lady's house and delivered the second lot. The y observed that when he had delivered what was presum- ably her quantity, he had one or two bags left. She asked the man if he had anywhere else to go, and he paid that he had. Then he altered his remark and wild he had not to go anywhere e!se, and the bags did not contain coal. He ulti- mately admitted that there was coal in one bag, which he seld, he himself had bought from the company, and would pay for in the usual courie. ' The lady thought the circum- stances suspiciou,, and informed the Slate Company. On Saturday morning. Mr. Henry Roeerts, the managing director,' taxed the prisoner with having stolen the coal. First of all he denied having the bag at all but subsequently said he told the man at the yard that be was taking it and would pay for it. The clerk at the coal yet-4 was telephoned to and at once said that nothing what- ever had been aaid by jeffs, about the coal. Until that morning the prisoner had maintained that he intended pay- ing for the coal, but now he admitted stealing it. The company did not wish to be vindictive. They were sorry for the man and if it had not been for ether complaints they had received they would have been content to discharge him. They did net wish him to be sent to prison and would be satisfied if the Bench recorded a conviction and ordered hint to pay the costs.—The Chief Constable said the man had been in austoly since Saturday and felt his position keenly. Up to the present time he had borne a good charac- ter --The magistrates recorded a conviction and ordered Jeffs to pay 51 costs. A VIOLENT CHAR ACTA:R.—Fred Price, a farm labourer, of Stratton, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Rose and Crown-street, at 6-45 on Saturday evening and with wilfully breaking a window at the Rose and Crown Inn, doing damage to the amount of M.—George Lenz, the licencee of the Rose and Crown Inn said that on Saturday evening prisoner came in and called for a drink As be wee , in a drunken condition witness refused te serve him and he went outand smashed one of the glass panels of the door either with his elbow or fist —Prisoner was fined 5s and costs and ordered to pay the amount of the damage. A. DESERTER. —John Griffiths was charged with being a deserter from the 6th Bettalion of the Eth Kings Liverpool Regiment, stationed at Orford Barracks since the 6th inst.— P.C. ! ewis proved the case, and the prisoner was remanded to awe% an eseort.—The officer was recommended for the usual reward. AN OLD 'Bus Damn IN TROUBLE—Thomas Daly, 'bus driver, of 6. Cloth Hall-yard, was summoned for-having been drunk in charge of two horses and a 'bus in Manchester-road. on the night of the 29th September—P.C.' Bennett proved the nase.—The Chief Constable said that the defendant was a very respectable man. There was not a more capable, civil and obliging omnibus dr:ver in Warrington. On the Satur- day evening mention.d he received eompleints from various persons to the effect that_the defendant was very drunk and was driving all over the road. He had to bring the case to court in the interests of the publics. Unfortunately there was a public -house at either end of the route, and the temptation was great. The men had to work very long hours, and he felt sorry for some of these drivers.—ln answer to the Chief Con- stable, defendant said that he had had his licence since the first commencement of the 'bus service, six years ago —The Chairmen said defendant would be fined ss. In his opinion these 'bus drivers were more sinned against than sinning. _</p>
        <p>" lONIA' SEVEN CLIMES op BEER."—john Morren. who lives in Jame.-street. was charged with having been drunk and disorderly at the Hop Pole Inn, on Saturday night, and with refusing to leave when requested to do so by the land- lord —The latter proved the case, and said the prisoner made use of obscene lenguage in the " music " room, and that was the reason he asked him to lewe. He did not consider that he was drunk —Prisoner refused to go out and had to be ejected by force.—P Hew was of the opinion that the man was drunk.—Prisoner: I only had seven glasses of beer all day. and that would not make me drunk. We were arguing about the election.—Fined 10s and costs. DISORDERLY CONDUCT IN DUDLET-STREET.—John Brown, ue, Dudlev-street. Arthur F mess 183, Lonvford-itreet, tames Burns, 54, Dudley-street. Alfred Ward, 16. Sharp-st., Harry Hughes, 152, London-street. Harry Foulkes. 179. Longford-street, Frank Foster, 23, Dudley-street, and Horace Beddard, 40, Winwick-street.boys whose ages ranged from 10. to 13 years, were summoned for disorderly conduct in Gulley-street. —P.C. Clough said he saw the defendants shouting, pushing one another about, and kicking a tin can about the street Their emduct was scandalous —Mr. A. Browne, on behalf of the defendants, denied the offence. —A fine of is each was Imposed. ToseDAY—Before Aldermen Shaw Green (in the chair), Hut</p>
