      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETI FION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETI FION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Lose Again.</p>
        <p>Swinton repeated las', year's success at Wilderspool by defeating Warrington on Saturday. The Blues nave won all their m Itches this season and are goiog so strongly for the championship that Warrington were hardly expected to ch.i.:ll their victorious career A very good game was witnessed however, Warrington only losing by the marzin of a try after hiving a similar point disallowed. The boisterous wind which prevailed militated against the best football, other wise the condi- tions were most satisfactory and there would be a gate of quite 6,000 people. Mr. Kennedy, of Broughton, was the referee and the players lined up in the following order :</p>
        <p>Warrington : Haflim, ftitl hick ; wish, Ishe,rwood, Lawless and Hockenhall, tbrerquarters ; Bite and Bur- gess, half backs ; Viyhr, Niereweather, Boarthman, Scholtze, Eden, Swift, Morrison. and Andrews, for- wards. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Swinton : Chorley, full baek • Lewis, Messer, R , Valentine, and V Elarnpson, thresquarters ;Dai Davies and Morgan, half backs ; J Valentine, Vigors, PalLitt, Janes, Tickle, Harris, Berry, and Evans, forwards. Warring' on won the toss, and .1 Valentine set the ball in motion against a strong breeze, Burgess returning to the Swinton half. The visitors were the tir:t to get possession, Davies, Morga a and Hampion passing neatly, the latter being thrown in touch at centre by Bate. A punt by Fish carri-d play to the line when Warrington tried hard to bi.oak through. Slow move- ments behind the scrimmage lost them the op- portunity however, Lewis racing to c sntre fr )m Davies' pass. Warrington were quickly back again, but more smart passine by Swinton gave Lewis another opportu- nity to relieva, which he did fiiieT, r aching centre be - fore Isherwuod stopped him. Warring:on did not u-e the wind to the best advantage, several dead balls being registered. but generally they were near the Swinton line. It Valentine did a lot of excellent defensive work, Eden once grassing him almost under the cross-bar- Lawless knocked on badly from Bate's pass, and then the two half backs made the running for Isherwood, who was likewise at fault. A punt by Taylor was splendidly followed up by the home forwards, and Morrison had the hardest of luck in being stopped just short of the line. B ate got hold and attempted to dodge over but he also was stopped on the line. Hesitation by Burgess lost another chance, and Davies removed play to the quarter filg by a strong run. Fine passing by the whole of the visitors three-qaarters caused an inva- sion of the home half for the first time, Hallam promptly responding with a huge kick, Fiih racing up and grass- ing Chorley. Warrington attacked again, Isherwood trying a drop-kick which failed. Morrison was hurt and left the field, Warrington immediately redoubling their exertions. Fish secured and made a brilliant run to the line, apparently grounding over the rut, but the try was not allowed. He also had two shots at goal from free kicks, but again ill-luck was his portion, and half- time arrived with no score. The second half was commenced with the teams on even terms, for Morrison returned. Swinton by the aid of the wind attacked, and Lewis was early speeding down touch, Burgess tackling the sprinter capitally. Isherwood intercepted a pass, and took play back to the Swint m half, Chorley being tested. Warrington fell back again, Hallam being cheered for a fine piece of defensive play. Lewis was hurt in a tackle, but a few minutes later he received from Messer, and dashed over at the corner. J. Valentine failed at goal. Warringron rallied and forced the Blues hack Fish placed the ball beautifully under the bar.where Morrison again tried hard to break through. R Valentine broke away cleverly and passed forward to Hampson when a score see:ned cer- tain. Hallam was again called upon and responded brilliantly, by running and kicking into the Swinton half. Warrington played with great determination, and the Swinton position seemed by no means secure. Lewis , was lame and Fish gave him a lot of trouble. If Isher- wood had only passed properly on one occasion Fish would probably have made the score even. Burgess broke away but was not supported, and another chance went begging. Towards the close Swinton made another effort, Me.sser runr4ing clear and passing inside to Ela.mpson who Was pulled down on the line. The I whistle soon sounded:for the close of play, Swinton win- ning by a try to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Runcorn Defeat Oldham Again.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Defeat Oldham Again.</p>
        <p>Time was when a meeting of Runcorn and Oldham at the little Cheshire town would have caused a couple of train loads of people to jlurney from the hilly town. On Saturday the railway company ran a special train, and a veracious chronicler informed me that it did not cal ry 30 passengers, and his explanation wle that the Oldham supporters were feeling very badly the after-effects of " election fever." Unfor- tunately for the high.class play usually assr- ciated with the fixture, half a gale swept across the ground, and if there was no lack of lively and exciting incidents, there was far too much marking and kicking, and as the wind carried the ball into touch on a thousand and one occasions, a good idea of the game may be formed, Runcorn were without Butterworth and Jolley. Oldham had the advantage of the elements in the first half ; and halves and three-qoarters nearly always put their foot to the ball, with the result that Houghton, Wilkinson, and Riehardson did plenty of kicking in reply, and, when it served, touchdowns were yielded with good grace. At length Ferguson kicked a penalty goal, and the same player had hard lines with another, while on the other side Richardson and Walkr made gallant efforts for tries, failing by about a yard of greensward to cross the line. _ _</p>
        <p>Ends were changed with Oldham leading by two points, and Runcorn at once took up the aggressive, but</p>
      </div>
