        <head>chlnson and Heaton and Dr. Fox.</head>
        <p>chlnson and Heaton and Dr. Fox.</p>
        <p>A NEGLECTFUL RUSE &amp;ND GETS TTIREE Mori'H%—lohn Garnett, a bricksetter, was charged on a warrant with non- payment of arrears of maintenance due to his wife amount- ing to £25 10s od.—Complainant stated that her husband had not paid anything since the order was made twelve months ago.—The Chief Constable informed the Bench that the prisoner would not work. His wife was a very hard work- ing woman.— Prisoner said he could not possibly ray the money.--Fie was committed to gaol for three mouths with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before ..Messrs. Henry Reynolds (chairman). J.</p>
        <p>Ockler on. and NOrmln</p>
        <p>LICRINSING.—Temporary authority was granted to the following persons : —From Thomas Harrison. to Henry Le Coutenr. Sloop Inn. Look-street; from the late Charles Houghton to lilizabeth Houzuton, Mersey V tults. Mersey- street, and from Alercand -r Cirruthers. to James Woodier. Oddfellows Arms.</p>
        <p>DRUNKERWESI.—John Burke, of Runeorn. was fined Is and 8s 6d costs, or seven days, for being drunk and disorderly. CRUELTY TO A WIFE AT WESTON.--James Routledge, of Companies Row. Weston. pleaded guilty to persistent cruelty towards his wife, and the Bench granted her a separation order, the hugs:wand to pay El Is Od eosts, and contribute 8s a week,—From the circumstances of the case it appeared that the parties had been married about 8 years. The wife had some money of her own, but the husband was only a casual labourer at the local chemical works, and earned about £1 a week. Per a few years presumably whilst the wife's money lasted, the 'couple got on happily. Hach succeeding year, however, saw the husband bee sme more harsh and cruel ; and on several occasions, he best and kicked his wife with his clogs on. The woman ayes consequently weak, prematurely, age land decrepit, and conld only be assisted into court The case was. indeed a pitlable oee, and bat another of the many '• black pages "that stand to the record of drink, for it was proved that the husband was almost an habitual tippler Baum (Um —John Ralican. and W Wynne, both of Weston were fined 61 and 4s 61 costs for neglecting the education of their children —The ease: were proved by Yfr W B Brimelow School Attendance Officer to the Russcorn Board of Ouardianc—The Chairman called attention to the new law, whereby people could be fined 201 for neglecting their children's education ; and warned the parents that if need be they were prepared to put it into operation.</p>
      </div>
