      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ME EXAMINER, FRIDAY OCTOBER 12, 1900.</head>
        <p>ME EXAMINER, FRIDAY OCTOBER 12, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>the Oldham forwards proved very strong in the scrum- maging department, and screwed three or four scrum- mages in good style. Because, however, they failed to get away on these occasions, they abandoned these highly appropriate tactics, with the result that Run- corn pennod them in their own half for the greater part of the second half. The Runcorn hacks did some neat passing, but the Oldham defence was watchful and keen, and usually the ball did not travel far. However, Houghton proved highly successful with his goal-kicking• From marks made by Richardson, he twice sent the ball sailing beautifully over the bar, and he also added a penalty goal. Run- corn thus won by three goals (six points) to one goal (two points). To closely criticise the game under the circumstances would scarcely be fair,for the wind made the ball at times unmanageable, but Runcorn played the better game under the conditions 'prevailing, find de- served their victory. Both teams defended 'far better than they attacked. For Oldham, Thomas, Fletcher, Lees, Ferguson, and Telfer were ever prominent. For Runcorn, Houghton, C Butterworth, Wilkinson, Faulk- ner, and Walker were best. Salford v. Widnes.</p>
        <p>Interest in this c)ntest at Salford was considerably marred by thn dlcded surrriority of the home contin- gent, who had rnattmrs entirely their own was' from the start, and ultimately won by three goals and seven tries (21 points) to nil.</p>
        <p>Mayor, Banks, Green, Knowles and Rowlands, forwards. The home skipper won the toss and he sat Haydock to face a boisterous wind. Tho visitors were the first to become dangerous, a promising move by B tisk] and Mayor being foiled by Cottons. Not to be denied Hay_ dock returned to the attack and Banks shot into the net but the whistle had previously cue for off•side. Tnc resultant free kick put Settle in possession and racing off at top speed, he let fly from loog range, the ball roll- ing across the goal mouth and out of play. Bartley was going nicely for goal when he was foully fl)ored by Gleave, the ensuing free kick being sent over the bar. The visitors' forwards next made a sudden dash to the other eni and tricky work by Rowlands left him with an open goal but his shot -vas sent high over the bar much to the disappointment of the Haydock portion of the crowd. Notwithstanding the disadvantage Hay. dock were put to in having to face the wind, they kept pegging away and up to the interval had every bit as much of the game as the hornesters. The play of the forwards in the open was very smart and tricky but their efforts in the goal mouth lacked finish, erratic shooting spoiling two or three glorious chances. On the other hand Earle,stown were nearly as bad as the visi- tors when it came to shooting but they did give Gleam() a few teasers to deal with whereas Chew hardly bad a decent opportunity of distinguishing laimcelf the whole of the first half. However the whistla blew for the in- terval with the score sheet blank.</p>
        <p>On resuming the Havdoek right wing got the ball into the home goal but Cottam very soon turned them to the rightabout and at the s IMP time placed Hayes in posses- sion. This player swung the ball out to Settle who dribbled a short distance and then got in a correct centre which left Hayes with a nice opening. He made full use of his chance as he swung round on his left foot and let fly with his right, the ball entering the far corner of the net quite out of the reach of the custodian. The goal was the result of a fine piece of work capped by a lovely shot from Hayes who received the ball at a difficult angle. The cheers had hardly died away when Leach got clean away and kicking the ball past Gleave, he dodged the back and pounced on it again as it was rolling out of play and got a fine centre just under the bar, Settle getting his head to tbe leather and—Eirles- town were two up. Both the points came within half-a- minute of each other and after the second half had been in progress but four minutes. The play quiet ried down a little after this, and the ball was bept chiefly in mid- field until about twenty minutes before the close the homesters again got the ball past Gleave and made the game secure. After th's Earlestown were content to rest on their laurels and Flaydock fell away altogether, the final coming with Earlestown victors by three goals to nil.</p>
        <p>Taken as a whole the'play was of a very scrambling nature and more lik, 3 junior league match than an English Cup tie. Haydock are not by a long chalk the team they used to be and if they do not shape any better in their Lancashire League matches than they did on Saturday, they will find themselves very near to—if not actually—the wooden spoonists when the season clQ.es. The experiment of trying Gieave in goal instead af Dineen was worthy of fu ther consideration. Gleave shaped exceedingly well ald was not responsible for any of the three shots which heat him, The visitors played a very stubborn game up to the interval but as soon as Earlestown scored their two first goals they seemed to go all to pieces and never once afterwards did they look at all like scoring. They have a couple of good backs,and a reliable intermediate trio but their forwards are only a very moderato lot. There is no method in their play and they are utterly at sea when it ' comes to shooting. Chew only had about half a dozen shots to negotiate during the whole of the game and the majority of these came from the half- b Icks. Chew played his usual masterly game between the sticks. The backs were in fine trim and kicked and tackled with rare judgment. Flannagan has only turned out for Earlestosan twice and curiously enough both times have been against Haydock and he has played a grand game on each occasion. He is a lad who should be persevered with and were he a little taller he would be a class bick. The hall-backs were altogether too good for the Haydock forwards and easily accounted for their fitful rushes, Pennington being the pick of the trio. Tne forwards were very fair, Hayes filling the position of centre forward very creditably although he is far t)o bustling for a forward. Leach and Settle were the best of the bunch. The gate was the most disappointing affair of the whole match, although the fact of the fixture clashing wi h Haydock Park races would account for a good many spectators being absent.</p>
        <p>" A MEAN AND CONTEMPTIBLE TIIEFT."-AN UMBRELLA</p>
        <p>STOLEN FROM THE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>A respectably-dressed man named i7V-iniam Venables, was brought before the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, on a charge of stealing an umbrella of the value of 53, the property of William Holland, from the Reading Room at the Warrington Library.—The Chief Constable said the man was charged with what he (Mr Talbot) considered to be a very mean and contemptible theft. It appeared that on Friday afternoon, Mr William Holland, who was a pianoforte tuner and resided at 21, Liverpool-road, went to the ipublic reading room, and placed his umbrella in the stand provided for the purpose. He remained there for about an hour, and as he wished to leave, he went to the stand for the umbrella and found it was missing. He complained to the officials at the Library, and the prisoner having been seen in the reading room,suseicion I was attached to him owing to some previous conduct of his at the Library. Inform ition was given to the police with the result that P.C. Clough went in plain clothes to the prisoner's lodgings at the British Work- man, Ehrsemarket-street, and in the stand he saw the missing umbrella. He found the prisoner and charged him and he appeared to be perfectly callous to the charge. He said he had nothing to say and he had remained silent ever since • He was before the Court on September 17th on two charges of theft' and was fined. —The magistrates now committed the prisoner to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>INHUMAN PARENTS SEVERELY PUNISHED.</p>
        <p>The Warrington magistrates investigated -a —shocking case of family neglect. on Monday morning,Samuel and Mary Jones, who reside at 25, Barnard-street, Sankey Bridges, being charged with neglecting their five childrien in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suff-.ring and injury to health.—Mr. A. Browne, who ccniucted the prosecution on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., intormed the Bench that the male pri•soner was a shingler,and used to work at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s works, and had constant employment until he was discharged for drunkenness. He had since been taking by-turns. He had allowed his wife 223 a week when he was working fu,l time. The prisoners had been warned over and over again,not only by P.C. Woods but by the present and the late inspector of the N.S.P.C.C. There were five children who had been neglected, and their ages were respectively 13 years, ten years, seven years. six years, and three years, and there was the infant, the female prisoner then held in her arms. One of the children had been kept by a neighbour for a month.and had been fed for several months, and the whole of the neighbours became so enraged about the conduct of the prisoners that they informed Inspector Hackett. The neighbours present would tell the Bench a terrible tale. When Inspector Hackett visited the house, accom- panied by P.C. Woods, he found that the woman was out,and that there was only one boy in the house. He was, however, well nourished, but poorly clad. There was no foed in the house, and the furniture consisted of a dirty box,a child's chair and an old perambulator in which the baby slept. There was fno chair to sit on, or any table upon chicta the food could be placed. Upstairs there were two mattresses wnich were in a filthy state. Subsequently the other children were examined, and were found to 131 in a dirty condition. One child's head was " running with vermin."—The Chairman : What led up to this ? Was it the drink ? Mr. Browne: It is eutirely drink.—A neighbour, named Barnett, said "the house was destitute." The male prisoner gave his wife money, but she got drunk. He also got drink,but there was "no proper home for hingto go to." Witness blamed the wife more than the husband. She had constantly fed the children, and other neigh- bours had acted in a similar manner.—Mr. T. J Garnett, relieving officer, said the female prisoner on Tuesday morning last applied to him for an order for the Work- house. He subsequently called at her house. The youngest child was asleep in an old perambulator in the back room, while another child was lying asleep on the floor in front of a small fire. The " furniture" con- sisted of an old box.—lnspector Hackett having given evidence, the female prisoner was sent to gaol for six months, and the male for three months.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mer- cantile Gaztte." the number of new bankruptcy pro- ceedings in England and Wales gazetted duricg the week ending October 6th, was 69. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 68, showina an in- crease of one, being a net increase in 1900, to date, of 181.</p>
      </div>
