      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Rochdale Hornets v. Wigan.</head>
        <p>Rochdale Hornets v. Wigan.</p>
        <p>Owen soon dropped a goal for the visitors, at Roch- dale, and then Anderton added another from a try by Eckersley. Rigz replici with a penalty goal, and one —from a try Whiteman ; consequently the game was left drawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Leigh v. Barrow.</head>
        <p>Leigh v. Barrow.</p>
        <p>Johnston eponed the scoring for the home side at Leigh, and Williams evalised. Then D ivies and Towers eros3pd the Barr®Nv line, and as Molyneux converted the former's try, Leigh won by eleven points to three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Brouzhton7Rangers v. Millom.</head>
        <p>Brouzhton7Rangers v. Millom.</p>
        <p>Tao opening stages of this match at Broughton wero very oven' y fought, but the Ranlera gradually asserted their superiority, and finally won by three goals and a try to a try Lancaster). Hardisty, Harry, and James contributed t 3 the winners' score.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>St. Helens v. Stockport.</head>
        <p>St. Helens v. Stockport.</p>
        <p>These teams brough t their evgagement to an issue a S. 1131e08. The play was of a stubborn characte t!inughout, the home club proving su'.:cmsful by a tr t 3 nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Leeds v. Holbeck.</p>
        <p>Nothing was sccred at Leads until half-time was fast approaching, and then points came freely until at the close Leeds claimed the victory by three goals an,-I bur tries to a goal and a try. Malins,Jagger, Willey,Leonard, Daitry, Luintey,Goolfadow,and Garrity scored for their respective sides.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Halifax v. Liversedge.</head>
        <p>Halifax v. Liversedge.</p>
        <p>In miserable weather these fifteens met at Halifax. The home side had a little the better of the play all through,and al3r leading by a goal and a try at half- time, they won in the end by thirteen points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Wakefield v. Batley.</head>
        <p>Wakefield v. Batley.</p>
        <p>Play was very keen at Wakefield—the venue of this encounPer. In the opening half Stubby obtained a try which D ivies improved upon for B ttley, who won by five points to a dropped goal (two pone)—secured by Lockwood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Castleford v. Leeds Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Castleford v. Leeds Parish Church.</p>
        <p>All the scoring in this encounter at L,eds was re- corded prior to half time. Mosley kicked two penalty goals and plac.d another—from vVade's try—for Leeds, who won by six points to nil. Bradford v. Hull.</p>
        <p>Though unable to defeat their powerful rivals. at Hull, Bradford manazed to retain their unbeaten record, as the game requited in a draw of five points each. Broadley and Eager, scored for Bradford, and Parkinson for Hull.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Huddersfield v. Bramley.</head>
        <p>Huddersfield v. Bramley.</p>
        <p>Playing upon their own ground Huddersfield ha.d the best of matters, and led by a: couple of tries at the change over. Afterwards they scored two tries to their oppneats' goal, and won b 7 twelve points to two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Brighouse Rangers v. Hunslet</head>
        <p>Brighouse Rangers v. Hunslet</p>
        <p>vigitors won this match in the earlier portion of the game at Brighouse. During this period they ran up a score of throe goals and three tries. Subsequently the Rattlers notched a goal and a try, and suffered defeat by ten points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Manningham v. Hull Kingston Rovers.</head>
        <p>Manningham v. Hull Kingston Rovers.</p>
        <p>Manniagiaam was the scene of this match. Up t ) the int.rval the R ,V3rS were sen to advantage, and crossed over leading by nineteen points. Towards the close the home side scored a goal and a try (five points) and lost by that number to nineteen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>" A " Team Competition.</head>
        <p>" A " Team Competition.</p>
        <p>Swinton v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>Warrington " A "team sustained a reverse on Satur- diy when they j)urneyed to Swinton. The home team had the al vantage of the wind in the first half, and after ten minutes' play Goodman scored, but no goal re- sulted. Warrington had now a turn at pressing, and a combined passing movment ended inldlores scoring. A further score was made shortly afterwards by Smith, and just before the interval Barrick got over for Swin- ton. Upon resuming, Swinton opened the attack, but after a few minutes defending the visitors by good back play relieved to the centre, and the danger was ler a timel removed. Goodman and Jones were in turnipro- minent, and play was again taken to the Warrington quarters. Here a pretty piece of passing enabled Williams to score. ThC goal kick failed. At the call of time Swinton were leading by eleven points to six.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Lancashire Junior League.</head>
        <p>Lancashire Junior League.</p>
        <p>LATCRFORD RANGERS V. RUNCORN JUNIORS.—This match was played at Latchford. The play for some time was confined to tie centre. Latchford then pressed hut the juniors drove them back Half-time—no score• ft-tiParting„Latchford at once pressed and Lomas scored the goal kick failing. Latchfori continued to have the best of the game tut failed to add any more to the score. Fina::—Latchford Rangers,three poiats; Runcorn Juniors, nil. WIDNES WHITE STAR V. Sr. HELENS SEIAMROCKS. At Widnes. A Pet- n kicked off for the Scar against a strong wind, and the pl ty soon became very fast, the referee having his work cut out. Cockram scored for the Stir, Potors converting. Pefers also added a goal. Final :—Star, one goal two tries, Shamrocks, one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Other Matches.</head>
        <p>Other Matches.</p>
        <p>FLETcnsa RU4SELL'S V. LEIGII " A."—Plaved on the ground of the former on Saturday. Fletcher's lacked the assistance of F)den and Brown. Leigh started against a strong e.ros,- wind and no return was made. In the scrimmaging which followed Fietcher's held the advantage ad soon they got within scoring distance of the visitors' lines, but the defence proved equal to the occasion. Fletch -es superiority in the front rank repeatedly'gained fr them possession, but Scott and Ashurst failed to make headway. C qless and Clare were next prominent with some really fine play, and a grand effort alrnoit resulted in the latter crossing tbe line. T Knight got possessien at centre, and passing all opposliou give to Corless, who was thus enabled to score for the home team. Ashurst took th 3 kick and landed a goal from a difficult position. Ilalf-time, Fletcher Russell's, one goal one try ; Leigh " A," nil. Restarting, Leigh pressed until r Knight came to the rescue and a nice run and kick transferred. A free kick to Leigh did not gain them anything. Final : Fletcher Russell's, one goal one try (five points) ; Leigh " A," nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>English Cup.—Preliminary Round.</p>
        <p>Earlestown v. Haydock.</p>
        <p>These two local rivals met on the idesnes enc!osure on Saturdry in the first English Cup Tie ever played on that ground. In recent years a meeting between these two teams would draw a crowd of five or six thousand. But that was when the spirit of rivalry was so keen, and never since the differecces between the Haydock players and their Committee of last season—owing to which the majority of the H tydoek cracks left the team—have the colliery eleven had a look in with E trlestown. In the old Lancashire Alliance matches the encounters between the two teams used to be one continual run of excite- ment from the beginning of the match to the end, and nothing could be heard for the whole of the ninety minutes but yells and roars from five thousand enthusi- astic throats. But what a difference now. Why on Saturday there wasn't five minutes excitement in the game, and excepting now and again a cry of" Play up, Earlestown " or Haydock as the case may be, and a cheer when a goal was scored. hardly a sound was to be heard. And as for the spectators, instead of five thou- sand there would barely be five hundred present. Both teams were fully represented, although Whitehouse failed to put in an appearance for E arlestown, Hayes being shifted to centre forward, and Fiannagan taking his place at right full back. Punctually at half-past three the trams faced each other as follows :</p>
        <p>Earlestown : Cnew, goal ; Flannagan and Cottam, backs ; Pennington, Green, and Ciibborn, half backs ; Bartley, Linton, fiayei, Leach, and Settle, forwards. Efaydock : J G'eave, goal : T Gleav and Birendale. back 3 ; Davies, For&amp;haw, and Howarth, half backs ;</p>
      </div>
