      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>nly regret is not haying tried</p>
        <p>Scott's Emulsion sooner.</p>
        <p>S CLA (From a Photoaraph.)</p>
        <p>38, Park Place, Christchurch, Hants, March 19th, 1900.. Dear Sirs,—lt is with much pleasure lam writing to testify to the great benefit my baby has derived from taking Scott's Emulsion. He suffered from indigestion when a fortnight old; he cried almost incessantly and had no rest night or day. I tried various kinds of food, and almost everything suggested, but nothing did him any good, as he could not digest his food. He did not thrive, and at seven months old could not sit up, and was so' weak that with the mere exertion of taking his food the perspiration rolled off him. I never expected to rear him. When I saw in the papers what good other children had derived from taking Scott's Emulsion I decided to give it a trial, and only regret not having tried it sooner. After baby had taken it a few days I could see a decided improvement, and he began to gain both flesh and strength. Before taking Scott's Emulsion he gained One pound in three months; after taking the contents of four bottles, gained ten and a half pounds. He is now twelve months old, and a strong, healthy boy, as you can see by the enclosed photograph. No one would believe him to be the same child. He can eat well, sleep well, and now just as happy as he was miserable before, all due to Scott's Emulsion' I hope all mothers who have delicate or weakly babies will be induced t ...e.° give Scott's Emulsion a fair trial. It must be very pleasing to the taste, my baby is always delighted to see the bottle and prefers it to anything can give him. Wishing Scott's Emulsion every success it deserves,</p>
        <p>I remain, Dear Sirs, Yours truly, (Signed) ANNIE TILLEY.</p>
        <p>If you have a little baby that is suffering from in- digestion. you cannot do better than to give that baby Scott's Emulsion. If the baby happens to be teething, Scott's Emulsion will furnish lime for the teeth, will sooth the nerves and have a quieting effect upon the entire organism of the child. The digestion will be put right, and you will see USIMiS• takable evidence that Scott's Emulsion is doing the baby good in many ways. Mothers can hardly realise what a comfort ScAt's Emulsion is to children until they try it. At every pericd of growth Scott's Emulsion is of great value in overcoming the wasting of the system, promoting the making of healthy flesh enriching the blood and giving the child that vigour and strength which means so much in after years. There is no mystery about Scott's Emulsion. It is merely the best possible way of taking cod-liver oil, hvpophosphites of limo and soda, and glycerine. No other preparation is like Scott's Emulsion, and none compares favourably with it. The way Scott's Emulsion is manipulated and the way its ingredients are</p>
        <p>blended tog( thEr have never been successfully tated. There is something about Scott's Eulsion ta which will produce beneficial results when other. remedies will not. You will readily be convince° that this is truo if you will give Scott's Emulsion 3 fair trial. Not only is cod-iver oil rendered P_ler- fectly palatable in Scott's Emulsion, but it is °gel-t— -in a form that aids digestion instead of disturbil* it. The genuine Scott's Emulsion may easily be diSi tinguished by our trade mark on the tvraPPerL every bottle. You will be amply repaid if you tail' the trouble to see that the bottle you purchase con: tains this trade mark, which stands for the best CO liver oil preparation that has ever been offered tO the public. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion bY sending threepence in stamps and mentioning t" name of this paper, to Scott and Bowne, LiMited. 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E.C.</p>
