      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT-</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT-</p>
        <p>FRlDLY—Before Mr._ Registrar Ridgway:</p>
        <p>_ /to J us Nicor..—This debut. formerly a jeweller in Buttermarket-street. appeared again for a public examina- tion.—Mr. Johnson (assistant Official Receiver), asked for the examination to be further adjourned so as to enable the debtor to do something more in the matter of presenting his statement of accounts than he had dose.—Mr. Fowler, who appeared for the relative,• said the debtor had rendered all the accounts be could.—The Registrar said the presentation of accounts was thoroughly unsatisfactory and he was dis- posed to give the debtor another chance to perfect tbam.—Mr Fowler did not think the debtor had the materials to do so. tie did not keep proper books and therefore was not in a position to present a full statement,—The Registrar said the debtor ought to have the invoices of the firms with which be dealt with for three or tour months prior to filing his petition. The examination was most unsatisfactory.—Mr. Fowler said it was in some respects, but be was prepared to go into the dealings with Messrs. Reed and Son, and put a batter complexion on the matter. —Mr. Johnson said in this case it was necessary that the debtor should include in his statement the large quantity of goods he received. The examination was adjourned. _ .</p>
        <p>RE JOHNSON Joivsox.—This debtor who resides at Penketh appeared for his lint public examination in bank- ruptcy.—Mr. A White represented him.—Mr. Johngon said he was not prepared to go into the matter that morning and asked for thecaae to be adjourned until November 2nd.—This was agreed to.</p>
        <p>A BANKRUPT WEIGHING MLCHINE FITTER.—Tohn Eccles, of 46, Nicholson-street, Warrington, and now a journeyman weighing machine fitter, lately carrying on business as an engineer and weighing machinist at the Falcon Works. John-street, appea-ed for his public examination in bank- ruptcy. The statement of affairs showed a deficiency of £269 12s 31. In his statement to the Official iieceiver the bankrupt alleqed as the cause of his failure " Ili-health ; loss on a contract; and heavy cost of patterns, which, in many cases, were only usad once." He also stated that he com- menced business in March, 1834, without capital ; executed deed of assignment for benefit of creditors early in May last ; that than aseets were, except as to us Bd, wholly absorbed in payment of rent and solicitor's and trustee's costs. Not having kept proner books of account °Particularly no cash hook, and only MI impeeeet creditor's ledger, he could not file prescribed deficiency account. He first became aware of his insolvency early this year and had since con- tracted only a very few small dects. The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RECENT FANCY DRESS PARADE IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE RECENT FANCY DRESS PARADE IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Satisfactory Result.</p>
        <p>Mr Charles Leigh tne hon. treasurer for the grand fancy dress parade and carnival held on Saturday, the 25th August, in aid of the Warring- ton Infirmary and Dispensary has favoured us with a copy of the balance sheet. It is satisfactory to record that the amount handed over to the treasurer of the Warrington Infirmary Workingmen's Com- mittee is £263 17s 21. Readers will remember that an effort was made to realise £2OO and all the will ing helpers on that occasion must be gratified that that sum was more than realised.</p>
        <p>The total receipts were £279 Is 9d, made up as follows: donations, £1 63 ; sundry collections, Miss Beatrice Crompton 183 9d, R Scari3brick qd, Miss Marshall £2 7s 71-'l3, Mrs Chester 193 Thd, Mr Harry Toft 53 01;-1 ; Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club, is 114(1; Pattern Arms Hot-1 (hotel £2 7s 511,d, vaults 23 34) £2 93 8, total ..C7 3s ; hurdy- gurdy, Longshaw ..E1 93 1, B tlibbert 14s 81d (less 63 hire) total £1 173 9.d; stand 13:a. collection £8 us 0; pole bags £2B 5s 5d ; band bags £2l 153 lid; bcxes £9l 16 11J; lurries (Warrington Slate Company, £1 193 ; Isherwood, Li 813 90, James Newton, £1 133 21), total £5 is 54 ; Padgate Cycling Club, £6 163 lid, proceeds of concert £3O 53 :91 (less expenses £6 17s), total, £23 Sc 9d; programmes £l2 103 61 ? book collection, £65 8s id ; received from Prize Committee, balance in hand after providing prizes £4 93 Od ; bank interest us 61; grand total £279 Is 9d.</p>
        <p>The payments were printing and advertising £ll 133 61 : :,undry expenses of parade including postages, carter 3 and 'bus and railway fares, £3 us id, total, £l5 43 7d ; thus leaving a balance in aid of the Infirmary of £263 17s 2d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FINAL MEETING OF COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINAL MEETING OF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The final meeting of the General Committee of the above Parade was held at the Patten Arms Hotel. The chair was ocoupied by Mr D Davies (Warrington C.C.) and over 30 members of the com- mittee were present, including representatives of all the athletic bodies who took part in the parade. Reports were received from the various committees which were all deemed satisfactory. It was decided to give extra prizes to all those who were recom- mended for the same by the judges at the parade. The statement of accounts, shewing receipts ..£279 is 9d, and expenses £l5 4s 7d, was submitted by the Hon Treasurer, Mr Charles Leigh, and it was unanimously adopted, a hearty vote of thanks being given to the Hon. Treasurer and the Finance Committee for their services, and also to Mr Arthur Bennet, tor kindly auditing the accounts.—Votes of thaVrs were passed to all the members of the various Committees, whose efforts were rewarded with such a magnificent result. The best thanks of the Committee are also tendered to Mr H P Greenough, Messrs J and W Dutton, Mr W P Chorley, and Messrs Armitage and Rigby, Ltd.. whose names did not appear in the official pro- gramme. The Committee wish, through the medium of the Press, to give special thanks to all the ladies who gave their invaluable services in col- lecting with books, boxes and bags, and hope that, at any future parade which may be held for such a deserving object, they can again obtain the services of such a large and enthusiastic body of fair collec- tors.—Councillor John Roberts proposed, and Mr G Legge seconded a hearty vote of thanks to the hon. secretaries (Messrs E Pickavance, junr. and A Booth) and the hon, treasurer (Mr Charles Leigh) for their services in connection with the parade, and after these gentlemen had responded, a vote of thanks to the Chairman brought to a close the arduous,but pleasant duties, which have resulted in the sum of .£263 178 21 being handed over to our noblest local charity, the Warritr,ton Infirmary and Dispensary. I The Hon. Treasurer will be pleased to give parti- culars to any lady or gentleman who wishes to know his or her individual collection, and afford anyone all information, on application being made to him at Bew.,ey Chambers, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR. W. J. BLECKLY.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR. W. J. BLECKLY.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, October 3rd, the little villages of Ockbrook and Borrowash were en fête on the occa- sion of thef marriage of Mr Wilfred J Bleckly, second son of MrlWr H Bleckly, J.P. of Thelwall Lea, to Miss Barbara C H Whiteley, second daugh- ter of Mr W A Whiteley, Derwent House, Borro- wash. The bride accompanied by her father, arrived at the Parish Church, Ockbrook, punctually at the hour of one. She looked charming, attired in a handsome dress of white satin richly embroi- dered with pearls and elegantly trimmed with lace and chiffon. She wore her mother's veil of embroi- dered Brussels net and carried a bouquet of choice exetiss, the gift of the bridegroom. The church was filled with numerous spectators, the bride be- ing well known in the district. The chan- cel and first four seats in the nave were reserved for the relatives. The bride, who was given away by her father, was at- tended by three bridesmaids, Miss Mabel Whiteley her sister, and Miss Blackly sister of the bridegroom, and Miss Hilda Pratt cousin of the bride who were attired in gowns of blue voile over blue silk, trimmed with guipure lace threaded with gold long lace fichus, caught at the left side and falling to the knee. Black panne waist bands and black picture hats completed this tasteful toilet. Each bridesmaid carried a bouquet of golden Yellow chrysanthemums, which together with gold and platinum bugle scarf pins were the gifts of the bridegroom. The Rev Lewis Lewis, Vicar of Ock- brook officiated and the Rey T Pym Williamson, Vicar of Thel wall performed the ceremony. The newly married pair left the church to the strains of Mendelssobn'e wedding march (brilliantly played by Mr A J Cotton) amid showers of rice and good wishes. The bridegroom was attended by his brother Mr A S Blackly as best man; Mr W C John- son and Mr Jack Lord acted the part of groomsmen. 'A reception was afterwards held at Derwent House at which only relatives were present. Mr and Mrs W J Blackly left early in the afternoon for Bristol en route for Cornwall where the honeymoon will be soent,the bride travelling in a pastel blue coat and skirt trimmed with velvet and steel buttons and a- bodice of blue and white silk trimmed with lace. Her hat was of grey felt trimmed with blue panne. She also wore a sable boa and muff with a gold muff chain the latter being the gift of the bridegroom. The bride's mother wore a rich serpent green silk dress tastefully trimmed with miroir velvet and oriental silk with vest of embroidered white satin. Her toque, see uite, was trimmed with shaded roses and she carried a bouquet of the same flowers. The bridegroom's mother was attired in a mouse col- oured ribaline cloth dress with black satin stitched bands richly embroidered in a lovely rose pattern on white chiffon. She carried a bouquet of crimson roses and wore a black chenille toque with white lace and roses to correspond. Over a hundred wed- ding gifts were received among them being many handsome pieces of furniture and silver.</p>
        <p>To MOTHERS.—Mrs. Winrlow's Soothing'Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their zhildren while teething, with perfect success. It will relieve the poorsuffcrer immediatPly It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural oniet steep, oy relieving the child from pain anfi the ittle (Atonal) awakes "as fright as a bylton." Of all Chemists is lid per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>WARRINGT3N. Presentation to Alderman Shaw Green.</p>
        <p>The annual social meeting in coon ection with Silver-street Sunday School was held on Saturday when about 80 members and friends sat down to tea. After the tea the meeting was presided over by the Rev C H Hocken, and wad of a varied character, being interspersed by solos ren- dered by Messrs. Whitehouse and S Gough, Mrs D S Stone, and Miss M Mason, whilst a recitation was given by Miss G Shaw Green. Mr. J Warburton, one of the oldest members of the class, spoke a few words of congratulation to Alder- man Shaw Green on the attainment of his new office, and also said that he had a very pleasant duty to perform on behalf of the past and present members of the class,namely that of presenting Mr. Green with a gentleman's easy chair, hoping that God would spare his life, to enable him to enjoy many happy hours in it. Words could not express the deep feelin;; of respect and appreciation which every member felt for him. They had happy memories of the past which could only encourage and spur them onwards. When thinking of the many lessons taught by him, they regretted that through ill health he had been compelled to resign his office as teacher. Mr J W Hayward and Mr Wise also spoke in appreciation of Mr Green's services. They were all working for the same Master, and this was the greatest joy on earth. Alderman Shaw Green, in responding, said he could not express the thanks he felt. He was 80 overwhelmed at the kindness shown, and the present was so unexpected, that words failed to express the deep sense of gratitude he felt. It had been a bitter trial for him to resign his work in Silver-street Sunday school as superin- tendent, but especially to sever his connection as teacher with the class where some cf the happiest hours of his life had been spent. He spoke at length of his association with it, and said that when resting in the chair it would ever remind him of the love which had prompted the gift. Mr Mitchell proposed a vote of thanks to the ladies for ' their kind assistance, and also to the committee. The meeting was then brought to a close by sing- ing tha Doxology.</p>
        <p>Warrington Markets. At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday, newiwheat fetched 33 81 to 43 (701 b to the bushel) ; new oats,2s 3d and2s 41; old oats,2s lid; flour (best seconds) per 2401 b., 21s; bran, lid per 201 b. At the Provision Market prices were as follows : Fresh butter, Is 3d and is 4d per lb ; cheese, 61 7d and 84 per lb; fresh eggs, 7 and 8 for is; fowls, 4g to 53 6d per couple; English beef, 61d to 10d, mutton 7d to 10d ; lamb, 9i to lid ; veal, 7d to</p>
      </div>
