      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>Terrible Explosion in</p>
        <p>America.</p>
        <p>Ten Thousand Pounds of Dynamite</p>
        <p>Blown Up.</p>
        <p>Hundreds of Men :njured.</p>
        <p>Terrible havoc has been wrought in Minnesota by an explosion of a powder magazine containing 10,000 pounds of dynamite. A. hole 200 feet square and thirty-five feet deep marks the spot where the explosion occurred. There is not a piece of glass a foot square within a radius of three miles. The loss is estimated at 75,0001015. Several men were killed, and 200 persons were more or less seriously injured. The explosion was felt at a distance of twelve miles. The cause of the disaster is unknown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. Another Railway Disaster.</p>
        <p>Serious Casualties.</p>
        <p>The War Office has issued a telegram from Lord Roberts which states.</p>
        <p>Pretoria, October 10, 4.40 p.m.—An unfortunate accident happened at litapmuiden,owing to a train upsetting on the diversion over the Kw River. Three men were killed, fifteen injured. and Lieu- tenant Chawkes had his leg broken, all belonging to the 6th Battery Royal Field Artillery. About forty animals were killed or badly injured.</p>
        <p>An engine, with truck conveying two Royal Engineer officers, and Captain G. Paget, Rifle Bri- gade, and eighteen men or the Vlakfontein garri- son, proceeded along the line yesterday to ascertain the nature of the damage during the previous night. The Boers were lying in wait, and opened fire on the party. On hearing of the attack Captain A. D. Stewart, lite Brigade, went with forty men in sup- port. The casualties, I regret to say, were very heavy. Captain Stewart and a private of the Rifle Brigade were killed and the following wounded .—Captain Paget (dangerously), and five men attached to the Royal Engineers; Lieutenant J H Stubbs, severely. Taken prisoners :—Lieutenant Sewell, Royal Engineers, and ten men of the Rifle Brigade. _</p>
        <p>• Barton, who is clearing the country north of Krugersdorp, reports an engagement with the Boers yesterday, when the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, admir- ably led by Sir Robert Colleton, behaved very well. Several dead Boers were left on the ground, and three prisoners were taken. Our casualties were:— Killed, Royal Welsh Fusiliers ; Second Lieutenant J R iWilliams Ellis. Wounded Captain Gabbett, dangerously, head; Second-Lieutenant H Kyrke, severe head ; and eleven men. Royal Scots Fusiliers, Captain H. M. Trenchard, dangerously chest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Messrs. Crosfield's Soap in the Field Hospitals. An Interesting Missive.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield's Soap in the Field Hospitals. An Interesting Missive.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, who it will be remembered, supplied some of the field hospitals with soap free of charge, have received the following communication :—" Icnperial Yeomanry Hospital, Deel- fontein, Cape Colony, Sep.:amber 18. 1900. Dear Sirs, We, the undersigned beg to enclnsa tbis note of good wishes found in one of your eases of carbo!ic soap which is largely used in this hospital, thinking that it might be of interest to your firm. Yours truly, F B Newman, H H Cook, A H Tyler, Staff in tha Qlarter Master's Stores." _ _</p>
        <p>The note referred to was a piecl of wrapper paper on which the following bad be' written by one of till em- ployoi :—" February 2, 1900. Crosfisld'a Soap Works, Warrington, Lancashire. Success to nor British soldiers. Hoping you will soon hoist the Union Jack over Pretoria."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>THOUSANDS OF WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>THOUSANDS OF WARRINGTON</p>
