      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>– – – . S Vt?"`..„. .mr. *---' • • . – • _ _ • – _</head>
        <p>- - - . S Vt?"`..„. .mr. *---' • • . - • _ _ • - _</p>
        <p>GENERAL ELECTION</p>
        <p>NORTHWICH DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Political Fever at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Runcorn has at length awakened and is now in the height of its Political Fever. Throughout the week, meetings have been held in and about the town,:and the utmost enthusiasm prevails. Large crowds, towards the latter end of the week paraded the streets singing the praises of Brunner and predicting the fall of Simson. The Liberal Club, Regent-3treet, which is the central comreit:ee room his been the scene of great activity, and Liberals have been brought together for a purpose. Devonshire Square was occupied on Wednesday night by Mr E NlciElugh, a labour representative well-known to the Liverpool Docks porters and stevedores, and well- known tor his efforts on behalf of the salt workers of Nortnwich, Winsford, and Runcorn. For four solid hours he addirtved a large concourse of working-men in Devon3hire Square, right under the wind° s of Mr Samson's Committee Room, and proved to his hearers conclusively that Sir J T Brunner and tha Liberal Party were their friends. Subsequently a large crowd of about 300 people who had been I- (Ming a contem- poraneous meeting at the Liberal Club, marched round the town and finally asaemi.ling round the Tory Com- mittee Rooms same with unmistakable enthusiasm "So we will again." The gathering dispersed about 12-30 after reassembling in the Liberal Club and singing the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LIBERAL CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERAL CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>Enthusiastic Meetings at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Oa Saturday last, meetings in furtherance of Sir J. T. Brunner' s candidature, were held at Weston, Clifton, Weston Point and Halton, and in the afternoon Sir John attended the football match at Irwell.lane, and evinced considerable pleasure at the doings of the home team and lustily applauded their well-earfied victory.</p>
        <p>Saturday Night's Meeting at the Public Hall, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>This meeting was presided over by Mr.Sarnuel Taylor. J.P., who was supported on the platten:a by Sir. J. T. , Brunner, and Lady Brunner,Miss Bruener, Messrs. Ellie- Griffith, M.P., M C Macinerner, Q C., A R Norman, j.p., C.C., J R Frith, J M Frith, W Bing, George Bellamy, S Walton, Miss C Taylor, Rest. W Eere, Councillor Denton (Liverpeol),F W Hutton, D Bisbrown, E U EnivetoneT Lea, W Booth, and other ladies and gentlemen. The meeting cemmencel with the singing of an in- spiring election song to the tune of '1 Soldiers of the Queen." The Chairman, in introducing the speakers, took the opportunity of saying that it was himself ani not Sir John Brunner who at the last meeting dwelt upoe Sir John's generosity. Sir John was the type of gentleman I who never let his right hand know what his left was Idoing—(hear. hear, and applause),—and he could not but express surprise at the utterances of the Chairman of Mr. Samson's meeting which took place in that hall the previous Wednesday. Referring, to the Agricultural Land Rating Act, Mr Taylor went o to say that he had seen it in print that Runcorn contributed £l,OOO to the purposes of the doles given to the Bill, and the parsons. Based on a population of 20,000 inhabitaws, it worked out to about is per head for each individual ; so that the poor workingman, with say a family of eight chit- dren,and a wife,had to contribute 10a to the relief of the landierd, or tenant of a farmeand to the relief of denoire- national schools. (Shame.) It was eeough to make the stones cry out. He contended that the money die not 'relieve the agricultural labourer one cent ; and if any- one needed relief from taxation it was those honest, straightforward, hard-working labourers who'were called upon to contribute so heavily to those doles. (Applause). Sir John Brunner, who was warmly received said he hoped that from now, until after the counting, they would treat Mr. Samson wi'h courtesy that was due to a stranger. (Hear, hear and applause.) He hoped nc- body would ask what wages he paid to his clerks— (applause)—nor what hours they worked. (A Voice: "No personalities.") Quite right ; let them have no personalities. (Applause.) He heard that a further placard had been put out about the question of the 30 years of age limit at Winnington. It wis said that Jabez Weedall said to him (Sir john) that a notice had been put up in their time office that nobody should be taken on over 30 years of age. His (Sir John's) eight was not very good, and he did not recoeni:e his friend Mr. Jabez Weedall, and he thought the man at the other end of the room, who was almost invisible in the uncertain light, was a man who spoke with a knowledge of what he uttered. He took what he said about the notice being put up in the time office as gospel truth. Immediately atter he had sat down friends on either side of him said " Whet did you answer that fellow for? Why that is Jabez Weedall." He (Sir John) said '6 Was ' it ?" His occupation in life was described to him, but he could not repeat the word used. A jocular friend of his had described him as an extensive local game dealer without a licence. (Letsehter, and app'ause.) Jahn attended a meeting at Nerthwich a few days ago, and again brought up the question. He went on and on. being Extremely good humoured exert it, until at last he (Sir John) said "Jabez, the only way, to keep thee quiet, ie to hang a hare in front of thee." A number of the audience on the side of the gallery did not appar- ently realise the bunittir, and Sir John turning to them exclaimed "You don't catch the joke somehow." (A Voice : "He would soon have it," and laushter.) The day following: the meeting he wrote to Dr Hewitt, his colleague, who knew all about those thin, asking him if such notices had been put up, and he wrote luck that no such notices had ev,r ben put up in the time office or anywhere else. (A voice : "It nets put up inithe office.") Some little uproar ensued during which a voice exclaimed " Chuck hi:n out. Oal at a time." The originator of the interruption was reepelted by several of the audience to go on the s'ivt; but refused.) Sir John then repeated what he had said—no notice was nut up. (Leei apolense.) He noticed that in spite of the fact that he had publicly denied by word of mouth and po.ters that there W4q a rule against taking on men over 30 years of' age. Mr. Samson himself with his own mouth hai repeated the charge. (Shame, and hsar, h.:ar.) He had said that he had the ryestest personal respect for him (Sir John). If he had, let him take his word. (Loud app'auss). Let him talk to the men for a minute. Tehey did not carry on Brunner, Mond and Company's works as a philae- thropic institution. It was not a hospital. (Rear, hew, and applanae.) Every man who:had worked for them ten years, if his health failed.and he ceuld nab work any longer, would receive 5; a week if he was in need of it. (Applause.) If he had worked eleven years *they gave him 5561 ; 12 years, 6; 6 1 ; 13 years 63 6d, and so on to a maximem of 101 per week, When they had made up their minds to give anybody who served them well for ten years 53 a week for the rest of his life, this, had carried it on to the widows in a good man z cases. (Loud applause.) Having made up their mind Ye that, they were not going to take on anybody else's cripples. (Hear, hear.) They paid Trades Union wages through- out, and they worked less than Trades Union hours. That being so they were entitled to the best men to be obtained in the cluntry—(hear, hear)—and they got them. (Loud applause.) Was it quite understood ? Be did not want to be driven, by fear of losing that election, into talking to them like a humbug. Then gave Trades Union wages, and they expected good work for it—(hear, hear)—and it was because they gave good wages, and because they got good men that they made their bandsems divi- dends at the end of the year. (Loud applause.) After Mr. Samson had once apologise.' te him, he had again repeated that false charge against Brunner. Mond and Company, and it seemed to him that that was his way of evincing his personal respect. On the previous Sater- day night he was in Runcorn, and a genttemen oroviled him with a written report of what had been said by Mr. Samson the night before. He had said it at previous meetings, and he repeated it that night. He challenged him (Sir John Brunner) to say" Aye or no "to three questions with regard to his being's member of the Stop- the-War Committee, contributing to the funds thereto, and attending a meeting in Exeter Hall. Seven times were those terrible questions asked. He never went near a Stop-the-War Committee, never became a mem- ber, and never gave a COODer to itg funds. (Loud ap- plause, and a Voice : "You are a coward.") All this thunder was intended te prove to them that he was a pro-Boer, and he never went near the place. Mr. S am- son not only asked qhim the questions, but he said he did. He also said he believed—and in this particular he was more careful—that he did subscribe. It was lei- possible for one man to know what another did. Mr. Samson had never seen his (Sir John's) cheque book, so he acted wisely. (A. voice : "I should like to see it," and laughter.) On Tues lay nieht he (Sir John) read a speech of Mr. Saminn'e delivered at Helton, and there, to hie surprise, he found the following passage :—" Sir John Brunner said the other night at Runcorn that he had never b oen a member of the Stop-the-War Committee. I have not access to the mieute book in which the record is kept of all the proceedings of that committee." Mr Sam :on said he had not access to the minute book of that com- mittee, nor the cash book or ledgers, and yet he said he did not believe his (Sir John'Astatemente ("Shame.") IRe did not believe him when he denied ever having had anything to do with that committee, and yet Mr. Sam- son lensed before thew as a man in whom they should nut their trust—the highest trust that one men could put in another—that was to say to send him to the House of Commons. (A voice ! " He is not going.") 01 Thursday last ha knew that Mr. Samson'was to attend a meeting at Northwieb, and that Sir Joseph Verdin was to take the chair. He thereupon wrote to Sir Joseph Verdin, telling him exactly what had appeared in the newspaper, and said that the report was far too detailed and circumetantial to permit of the supposition that , there was say material mistake is it, and he therefore</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1900.</p>
        <p>asked him in his capacity of Chairman, and as a man of honour, to demand from Mr. Samson an ample and frank apology for what he had said. (thud applause). Mr. Samson was reperted as baying said at that meeting that, when he refused to believe his (Sir John's) denial that he had been a member of the Stop-the-War Committee, tbat be dis- believed him on the faith of evidence in his possession. I Was it come to that,that any written or spoken evidence given to Mr. Samson was to be taken against his word. ("No.") Think of it. Here was a gentleman of trained legal intelligence, who took not the smallest trouble to ascertain what were the facts before he charged him with those things in order te prove that he was a prc- Beer. The meeting mentioned was held in Eceter Hall on January 11th. They knew all about biro, and would, therefore, understand that he was not boasting when he said that when s cemmittee got him to attend a meet- ing they took vary good care that his name appeared in the papers. He was a bit of a catch. (L tuehter.) Next morning there appeared a report in the Lon ton papers of that blessed Stop-the-War Committee, and his name did not appear amongst those attending. (Applause.) He did not say that to prove he was not there ; he only said it to show them how extremely earelena Mr. Sam' eon had been before he uttered charges whirl woidd damn a man's political character. (A eolsus ) On Tuesday night last Mr. Samson sal I he h,er I tren his friends that he (Sir J3hn) had denied bsine a rn.mber of the committee,and he found oat that his friend+ believed him because they knew him. (Applause.) If he and those present had been behind the scenes he was satis- fied they would know that Mr Samson's friends made it se brolly hot let him that he was glad to apologise. (Loud applause.) During the ten or eleven years he (Sir John) was in Widnes, he earned a high character as a goon Radical Tnere were men on that platform he had known f or 39 years. Mr Frith had been acquainted with him for 37 years. He went from Widnes to Win- , nington, and he had maintained his character as a good sound Radical ever since. (Loud applause.) He asked them t support him not because he was a trader in a big way of business, but because he was a Radical poli- tician—(loud applause)—deairing above all things that every man should be equal before the law, and that every child in this country should twee the best possible opportunity of developing its mind and character until it did the best for its own life and the best for its own family—(loun applanse)—and the best for this country that God enabled it to do. (Cheers.) At what point in all the recore of 25 years did he cease to become an Eng- lish patriot ? (A Voice: " Not at all.") If they had not believed him to be a lover of his country, a lover of Cheshire, and a lover of them, they never would have put him into the offices he had occupied. (Loul ap- plause.) He asked them to believe that his patriotism as it began when be was a lad had cereinuei all his life, and that it was not a matter of patriotism of three week's duration. (Loud applause.) Mr. Ellis Griffin', M.P., who was the recipient of a splendid ovation, asked them as a Runcorn audience and a Cheshire audience to give a fair hear- ing to a man who was addressing them, although they might not agree with everything he said. (Applause.) Words broke no bones although a fall from a gallery might, and he saw uo reason why they should not have a quiet and harmonious half-hour. The duty that called them there that night was not to support the Liberal party, but to hear see of the candidates who was asking for their suffrages. He trusted many of them would go to hear Mr. Samson, and after hearing both candidates it would be their duty and pleasure to vote fir the gentleman who on the whole they thought was the more capable of representing them in the House of Commons. (Loud applause.) He heard someone sly something about pro-Boor. Let him say one word personally in reference to himself. He was one of those Liberals who from the commencement to the end of this war supported the Gsvernment in the House. (Loud applause.) He might have beea right and he might have been wrong, but nobody at any rite could apply pro-Beer to him. What did pro- Boer mean? (A Voice: "Pro means against. Beer means Tory." Laughter.) He thought they had had a fall explanation from Sir John Brunner that during his pubic life he had been a good citiz m of this country, and he (Mr Griffith) could assure them that the neigh- bouring counties of Cheshire envied them such a man to live in their midst. (Applause.) If they inquired into the nickname—pro-Boer—it meant very little. It was like every other insult and offence, it would not bear looking into, and he thought the fact that he was upon that platform, pleened and proud to be present in sup- port of Sir John Branner—(houd applause)—proved that he like the Cheirinau, believed that there was not a morsel of truth in that accusation that was brought against him. (Loud applause.) He was not sure whether he would not rather be a pro-Boer than a pr:-beer. He hoped they were going to prove that they were neither pro-Boers nor prc-beers, but pro-Brunners. (Applause.) He had felt it a pity that Sir John Brunner from the beginning to the end of his speeeli had to, deal with those personal accusations. Why could they not fight those battles on political grounds. When he read the newspapers about tte con- let in that constituency he began to think that the Literal candidate was a limited company. (Laughter.) He thought Brunner, Mond and Clamany were standing for the division. (Laughter.) Was it not time for Mr. ;Samson to devote himself to politics? (Applause.) Why ' should that campaign of calumny go on ? Was it not high time, and was it not really due to the respect that .both candidates held for them as electors that they should raise them above that platform of personalities and oreceed to thy question of politics ? Tne matter of the 30 years of age limit had beau absolutely and satis- factorily disposed of. If it was not show him the man who had yen the notice. (Applause and uproar.) Is was all hearsay evidence, and if thy were in a court of law, as he supposed Mr. Samson was occasionally— (Voices: " He will be and he ought te b 3." (Laughter.) —all the evidence which he had, not only would have no effeo% but would not be admissible. That was the evidence, upon which they were asked to throw out a man who had served them faithfully and well for 25 years. There was only one fact, and that was that that sort of thing would recoil upon his own head. (Applause.) What was the issue that was going to be foullet in that constituency. Mr. Chamberlain ought to know something about thit election ; it wail higond he told thrm the iggde WO war. . Tees, had it in trA lieWiPaDeke that the war was over How then could the issue be war? (A Voice: A settlement.) He, thought that gentleman was quite right. Tney were all agreen upon whatever they might think of the war, and there was not a single Liberal standing for a constituency who did not believe that annexation was inevitable. More thin that, they were agreed upon the steps that must be adopted after annex Won bad taken place. They believed that for a time it must be a Government under military administration. They were agreed that it would theta be governed as a Crown Colony, and that the day would soon come when they give self-governm-mt to those two co Oein in South Africa. They were all agreed, he thought, that as soon as they could safely trust the people of the two ninnies with the right of governing themselves it was the traditional policy of this Government to do it. Mere was one final aril conclusive argument to this point. If the war or the settlement was the issue would the Conservative Govern- ment promise to resign the mement they had dealt with it? (A. Voice . "No,") But if they were honest they oueht to resign after the matter was dealt with, and then appeal to the country for a fresh mandate. (Applause.) This matter, he believed he was right in saving, would be settled by next June. He did not think there would be any din- pute about that in the H3U33 of Commons. There would be nothing to do in the House of elommens after the settlement, and what would happen? The Conservative Government would be in office for five or six years. and what were they going to do in that time ?'(A. Voice: "Nothing.") In 1895 they promised a great many things. They fra.got to do them, and did something else. Now they were promising nothing, ) and they could not do much less. (Laughter and ap- plause.) He asked every Conservative present— not merely as a Conservative p oliticien—wnother he thought in his heart of hearts it was a fair thing to appeal to the people of this country on the false issue of patriotism ? Mr. Chamberlain the other day said two-thirds of the Liberal party were traitors. Whet a fine thing to tell President Krueer !, They knew perfectly well teey were net. Patriotism was something more than Mr. Chamberlain's idea of it. Mr. Chamberlain was getting on so rapidly that he sent his messages to the people. There was a message of " patriotism before politics." He (the speaker) agreed with it. He agreed also that Mr Chamberlain ought to have added" and Chamberlain before both." (Laughter.) Did they think that Mr Oha.mb nlain ani his party were the only patriots in the country ? Did they not think they were using their Army and Navy f-or party purposes? The Army and Navy were the Queen's, nor Mr Chamberlain's. (Applause.) Tee colonies were the Queen's and not Mr Chamberlain's. (A Voice ; " The Colonial Secretary.") Did they think the secretary of Brunner. Mond and Co. was the firm itself. (Loud applause.) Mr Chamberlain was the Colonial Secretary, and his salary came from the taxes. How did Mr Chamberlain begin life? He began life as a Republican. Now what did every Cosservative think of that? A red Repehlican. He became Mayor of Bir- mingham, and a Royal personage came down and shook Mr Chamberlain by the hand, and his manhood trem- bled. (Laughter.) Mr Chamberlain believed in the doc- trine of ransom. He thought he ought to take the pro- petty of the rich, whom he knew by sight, to give it to the poor whom he knew by hearsay. He then became an extreme Radical—the real genuine article. Now he had become a Liberal Unionist, and for ought he (the speaker) knew a Conservative, and he sent messages to the p (Laughter and applause.) Now was that man's career the career of a man they could trust? (A. Voice : " No.") Although he agreed that personalities ought to take no part in elections, yet, believe him, the issue of this election was not so much a Cause or a person—the question really was, is Mr. Chamberlain to rule over this country ? He was waiting, it was said, for his reward—to be Prime Minister of England. He (Mr. Griffith) did not think he could be trusted. He thought he had one sphere in which he excelled—he was an excellent family man. (Laughter, and a Voice: "Say that again.") He was</p>
        <p>n excelleet facnilv trial. Ha wss an affectionate tius- band, a kind father, and an induleent brother-iee•'aw. (Laughter). Ile believed in that political charity which began and ended at home. (Laughter and applause.) They hal heard a great deal about the Birmingham trusts. Did not that show them that they ought to have no trust in Birmingham? Mr. Chamberlain said that statesmen in this country should be like Cmear's wife- -above suspicion. Ile did not see any resemblance be- tween Mr. Chamberlain and Caesar's wife, hut he did a resemblance between him and Ceesar. If he got into power did they not thiek they were introduc- ing the family idea into the politics of this country? If they wanted a man to thwart Mr. Chamberlain they must send Sir John Brunner Applause.) If they wanted a watchful eve kept on Mr Chamberlain so that politics would he kept more pure let them send nir John Brunner. If they wanted to see a man stand up in the House of Commons, courageous and fearless, without being brow-beaten, and bowled down, they must send Sir John Brunner to represent them. (Applause.) What were they going to do with the five or six years that remained after the settlement I was achieved? They must judge any man by his pest, and they must judge the Conservatives by their past. They promised old-age pensions, but how much the amount was to be depended a good deal whether reporters were present. (Laughter.) Reporters were said to have such a damping effect upon political promises. (Laughter.) If the audience was young and vigorous then the pension was to begin at 55; if it was a sort of half-and-half audience that was to begin at 60; but if they were rally aged Conserva- tives it was to begin at 65. For all he knew the pension was coming still, but it had not come, Five years had gone by, and no pension given. What did they think of people who promised things like this? His complaint was not so much what they had failed to de, as about what they had done. When they got into office the first thing they did was to give money to the landlords and parsons. If there was any public money going about who din they think ought to get ? Did they think the landlord ought to have it? Were they the most needy class? Had they ever seen a poor lan lord ? Deese were the people for wh An all these millions of publio money had been given. Whom did they think they said they gave the money to ? It was semething like the terms pro—Beer, and autonomy. Troy said they gave it to:the agricultural interest. How could they give money to anything unless it had a pocket? They could not give money to an abstract noun, but they could abstract the money. (Laughter and aopleuse.) Were they content? It was for them to say if they wou'd subscribe a thousand pounds a year in order to give that money to the landlords of the country. If there was any money going to the agricultural interest didn't they think the first claim was on behalf of the agri- cultural labourer? (Applause) What had become of the money given to education? He knew Sir John Brunner's views upon this question were irresistible. There was no greater friend to education than Sir John Brunner in this country. He said God-speed to every movement that gave a better chance to the children in every little home in their country. If there was going to be money spent on their public schools let the representatives of the public have the spending of it. There was a certain number of millions of money spent upon public educa- tion in this country, partly in board schools and partly in schools that were not board school's. He did not understand these millions, but he understood the simple coin called a slanting.. Out of every shilling that was spent in the voluntary schools of this country ap came from private subscriptions and 4cl from the eacheqier of the country. That was a fact. He did not know execly what was meant by the ex- chequer, except that every man, woman, and child in that room had the privilege of putting sernethine into it from time to time. (Laughter.) There were 8,000 schools in the country where then, was ni representation at all, where the parson spent all the money. They could not believe that was fair and square whether they were Conservatives or Liberals. (Ao- plauss.) It was necessary to pass some law to give the people of this country control of their 90 spent ire the voluntary school. There was the questien of army re- form. Whatever might be their views about the war,he was sure they were all of them sorry that their army system—they were not talking about the men—was not what it ought be in a business community. A long time ago he ventured to describe the Colonial policy on the war as long spoons and short guns. Was it n'ot a terrible thing that they sent their soldiers to fight against two small republics, and they had actually got better guns that we had in the field. Their troops had shown their splendid braverseno troops had done better. They wanted to de two things for themseives—first to put arms in their hands to fight, and secondly, when they had fallen in the fight, they wanted hospital accommodation for them. (Lead applause) They wanted to make them an efficient force. and to do something for them in the dark hour when they had fallen fighting their battle. How was that going to be done? In the first place they would never do it until they democratized their army. (Ap- plause.) It would never be done until they tore aside the red ttp from their ar.rnsilsystein, and society bad a smaller voice in it than it now had. (Applause.) A mail could not become an officer (unless he was promoted from the ranks) unless he bad got £5OO to join the mess. They wanted to democratize the army,and make it possible for the poor man to climb from the bottom. (Loud applause and a Voice : " Hector M icdoneld.'h Yes, they wanted more Hector Meodonelds. (Applause.) What: was the good of having erne of precsion unless they were going to get men by bettering the conditions of life in their army? They ceuld never breed soldiers in the slums of their great cities. They wanted to give the people snore fresh air, better sanitary surroundings, mere self respect, an] in ore independence in life. Then they would have done something to raise the status of the citzen, and when they had raised the status of the citizen they %Mid raise the possibilities of a democratic army in this country. (Applause.) In conclusion Mr Griffith sail it was very difficult to catch up a c b:umny when once it was started. More people read a calumny than Could possibly get to the explanati en, but he trusted that *wary man present, whether Liberal or Conservative, would explain that there was no truth in the scandalous accusations that were brought agaicst Sir John Brunner. He had the best interests of the Empire at heart by bringing happi- ness to the every day life of the people, and by doing their best to secure his return they would not only have done honour to themselves, but they would have rendered &amp; service to their conetry. (Loud and coa- ti eued applause.) Mr. Macineraty, &lt;oho was cerdiallo received, said their Chairman introduced him as a Queen's Counsel. It was a title of Which he was very proud because it was generally conferred upon men for merit, lire was vary proud of it because it Wag a position of great trust and earnest duty. He was sworn to give good counsel to her Majsstv. and if her Majesty would be pleased at this juncture to ask for his advice he would give it in a very few words, and would charge nothing for it. (Laughter and applause.) He would tell her to beware of Joseph— (laugh' er)—for after that speech of Mr. Griffith upon Mr. Chamberlain's historic career, from red Radicalism to the usurpation of the Royal function of issuing pro- clama.tions to the people of England, he thought that such a person would not be a very safe adviser for her elej 'Cy. He (the speaker) was beaten at Widnes tbe previous night, but he was there still. (Applause.) He was beaten by what a friend of his called the influence of pewter. (Laughter.) His opponent belonged to a firm which owned in his constituency alone 350 publichollses --111:tied houses. The managers were all at his service. His opponent had 313 canvassing booths. The fight of Samson with the dragon was mere child's play when compered with his. (Laughter.) The dragon fought ant died far his convictions. He did not entrench him- self in the trirric ides of beer barrels, he did not fortify himself with so Is and whisky and bring up his rear with soda water and tobacco. He fought in the open. (Laughter.) Sir John Brunner was known to them and to all England as one of the men who had made this country what it was. He W3q a great merchant, a good emplever, a man with liberal ideas, a man in the fore- front of social reformation, a man who for 37 years had lived amongst them, and bearing in mind all that he was pained to bear he was to he put on his defence to answer a charge made by a Manchester attorney. He , thought things were coming to a pretty pass when his friend Sir John Brunner had to enter into all these questions and argue the matter out with a Manchester attorney. Sir John commenced his address by wing that he had served the constituency for 15 veers, and he told them he hed done it faithfully with his whole heart, and that he had done it for the nation. It was for them to say that he had done it for that copnty. H. (Mr Macinerney) could vouch that he had dohe it for the nation. (Applause.) He had seen Sir John in the House of Commons. He knew the work he had done as a member of the Radical party in favour of the people, work for regenerating this country on behalf of the me.eses as against the classes. (Applause.) He had se.n Sir John professionally as chairman of some of the most important committees that had sat in the House, and he could bear testimony to his business know- ledge and to his capacity for winnowing the chaff from the wheat. It was the opinion of his profession that there was no better Chairman of Committees in either House than Sir John Banner. (Applause.) His (Sir John's) nephew was now risking his life in South Africa for the honour of the British flag, for the supremacy of the British Empire, while his uncle was accused by an attor- ney of being a pro-Boer. (Applause.) He hoped every- one present would do their best to see Sir John returned te Parliament. It would be a victory that would give strength to the Liberal party. It would be one of the fleet county victories for the Liberal party, that party which through all time and all changes was the party of the people, that had inscribed upon its banner not im- perialism and war and rumours of war, not annexation, not doles to the aristocracy.uot benefits to the best paid clergy in the wad 1. but low taxationoconomy, pregress and reform, and liberty for every man. (Loud ap- plause.) A resolution expressing confidence in Sir John Brunner and pledging the meeting to use all legitimate means to secure his return was put from the chair and carried unanimously. The meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>Liberal Demonstration at Northwich.</p>
        <p>Oa Tuesday night, Sir John Brunner had three capital meetings at Moulton, Davenham, and Northwich. The last was the largest Liberal demonstration at Northwich during th 9 present ea npaign. the hall being packed. Addresses vivre delivered by Mr Eenry Broadhurst, M.P. Mr Alfred Mond,late Liberal candidate for South Salford. the candidate, and others. Sir John was escorted to the meeting by a band and a torch-light procession. Mr Alfred Mond asserted that the issue was between jingo- ism and peace, retrogression and progress (cheers).—Mr Henry Broadhurst declared that Sir John :Brunner was one whose presence in Parliament was of the highest importance in the protection of the public rights and Liberties. Unless they rose in their might and stemmed the advancing tide, the country would become a despotism.—Sir John Brunner who had a wonderful re- ception, said this was a contest such as he never had ea- perieneed in the whole of his political life. Be was there, not to declare the principles of Liberalism, but for the defence of his character. (" Shams.") Be was called pro-Boer and little Englander. He was entitled to say that the man who called him this was an anti- Christian (Applause). His business went to the utter- most parts of the earth, and as trade followed the fig, he desired the extension of the British Empire (cheers). Human liberty and human progress were his desire, and unless the Tories mismanaged, the name of the British Empire would be a symbol for liberty and progress. Let the Empire be governed on Liberal lines, and he was a Great Englander, but let it be governed on Tory lines and he wanted no British Empire (cheers).—A resolution of confidence was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Temperance Question</head>
        <p>The Temperance Question</p>
        <p>The Winsford District Temperance and Band of Hope Un;on nas addressed questions to Sir John Brunner and Mr. Samsont regarding the sale of drink to chil- dren and the Local Veto. Both candidates favour measures to prevent the sale toyoune persons under six- teen. Mr. Samson is not in favour of Local Veto. The union has accordingly passed a resolution to support Sir John Brunner as the more favourable to their cause.</p>
        <p>Meeting at Weston Catholic School.</p>
        <p>A small but very enthusiastic meeting of Sir John Brunner's supporters was also held at West3n, on Tues- day night, when Mr A R Norman, J.P., preside&amp; Ad- dresses were delivered by Mr W Millington,the Rev A E Eyre, the Chairman, and Mr J Williams. Throughout the whole proceedings the utmost enthusiasm pre- vailed, and the meeting terminated with the singing of "God save the Queen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Labour Unions.</head>
        <p>The Labour Unions.</p>
        <p>Mr. Billam,president of the Upper Mersey Watermen's and P3rter's Ass3ciation, attended Runcorn on Tuesday evening, and delivered an address in furtherance of Sir J. T. Brunner's candidature. The meeting was originally announced to take place in Devonshire Square, but owing to the rain, took place in the Lecture Room of the Liberal Club, Regent-street. The chair was taken by Mr. T. Dean, and he was supported by Mr Billam, Mr E Gatley and others. The attendance was large and enthusiastic. Mr. BiHam, in the course of his address, called upon all to support SirJ T Brunner on the grounds that he was an employer who supported trade unions, and acted fairly between man and man. He paid a high compli- ment to Sir J T Brunner, and to the manner in which the firm of Brunner. Mond and Co. carried on their works and lookdd after their men. (Applause.) Finally he asked all Trade Unionists to vote for Brunner to a man, for he was a good employer and would be sure to make a good representative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>' Mr. Samson's Candidature.</head>
        <p>' Mr. Samson's Candidature.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night, Mr. Samson, the Unionist can- didate, addressed meetings at Acton, Little Leigh, and Barnton. He said there were without doubt domestic matters requiring reform, but they would certainly receive attention. The election must decide the country's agreement otherwise with the Government's conduct before and during the war, which, to his mind, was inevitable, unless they had resorted to a policy of surrender and scuttle. Asserting Sir John Brunner's pro-Boerism, the speaker stoutly denied the rumour that he had anologised for thus designating him. He pro- claimed it emphatically, and demanded from the North- with electors what reward they would mete out to men who bad been false to England. Were they going to listen to the voice of Burnley and Salford? During the last two days a red herring had been drawn across the path by a poster regarding the salt duty, intended to divert attention from the main issue. Sir John Brunner hid had years to effect a remedy, but had done nothing. The speak-3r expressed himself favourable to old-age pen- sinus, and the encouragement of the voluntary school system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Liverpool Members at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Liverpool Members at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held in Runcorn on 'Wednesday night in support of the candidature of Mr C L Samson, the Tory candidate. Mr C J Cunningham presided, and hq was supported by Colonel Sandye, M.P., Mr C M'Artbur, M.P., and Mr R Pierpoint, M.P. (Warring- ton.) After addresses from these gentlemen, the usual vote of confidence in the candidate was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Local Election Arrangements.</head>
        <p>Local Election Arrangements.</p>
        <p>To-morrow the all important election for the North- wich Division takes place.and for the information of the electors we publi sh the following arrangements as de- posed by Mr Benjamin Chaffers Roberts (the sheriff). There will be six polling booths : four for Runcorn North, and two for Runcorn South. The former will be situated at the National Schools, Runcorn and will be labelled No 1,2, 3. awl 4 respectively. At No. 1 electors numbered K Ito K 337 inclusive will vote. At No. 2 electors numbered K 397 to K 877 both inclusive will vote. At N. 3 station electors K 951 to K 1544 both inclusive will vote ; and at No. 4 from K 1707 to K 2269 both inclusive will vote. For Runcorn South the poll- ing stations will be at Victoria-road Board Schools, and numbered No. 1 and 2 respectively. At No. 1 electors rkumberNi L 1. to L 514 both inclusive will vote, and at station No. 2 elictors numbered L 637 to L 1048, both inclusive, and electors numbered L 1139 to L 1153, both inclusive, will be allowed tu vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>WIDNES DIVISION.</head>
        <p>WIDNES DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Result of the Poll.</p>
        <p>After provedings of a brief but effective character, the election in Widnes reached its concluding stage on Fri lay when palling to3k place. Th 3 day broke fine, tut rah% Bowl set in, and continued until about three o'clock, when the atmospheric conditions assumed a brighter aspect. In the evening, however, rain dr- scendei at intervals until midnight. The polling places comprised six at Widnes, situated in Farnworth,Haltoa, Simm's Cross, Victoria, Waterloo, and West Bank wards; one at Garston for that township and Allerton and Speke ; one at Halebank for Halewood and Hale ; one at Woalton for Much and Little Woolton ; and one at Huy- ton for that district and WI-Aston and Tarbock. Mr W T Husband and Mr B B Shores worked on behalf of Colonel Walker and Mr Macinerney respectively. There was also a large number of workers for both sides. the Conservatives having presumably the largest complement Mr C R Rutherford, of the firm of Messrs Miller, Peel. Hughes, Rutherford and Company, Liverpool, officiated as returning officer on behalf of the High Sheriff. Business during the day was not very brisk, but to- wards evening the workers at various poll stations were kept busily engaged by the voters who had not pre- viou3ly had an opportunity of exercising the fran- chise.</p>
        <p>Promptly at eight o'clock the ballot boxes were expe- ditiously removed to the Widnes Town Hall. Here counting was at once commenced. Towards 11 o'clock the members of the press were admitted to the Council Chamber. There were present, the two candidates. the Mayor (Alderman G I Neil), Mr H Wade Deacon, Major Lewis, Mr Eustace Carey, and other gentlemen. The Returning Officer explained that the poll had been made up. Unfortunately in the first count they thought a row of figures were added up wrongly. How- ever, these turned out absolutely correct. The result was as fnllows : walker (U) Macinerney (I)</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
