      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>WARRINGTON ELECTION. Lessons of the Liberal Defeat.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ELECTION. Lessons of the Liberal Defeat.</p>
        <p>[SPBCIAL.]</p>
        <p>The lessons of the Liberal defeat in Warrington are" writ large " to those who will take the trouble to observe and it is in the hope that these remarks may meet the eye of such men as desire to see the town resume its rightful position as a constituency on the side of" Progress" and that they may be of profit to such, that they are now submitted. First let us say that no serious attempt has been made for a many years past to keep the electorate pos'ed up with political information. It is quite true that the daily paper has done, and is doing much in this direction, but seeing how much and how continuously the Tory press misrepresents the facts and cloaks the misdeeds of its party in Parliament, no man can deny the necessity for con- stant and unceasing exposure from time to time of the blunders which are the inevitable sequence of Tory mismanagement. Public meetings to which all electors should be invited should be as frequent as possible. Unless this is done the "man in the street" whose knowledge of politics is limited to that which is the excitement of the hour becomes an easy prey to Tory blandishments and beer,and of course the result is obvious.</p>
        <p>The organisation of the Liberal party in Warring- ton wants overhauling from top to bottom. Certain Wards which need not be named are Liberal to the core and may be always relied upon to return a majority of,votes for the Progressive candidate,but in other wards, and some of them very large and populous, the Tory party is allowed to do just what it choses, simply because there is practically no Liberal organisation really worthy of the name. One is, of course, not blind to the fact that the " khaki " fever was running riot last week in Warrington as elsewhere, neither do we forget that it would not be difficult to explain why such an enormous vote was polled on the Tory side between six and eight o'clock at night, on a day when as most of the works in the town were closed either the whole day or the greater part of it, it would have been very easy for the "horny handed" elector to poll in the daylight. The war fever will however soon wane and the antidote of "pay, pay, pay," which the Tory Government will have very shortly to apply (and that in a drastic manner) will give it a tolerably lasting " quietus.'' We do not overlook the fact that a good many professed Liberals not only stood absolutely aloof from the contest, but withheld any assistance of any kind whatever. They thought the contest was a mistake and would neither assisr, with money, vehicles and in some cases not even with votes. Their excuse being that as the Tory party had caused the abominable mess into which the country was plunged by the war they (the Tories) should get the country out of it again. Such an argument is to our mind childish in the extreme and such con- duct most unpatriotic. Who is to say that the present Chamberlain—Salisbury party, which is a curious combination of unscrupulous cheek and bewildering incompetence, may not land this Empire into a far more serious "mess "upon some Eastern or other question, before long? Although it was apparent after the first elections that it was impossible, short of a miracle, for the Liberal party to be returned to power at this election, the menacing language used by the man to whom very shortly every Tory knee must bow, made it impera- tive that in the interests of the Empire the Opposi- tion should be made as strong as possible in order that Chamberlain's unscrupulous and mischievous propensities might at least be held in check. The leaders of the Tory party can no more keep him in hand than they can carry on the Government of the country next year with an Income Tax of one penny in the pound. We hope that the Liberals who abstained at this election will reflect in the time to come upon the folly of such abstention. To the absence of such men as we have already referred must be attributed the scanty " platform" at Mr. Crosfield's meetings. Now, while this "socially poverty-stricken appearance" caused the enemy to blaspheme, it does not strike us as a matter to be greatly regretted. All honour to the few men -w hose pockets are not as big as their hearts and who did yeoman service as the temporary leaders of the Party in the recent fight. The very least that those who abstained from help- ing can and ought to do, is to "abstain "from sneer- ing at men who at any rate she wed a much higher sense of duty and true patriotism than the" arm- chair Liberals "whose chief function seems to be to criticise and that in no friendly spirit the action of those who do the Party's work. . . .</p>
        <p>And to what end are tliese remarks intended and what are the practical suggestions for the better- ment of Liberalism in Warrington? I.—RBORGANIZATION : The removal of all incom- petent men and their replacement by others who have the interests of the cause at heart and the ability to do what is necessary to secure success. An active Committee in each ward with competent officers. 2.—REGISTRATION: Although this is by no means inefficient there are far too manygories on the Register whose qualifications are doubtful. 3.—EDUCATION: Every Tory blunder as it occurs (and there will be plenty of them during the term of the new Government) should be promply and ruthlessly and publicly exposed. Frequent meetings of the Party both public and otherwise should be held and the Social as well as the Political element thoroughly cultivated. 4.—LIBERAL CLUB: A new Liberal Club in a more central position, while not indis- pensable, would be an invaluable boon to the Party and would check a good deal of the Junior Conservatism of the town which has very properly recently retired into a back street. All these suggestions are no doubt capable of improvement, but whatever is done will require a much larger financial outlay than has been incurred for several years past. To their credit be it said that the " funds " required for party purposes, although shamefully inadequate, have been found by a very few stalwarts, who have also in a large measure done most of the work and incurred by far the greater responsibility. There are even yet a fairly large number of professing Liberals in War- rington, of good social and financial position, who do absolutely nothing year in and year out beyond recording their votes, and these men ought to be ashamed to continue to act upon these lines. It is true there is no such thing as "gratitude in poli- tics." and one sees the verification of this proverb in the curious spectacle of thousands of working- men who owe all they have to-day (as voters, and in the en joym9nt of untaxed food, untaxed litera- ture, untaxed light and air, and a thousand other privileges won for them by Liberalism, and in the teeth of the bitterest Tory opposition) voting for Tory candidates. But while ignorance and full stomachs may be pleaded as an excuse for such, there is no excuse for the meanness of spirit which is shewn by men whose knowledge compels them to realise every day of their lives what they owe i to the incorporation of Liberal principles n the laws of our country, but who are content to reap where others have sown without in the slightest degree realising that as they have freely received, so should they freely give to the cause to which they owe so much, and upon the continued progress of which their children and their children's chil- dren may depend for their religious and political freedom.</p>
        <p>The Stag Inn, Walt—on, to be Re-built. At a meeting of the Knutsford District Licensing Committee, held on Monday afternoon at the Sessions House, Knutsford, Colonel 11. France-Hay- hurst presiding, Mr. 'Browne, Warrington, applied on behalf of Mrs. Fanny Maddock, of the Stag Inn, Walton Inferior, for the confirmation of a pro- visional grant for the re-building of the Stag Ina, situated at the corner of Chester-road and Ellesmere- road. The premises had formerly been on the old main road from Warrington to Chester.but owing to the deviation caased by the Ship Canal the house was not now on the direct road, but was really in Ellesmere-road, about 20 yards from the corner of Chester-road. It was now proposed to put up a good house, with the addition of stabling and a bowling green, the present premises being in a dilapidated condition. Mr Browne pointed out that the licence had been granted unanimously by the justices at Daresbury, under the presidency of Mr J Charlton Parr: and that the applicant bad been in the unique position of being supported in the applica- tion by the Chief Constable of Cheshire, through Superintendent Pearson of the district. —Mr. William Owen, architect, of Warrington, explained the plans of the proposed re-building and setting back of the house, after which the Chairman signi- fied that the Bench granted the confirmation sought.</p>
      </div>
